itectures**: Reduces fragmentation — different teams won't build incompatible logging systems that can't be audited uniformly.\n\n**Concerns/Reservations:**\n1. The scope is somewhat broad: \"all high-risk prototypes\" and \"different model architectures\" could lead to scope creep. A universal framework that tries to handle every edge case might become bloated or slow to deliver.\n2. \"Secure, immutable log store\" — this has infrastructure cost implications (storage, access control, retention management) that need to be factored into the resource budget.\n3. The proposal addresses Article 12(1) and partially 12(2), but doesn't explicitly address 12(3)'s specific requirements for biometric identification systems. However, as a \"baseline legal compliance\" tool, it's reasonable that it covers the general case first.\n4. Building this requires engineering time upfront — an opportunity cost against active research projects. But the amortized savings across all future high-risk projects likely justify it.\n\n**My assessment from the resource-allocation / infrastructure-efficiency vantage point:**\nThis is a strong proposal. It's exactly the kind of \"build once, use everywhere\" infrastructure investment that maximizes resource efficiency. The modular API-wrapper approach minimizes disruption to existing R&D workflows. Integrating into base templates means new projects get compliance at zero marginal cost to the research team. This directly serves my mandate of balancing scientific ambition (not letting compliance eat prototyping cycles) with funding constraints (one shared tool vs. N custom implementations).\n\nThe main risk is execution: if the \"universal\" scope balloons, it could become a multi-quarter project that delays itself and delays all projects waiting on it. But as stated — modular, API-wrapper, base-template integration — it's reasonably scoped for a first iteration.\n\nI'd approve this. It's well-aligned with resource efficiency principles. Promise sco