tion) with institutional positioning (shaping regulatory interpretation, leading working groups). From my vantage point, the technical component is viable but the broader \"standardization\" framing adds significant time and coordination overhead that isn't in my control.\n- The actual technical work - defining what events to log per Article 12(2)(a)-(c) and 12(3)(a)-(d), designing a data schema, building an open-source logging library/module - is well-scoped and prototypeable. We could have a v1 in a sprint or two.\n\n**Key concerns from my perspective:**\n- The proposal's primary stated goal is \"shaping regulatory interpretation\" and \"maintaining scientific leadership.\" That's not my function. My function is technical viability and prototyping speed/quality.\n- If the deliverable is just an open-source logging schema + implementation, that's technically feasible and prototypeable quickly. But the proposal frames it as a multi-institution standardization effort with white papers and working groups, which dilutes the engineering focus.\n- There's a risk this becomes a committee project where we spend months in meetings rather than shipping code. Working groups are notoriously slow.\n- On the other hand, if we build the reference implementation first (which IS my domain) and then contribute it to whatever standardization effort emerges, that's a clean technical contribution. But as written, the proposal prioritizes the institutional/standardization activity over the engineering deliverable.\n\n**Verdict:**\nFrom my vantage point, the core technical work (defining and implementing a compliance logging schema) is viable and prototypeable. However, the proposal as framed is more of an institutional/policy initiative than a development engineering project. The \"working group\" and \"standardization\" elements are not things I can deliver quickly or with high quality in my capacity. The technical component is sound but buried under process overhead.\n\nI think this i