 layer than ad-hoc logging in individual projects.\n- Recital 71 emphasizes traceability, verifying compliance, monitoring operations and post-market monitoring. A unified framework supports all three.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- It's an infrastructure investment that requires upfront development resources before any single system ships. From a pure \"can we pass the next audit\" perspective, this is enabling rather than directly producing compliant artifacts.\n- The proposal says it wraps \"existing telemetry tools\" — this assumes those tools are adequate. If they're not, the middleware might inherit gaps (e.g., if existing tools don't capture the specific fields required by Art 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems).\n- It doesn't explicitly mention retention periods, access controls, or the ability to produce logs in a format suitable for regulator inspection. Article 12 doesn't specify these details explicitly, but Recital 71 mentions \"clear and comprehensive form\" and availability of technical documentation. The proposal is somewhat high-level on these operational compliance aspects.\n- However, as an R&D institution, we're in the development phase. Having this framework ready before systems go live is actually smart for compliance — it means every system that enters the market already has the logging infrastructure baked in.\n\n**From my vantage point (compliance, institutional survival, reputation):**\nThis is a strong proposal. It directly operationalizes Article 12's core requirement (automatic recording of events) at the architectural level. The risk of NOT having this is significant: if each project implements logging differently, we face inconsistency in what gets captured, which could mean missing required fields during an audit or market surveillance check. A unified framework reduces the probability of non-compliance across our portfolio.\n\nThe fact that it \"maintains prototyping speed\" is a nice bonus but not my primary concern — my concern