        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain concerns are institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2655 is about researching how to minimize performance overhead of high-granularity logging in real-time AI systems, exploring compressed logging formats and asynchronous data streaming.\n\nLet me assess this strictly from a compliance perspective:\n\n**Compliance analysis:**\n\n1. **Article 12(1)** requires that high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is an absolute requirement - the logging must be automatic and cover the entire lifetime.\n\n2. **Article 12(2)** requires logging capabilities to enable recording of events relevant to:\n   (a) identifying risk situations or substantial modifications\n   (b) facilitating post-market monitoring (Art 72)\n   (c) monitoring operation (Art 26(5))\n\n3. **Article 12(3)** for Annex III point 1(a) systems requires minimum logging of specific data points.\n\nNow, the proposal is about \"minimizing performance overhead\" of logging through:\n- Compressed logging formats\n- Asynchronous data streaming\n- Testing against resource-intensive models\n\n**Compliance concerns:**\n\nThe critical question from a compliance standpoint: Does compressing logs or making them asynchronous risk violating Article 12?\n\n- **Automatic recording**: The regulation requires \"automatic recording of events.\" Asynchronous data streaming could introduce delays in logging. If a system fails or is shut down before the async buffer flushes, those events are lost. This directly threatens compliance with Art 12(1) which requires logs over \"the lifetime of the system.\"\n\n- **Compressed formats**: Compression itself isn't inherently problematic IF the data remains fully reconstruct