n of risk situations.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a research project with open-ended questions. There's no clear deliverable timeline because the fundamental scientific problems (how do you define \"high-level cognitive events\" in arbitrary neural networks?) are unsolved.\n- A development engineer can prototype a logging system that captures input/output pairs, model version, confidence scores, and timestamps in days to weeks. But prototyping \"semantic event logging via latent space monitoring\" could take years or may never reach production quality because the underlying interpretability science is not mature enough.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what systems it applies to, what the performance constraints are, or how it integrates with existing infrastructure.\n- From a speed perspective: this is the opposite of rapid prototyping. It's fundamental research.\n\n**Does it meet the regulatory need?**\n- Article 12(1) says \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" This doesn't require semantic/cognitive-level logging. It requires that events relevant to traceability, post-market monitoring, and risk identification can be recorded.\n- Recital 71 mentions \"comprehensible information\" but this is about the technical documentation as a whole, not necessarily about the logs themselves being at a \"cognitive event\" level. The logs need to enable the purposes in Article 12(2)(a)-(c), which can be met by well-structured input/output/event logging without solving the interpretability problem.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (Article 12(3)), the requirements are very specific: start/end times, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of verifiers. None of this requires \"semantic cognitive event logging.\"\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a development engineering perspective focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality:\n- This is not technically viable as an engineering solution in any near