ey calibrated? This matters because if thresholds are too tight, we get alert fatigue; too loose, we miss real risks.\n- It's framed as a new build (\"We will build a monitor\") which implies significant resource commitment. Is this truly necessary beyond what Article 12 requires? Article 12 requires the system to \"technically allow for automatic recording of events\" — it doesn't explicitly mandate real-time alerting. However, Recital 71 speaks to \"monitoring of their operations and post market monitoring,\" which suggests active monitoring is in scope.\n- The proposal conflates several things: log analysis, drift detection, compliance alerting. This could become a scope creep monster.\n- From a pure Article 12 perspective, the requirement is about *recording* events (logging), not necessarily about real-time analysis of those logs. The alerting system goes beyond strict Article 12 into operational risk management territory. However, it directly serves the purposes stated in Recital 71 and supports Articles 72, 79, and 26(5) compliance.\n- Resource commitment concern: this is a substantial engineering project. As Compliance Officer, I need to weigh whether this level of investment is proportionate to our regulatory obligation versus simply maintaining good logs.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation), this proposal is highly relevant. The EU AI Act's architecture clearly envisions not just passive logging but active monitoring — Recital 71 explicitly mentions \"monitoring of their operations and post market monitoring.\" Article 12(2) requires logs to be structured to enable identification of risks and substantial modifications, which implies some level of analysis capability. A system that proactively flags potential non-compliance before it becomes a regulatory incident is exactly what protects the institution's survival and reputation.\n\nHowever, I should note that this goes somewhat beyond the strict letter of Article 12 (which is about record-keeping/logging) into operational monitoring territory. But since my domain includes \"regulatory compliance\" broadly under the AI Act, and this directly supports multiple articles, it's well within scope for me to endorse.\n\nThe main risk I see is resource allocation — building a full real-time monitoring platform is expensive. But from a compliance perspective, the alternative (passive logging with no analysis capability) would leave us vulnerable: we'd have logs but no efficient way to demonstrate to regulators that we actually monitor our systems. That's a weak position in an enforcement action.\n\nI'll approve this, with a high promise score because it directly addresses multiple regulatory requirements and protects institutional survival. The vagueness of \"predefined thresholds\" is an implementation detail that can be addressed during scoping.\n",
