        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2238\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly implements the core technical mandate of Article 12(1) — automatic, lifetime-spanning event recording — and institutionalizes it so that no project can ship a high-risk system without a compliant logging layer, which is precisely the systemic risk I am hired to prevent. The decoupling-from-model-logic design reduces the realistic operational failure mode in an R&D environment (rapid model iteration accidentally breaking or bypassing audit trails), and immutability plus time-stamping are essential for producing credible evidence in any regulator inquiry under Articles 72/79. My residual concern is that a 'wrapper around existing telemetry' may not natively capture the domain-specific fields mandated by Article 12(3) (reference database identity, match input data, and natural-person verification IDs); the SDK must be validated against every sub-paragraph of 12(2)(a)-(c) and 12(3)(a)-(d) before it can be certified as sufficient, but the architectural direction is unambiguously correct from a compliance standpoint.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 87\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2238 suggests building a \"Standardized Traceability SDK for High-Risk AI\" — a unified software library providing standardized API calls for automatic event recording and metadata tagging across all projects. It wraps existing telemetry tools to ensure logs are immutable, time-stamped, and formatted according to EU standards automatically. The goal is to decouple regulatory compliance from model logic to maintain high prototyping speed.\n\nLet me assess this from a compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n1. Article 12(1) explicitly requires that \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A standardized SDK directly addresses this requirement by ensuring every project has logging capability built in.\n2. Recital 71 emphasizes that technical documentation must be kept up to date \"appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system\" and that systems should \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events, by means of logs.\" A unified SDK ensures consistency across projects.\n3. Immutability and time-stamping are critical for traceability — a regulator (or court) needs to trust that logs haven't been tampered with. This is essential for verifying compliance under Article 12(2).\n4. \"Formatted according to EU standards automatically\" addresses the risk of inconsistent logging formats across projects, which would make audit trails harder to produce and verify.\n5. Decoupling compliance from model logic is smart — it means that even when teams iterate rapidly on models (as R&D institutions do), the compliance layer doesn't get broken or forgotten. This reduces the risk of shipping a system without proper logging.\n6. Standardization across \"all projects\" ensures no project falls through the cracks — critical for an institution with multiple concurrent AI development efforts.\n\n**Concerns/Risks:**\n1. The proposal says \"wrapper around our existing telemetry tools.\" I need to verify that existing telemetry actually captures what Article 12(3) requires for Annex III point 1(a) systems: (a) start/end dates of use, (b) reference database used, (c) input data leading to a match, (d) identification of natural persons in verification. A generic telemetry wrapper may not capture domain-specific fields like \"identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results.\" This is a gap I'd need to flag.\n2. \"Formatted according to EU standards automatically\" — the EU AI Act doesn't prescribe a specific log format (it's more principle-based). The SDK would need to be designed with enough flexibility to satisfy different risk categories while maintaining consistency.\n3. Immutability: this is good for compliance but needs to be carefully scoped. \"Over the lifetime of the system\" means logs must persist potentially for many years. Storage and access planning matters.\n4. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention Article 12(2)(a) — identifying situations that may result in risk or substantial modification. Does the SDK capture anomaly detection triggers? This is a more nuanced logging requirement than just \"record every event.\"\n\n**Compliance Officer Assessment:**\nThis is a strong, well-structured proposal that directly addresses the core technical requirement of Article 12(1). It's exactly the kind of institutional infrastructure investment that a compliance officer would want to see — it ensures that compliance isn't an afterthought bolted onto each project but is baked into the development pipeline. The decoupling aspect reduces the risk of model teams inadvertently breaking logging during rapid iteration, which is a real operational risk in R&D environments.\n\nThe main concern is whether \"wrapper around existing telemetry\" is sufficient for all the specific data fields required by Article 12(3), particularly the human verification identification and reference database tracking. But as a proposal at this stage, it's directionally correct and addresses the right problem. I'd approve it with the understanding that implementation must verify coverage of all Article 12(2) and 12(3) data points.\n\nFrom my vantage point:\n- Institutional survival: A unified compliance infrastructure reduces the risk of any single project being non-compliant, which protects the institution from enforcement actions. High value.\n- Regulatory compliance: Directly implements Article 12(1), supports 12(2), and can be configured to meet 12(3). Very high alignment.\n- Market reputation: Having a standardized, auditable logging infrastructure is a differentiator in B2B contexts where clients will demand evidence of compliance. Positive signal.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. The only reason I'm not at 100 is the \"wrapper around existing telemetry\" caveat — if existing tools don't capture all required fields, additional development work is needed and the proposal somewhat understates that complexity. But the direction is exactly right for compliance infrastructure.\n\nI'd rate this around 85-90. Let me settle on 87.\n",
