asters, improving ROI on scarce resources.\n- Performance drift detection is genuinely useful for R&D operations beyond just compliance.\n- It's scoped to a specific function (observability/logging), not an open-ended \"build an AI platform\" proposal.\n\nCons/Concerns:\n- \"Centralized dashboard\" implies building new infrastructure. Do we already have logging/monitoring tools in the lab? Most R&D labs likely already have some telemetry, CI/CD pipelines with logging, or monitoring stacks (Prometheus, Grafana, ELK stack, etc.). Building a *new* centralized platform might be duplicative and wasteful of resources.\n- The proposal says \"build a centralized dashboard\" - this is a significant engineering commitment. The question is whether we can extend/adapt existing infrastructure rather than building from scratch.\n- However, the proposal doesn't explicitly say \"from scratch\" - it could mean configuring existing tools into a purpose-specific view. But \"designed specifically for post-market monitoring\" suggests custom development.\n- The scope seems reasonable - it's not trying to be an entire MLOps platform, just focused on logging/observability for Article 12 compliance plus debugging utility.\n- From a resource efficiency standpoint, if we need logging anyway (Article 12(1) is mandatory), then building the *right* logging system with observability built in is more efficient than building minimal logging now and retrofitting monitoring later.\n\nThe \"dual-use\" argument is strong from a resource-efficiency perspective: we're going to spend resources on Article 12 compliance regardless, so designing that compliance infrastructure to also serve our debugging/monitoring needs means we get more value per euro of implementation effort.\n\nHowever, my concern about existing infrastructure remains. The proposal doesn't address whether this builds on or duplicates what's already in the lab. As Lab Director, I'd want to know if we can extend existing monitoring tools rather than commission a new platform. But the proposal as stated is reasonable - it's scoped, purposeful, and leverages the regulatory requirement as justification for building something we need anyway.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's:\n- Focused (not a sprawling ambition)\n- Dual-purpose (compliance + operational improvement = better ROI on resources)\n- Required by law anyway (so we're not choosing to spend, we're choosing *how* to spend the money that must be spent)\n- Scoped appropriately for a lab R&D institution\n\nMy concern about existing infrastructure is noted but doesn't rise to rejection - the proposal is a design direction, and in implementation we'd naturally assess what exists. The framing as \"designed specifically for\" post-market monitoring suggests it could be a configuration layer on top of existing logging rather than a ground-up build.\n\nDecision: Approve\nPromise: I'd say around 72-78. It's solid, dual-purpose, and required anyway. Not a 90+ because the \"build a centralized dashboard\" framing raises questions about whether we're duplicating existing infrastructure, and there's some ambiguity in scope. But it's clearly worth pursuing from my vantage point.\n\nLet me settle on 74.\n",
