estion is whether it translates into implementable engineering artifacts quickly. Anomaly detection on logs can be prototyped relatively quickly with off-the-shelf tools (e.g., statistical methods, autoencoders), but making it reliable enough to actually detect \"risk situations\" in the regulatory sense is much harder.\n\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) the technical logging infrastructure required by Article 12(1)-(3), and (2) an automated monitoring/anomaly detection layer on top of those logs. Article 12 requires the *capability* for automatic recording — it doesn't mandate real-time automated risk detection. The proposal is building something beyond what the regulation strictly requires, which means:\n  - It's a nice-to-have that adds value but isn't a compliance requirement per se.\n  - However, it does serve Article 72 (post-market monitoring) and Article 14(5) (human verification), so there is regulatory relevance.\n\n- From a prototyping perspective: The core logging infrastructure (Article 12(1)) needs to be built regardless — that's the mandatory part. This proposal adds an intelligence layer on top. That's separable, which is good for modular development. But the \"real-time\" requirement and the need for domain-specific risk detection models make this a longer-horizon project with uncertain technical outcomes.\n\n- Risk: The proposal frames this as turning \"a regulatory constraint into an innovative research stream.\" From my engineering perspective, I'd be cautious about letting research exploration consume resources that should go toward building the actual compliance infrastructure (the logging system itself). The mandatory part of Article 12 is relatively straightforward engineering — structured log recording with specific fields for Annex III(1)(a) systems. The anomaly detection layer is an enhancement.\n\n- Speed/quality concern: \"Researchers will develop\" suggests this is exploratory and may not produce deployable artifacts on a short