exactly gets logged. Article 12(2) specifies particular categories (risk identification, post-market monitoring facilitation, operation monitoring), and Article 12(3) for biometric systems has very specific requirements. A one-size-fits-all wrapper might either over-log (wasting resources, creating compliance noise) or under-log (missing required fields).\n- For prototyping speed: if the framework is too rigid/standardized, it could slow down rapid iteration during R&D. You want to be able to prototype quickly without waiting for the \"compliance layer\" to be fully specified. But you also don't want to retrofit logging later - that's painful.\n- The proposal doesn't address the tension between research prototyping (where you iterate fast, models change daily) and production compliance logging (where every event must be captured). A unified framework needs clear separation of these modes.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Consistent log format across different research teams\" - this is good for auditability but could be a bottleneck if the format is too rigid or changes frequently during early development.\n- The proposal is reasonable and technically sound as an engineering approach, but it's somewhat high-level. It doesn't address the specific technical challenges of hooking into diverse ML runtimes, handling real-time inference latency constraints (logging shouldn't add meaningful latency to a real-time system), or the operational complexity of maintaining immutable stores at scale.\n- For my vantage point: this is a viable engineering project that would save time long-term but has upfront cost. It's not revolutionary - it's solid infrastructure work. The risk is that \"unified\" becomes a scope creep target, and the framework becomes so complex that it actually slows down prototyping rather than speeding it up.\n\n**Decision:**\nThe proposal is technically viable - this is standard software engineering (logging frameworks exist in every ecosystem). It addresses Article 12(