quirements: period of each use, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons in verification. The proposal doesn't explicitly address whether it captures these specific fields.\n- \"Compliant technical report\" is vague - the EU AI Act requires very specific documentation elements. A generator that simply formats existing data may miss required compliance elements if they weren't captured upstream.\n- However, as a compliance officer, I see this as addressing a real and significant gap: ensuring that the documentation required by Article 12 (and referenced in Recital 71) is actually produced, maintained, and kept current. Without it, we risk non-compliance simply because scientists don't have time to write reports.\n- The proposal mentions \"pull metadata from our existing experiment tracking tools\" - this is good for data lineage but I need to verify that the existing tools actually capture everything Article 12 requires.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nThis is a solid, directly relevant compliance measure. It addresses the documentation and record-keeping obligation head-on. The fact that it automates the process reduces the risk of non-compliance due to human oversight or neglect. It supports traceability (Recital 71) and ensures technical documentation stays current.\n\nHowever, I have some reservations:\n1. It's framed as \"removes manual burden\" which is a productivity argument, not a compliance argument. But that's fine - the effect is still compliance-positive.\n2. The scope seems limited to what's already in experiment tracking tools. If those tools don't capture all required fields (e.g., specific Annex III 1(a) logging requirements), the generator won't fix upstream gaps.\n3. It doesn't explicitly address the \"automatic recording of events over the lifetime\" requirement - it assumes logs exist and just formats them.\n\nBut overall, from a compliance perspective, this is a clearly beneficial investment. It directly supports