ed, etc. But this is likely less than the ongoing manual documentation burden.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is a well-targeted investment. It uses existing infrastructure (the data already exists in our tools), addresses a recurring regulatory cost that will hit every high-risk system we build, and has moderate upfront cost with long-tail savings. The main risk is scope creep or overpromising on full automation for narrative elements. But the core value proposition — reducing manual documentation burden by auto-extracting structured data from tools engineers already use — is sound and efficient.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this. It's a pragmatic, infrastructure-leveraging solution to a real recurring cost. The build effort is bounded, the ROI is clear if we have multiple high-risk systems in our portfolio, and it doesn't compete with scientific research resources (it's engineering tooling).\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's a good use of resources, leverages existing infrastructure well, addresses a real need. Deductions for: the degree to which \"automated\" is achievable for all documentation elements (some narrative content can't be auto-generated), potential rework as regulatory specifications evolve, and the moderate but non-trivial build effort. It's not a 90+ because there's meaningful uncertainty about how much of Recital 71's scope is truly automatable from structured data alone.\n\nLet me settle on 75.\n",
