        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2487\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises the core Article 12 obligation (automatic logging over the system's lifetime) as a hard lifecycle gate, and requiring a technical demonstration rather than a paper attestation ensures the logging capability is genuinely functional before any user-facing interaction—protecting us from enforcement exposure under the Act and preserving market credibility. The main gap I see is that it frames compliance as a single pre-beta checkpoint whereas Article 12(1) speaks to logging 'over the lifetime of the system,' so ongoing re-verification will need to sit alongside this gate; additionally, the Annex III 1(a) minimums in Article 12(3) (period-of-use timestamps, reference-database identification, match input data, verifier identity) should be explicitly enumerated in the checklist rather than left implicit under 'Article 12 requirements.' These are refinements to the implementation plan, not structural flaws in the control itself.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 85\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Compliance Officer of an R&D institution subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2487 describes a \"Regulatory Traceability Audit Protocol\" — essentially a mandatory compliance gate in the project lifecycle requiring record-keeping sign-off before any high-risk system moves to beta testing. Let me assess this carefully.\n\n**What it proposes:**\n1. A mandatory compliance gate integrated into the project lifecycle\n2. Formal record-keeping sign-off before beta testing\n3. Verification checklist based on Recital 71 and Article 12 requirements\n4. Developers must provide technical demonstrations of logging functions\n5. Institutional approval required for deployment\n6. Purpose: prevent legal risk of releasing non-compliant systems into EU market\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\n\nPros:\n- This directly addresses the core obligation in Article 12 — that high-risk AI systems shall technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs) over their lifetime. Having a gate before deployment ensures this capability exists BEFORE the system goes live, which is critical because once deployed, adding logging retroactively is far harder and riskier.\n- The checklist approach based on Recital 71 and Article 12 is appropriate — it operationalizes the regulatory text into actionable verification steps.\n- Requiring technical demonstrations (not just documentation claims) means we actually verify the logging works, not just that someone says it does. This is important because Article 12(2) requires logging capabilities to enable recording of events relevant for specific purposes (a), (b), and (c). A demonstration ensures these are functional.\n- The gate before beta testing is well-timed — it's early enough to catch deficiencies without being so early that the system isn't sufficiently formed to demonstrate logging. Beta testing means real-world interaction, so having logging in place by then protects both compliance and post-market monitoring (Article 72).\n- \"Institutional survival\" angle: if a non-compliant high-risk AI is deployed in the EU market without proper logging, the institution faces enforcement action under the Act, potential bans, fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices; up to €15M or 3% for other violations), and reputational damage. A pre-deployment gate is a reasonable proportionate control.\n- Market reputation: being known as an institution that rigorously verifies compliance before deployment strengthens trust with EU market partners, regulators, and customers.\n\nCons/Concerns:\n- The proposal says \"before any system moves to beta testing\" — but Article 12 applies to the lifetime of the system. The gate should ensure logging is in place at deployment, but also that it remains functional throughout. However, this isn't really a con for THIS proposal — it's establishing a baseline gate; ongoing monitoring would be separate.\n- \"Before beta testing\" might be too early for some systems where the final logging architecture isn't fully determined until later development stages. But from a compliance risk perspective, earlier is better — if you're doing beta testing with real data/users, you need logging in place to satisfy Article 12(2)(b) post-market monitoring and Article 12(2)(c).\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address the specific requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), which have additional minimum logging requirements. However, it does say \"verification checklist based on Recital 71 and Article 12 requirements,\" which would encompass these if properly implemented.\n- Risk of creating a bureaucratic bottleneck that delays R&D timelines. But as Compliance Officer, my primary duty is to ensure compliance, and this gate is proportionate — it's a sign-off with technical demonstration, not an open-ended review.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a well-targeted, proportionate compliance control that directly operationalizes Article 12 obligations into the project lifecycle. It addresses institutional survival (preventing enforcement action), regulatory compliance (ensuring logging exists before deployment), and market reputation (demonstrating rigorous internal governance). The technical demonstration requirement adds substantive value over a purely paper-based checklist. I would approve this with high confidence from my vantage point.\n\nThe only minor reservation is that \"before beta testing\" could be operationally tight for some projects, but the risk of not having logging in place during any user interaction (even internal beta) is too high to accept. This is a sound compliance control.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: 88 — It's strong and well-aligned with my mandate. I'd dock a few points because it could be more specific about addressing the Article 12(3) minimums for biometric systems explicitly, and \"before beta testing\" as the sole gate point might need supplementing with periodic re-verification during the system lifetime to truly satisfy the \"over the lifetime of the system\" language. But these are refinements, not dealbreakers.\n",
