ow and DVC are standard research tools. If we're already using them for experiment tracking (very likely in an AI R&D lab), adding this metadata tagging is an incremental extension rather than new infrastructure.\n- The \"versioned database system\" language could be interpreted as heavy, but in context it's really about versioning the reference dataset hashes — which DVC handles natively and MLflow can track as parameters/artifacts.\n- This integrates with existing logging (Article 12(1) requires automatic event recording anyway), so it piggybacks on infrastructure we'd need to build regardless.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Automatically\" is doing a lot of work here. Ensuring truly automated tagging at scale across all inference endpoints could be non-trivial, especially if the lab has heterogeneous model serving stacks. But for an R&D institution (not a large-scale production deployment), this is manageable.\n- The scope seems right-sized for Article 12(3)(b) specifically. It doesn't overreach into (a), (c), or (d).\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped, resource-efficient proposal that:\n1. Uses existing/cheap open-source tools (low capex)\n2. Automates what would otherwise be manual compliance work (ongoing opex savings)\n3. Leverages infrastructure we likely already have (MLflow/DVC in research labs)\n4. Has a clear, bounded scope tied to one specific regulatory requirement\n5. Provides dual benefit: compliance AND internal debugging/reproducibility\n\nFrom my vantage point on resource efficiency and using existing infrastructure well, this is a strong proposal. It's not trying to build something novel or ambitious — it's applying standard tooling to a specific compliance gap in the most cost-effective way possible. The \"promise\" should be high because it's exactly the kind of efficient, incremental compliance work that makes sense for a resource-constrained R&D lab.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. It's well-scoped, uses existing tools