new teams don't spend weeks figuring out what to log and how. They plug in the library and are done. This accelerates prototyping cycles.\n- Con: \"Mandatory requirement for all project milestones\" could be a double-edged sword. If the framework is immature or inflexible early on, it becomes a gate that blocks progress. If a team is prototyping a novel architecture where the standard telemetry doesn't quite fit, they're blocked until the framework is extended.\n- Con: A single central API creates a dependency. If the library has bugs or needs updates, all teams are affected simultaneously. This is a coupling risk for prototyping speed.\n- Pro: Reducing \"redundant engineering overhead\" directly serves my domain — fewer hours spent reinventing logging in each project means more time on actual model development and testing.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- A unified framework ensures consistency in what gets logged, which helps with debugging across projects. If a compliance audit happens, we can pull logs from all systems using the same query interface.\n- The risk is over-engineering: building a \"grand unified\" logging system before we've validated what each specific AI pipeline actually needs could waste significant engineering time on abstractions that don't fit.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a fundamentally sound engineering approach. A shared logging library is technically viable, well within our capabilities, and addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) elegantly. The main risks are around flexibility (different model architectures need different telemetry) and the mandatory-milestone coupling which could slow prototyping if the framework isn't sufficiently extensible. But these are manageable design challenges rather than fundamental viability problems.\n\nFrom my vantage point: I'd approve this because it directly reduces redundant engineering work across projects, gives teams a standard to integrate against (faster onboarding), and is techn