idecar containers\" — technically sound approach that doesn't intrude on model logic. This means the logging persists even if model teams iterate quickly on their core logic, reducing the risk of a prototype going live without proper logging.\n6. \"Decouples regulatory logging from core model logic\" — this is excellent for compliance because it means logging can't be accidentally (or deliberately) bypassed during rapid prototyping. It's architecturally enforced rather than dependent on individual developer discipline.\n7. \"Preserves prototyping speed\" — from a market reputation and institutional survival standpoint, if the compliance burden makes R&D impossibly slow, we lose competitive edge. This design acknowledges that tension and resolves it well.\n\n**Concerns/Gaps:**\n1. The proposal mentions \"system events, timestamps, and input metadata\" but doesn't explicitly address all of Article 12(3)'s specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification): reference database, match results, natural persons involved in verification. However, this is a middleware/framework proposal — the specifics would be implemented per system type. The \"regardless of their specific architecture\" language suggests it's meant to be extensible.\n2. \"Across all high-risk prototypes\" — this is actually a strength from compliance perspective (over-inclusion is safer than under-inclusion during classification uncertainty), but also a resource commitment concern. However, as Compliance Officer, I'd rather err on the side of over-logging.\n3. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention log retention periods or access controls for regulatory authorities. But that's implementation detail.\n4. \"Immutable ledger\" — good for integrity but need to consider data protection (GDPR) implications of storing all input data immutably. However, as Compliance Officer focused on AI Act specifically, this is a manageable cross-cutting concern.\n\n**From my specific vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n- This is a foundational infrastructure investment that directly implements the core obligation of Article 12(1). Without it, we simply cannot comply. It's not optional.\n- The \"by default\" aspect is critical — if logging is opt-in per team, there will be gaps. Default-on ensures institutional-level compliance posture.\n- Decoupling from model logic means that even during rapid iteration or when teams are under pressure to ship, the compliance layer persists. This protects against the most common failure mode: engineers bypassing logging for speed.\n- Market reputation: If we're ever audited by a market surveillance authority (Article 74) or involved in an incident investigation, having a consistent, immutable, automated logging trail across ALL systems demonstrates robust governance. This is far better than piecemeal per-project logging that might have gaps.\n- The sidecar pattern is well-understood in infrastructure terms and doesn't require retraining any engineers on exotic technology.\n\nThis is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It addresses the core Article 12 obligation at an architectural level, ensures consistency (critical for institutional compliance posture), protects against human error/bypass, and maintains operational velocity. I would approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is very promising from a compliance standpoint. The main reservations are that it's a framework/infrastructure proposal rather than a complete implementation of all Article 12(3) specifics, and there are data protection considerations with the immutable ledger. But as a foundational piece, it's exactly what I'd want to see. I'd give it around 85-90. Let me settle on 87 — strong approval, minor reservations about scope completeness regarding the specific Annex III requirements and data retention policies that would need to be layered on top.\n",
