e. From a compliance perspective, we need not just detection but also documentation of what was done in response to alerts. The proposal doesn't mention the workflow after an alert is triggered (e.g., escalation procedures, documentation of responses).\n- The proposal focuses heavily on \"performance drift\" and \"unexpected input patterns.\" Article 12(2)(a) specifically mentions situations that may result in a risk under Article 79(1) or a substantial modification. Performance drift could be one indicator, but the scope of what constitutes a \"risk situation\" is broader than just performance drift. The proposal might be too narrow in its focus.\n- However, as a compliance officer, I should note that having SOME automated monitoring capability is better than none. Article 12(1) requires the system to \"technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs)\" — this proposal goes beyond just logging to active analysis, which strengthens our position with regulators.\n- From an institutional survival standpoint: if we're found non-compliant because we had logs but no active monitoring capability, that's a regulatory risk. Having this system in place demonstrates proactive compliance posture.\n- Market reputation: being able to demonstrate real-time anomaly detection would be favorable in any market surveillance interaction with EU authorities (Article 72 post-market monitoring).\n\n**Key question from my vantage point:** Does this proposal, as described, actually help me meet my compliance obligations? \n\nYes, it does — but with caveats. The proposal is a solid foundation for what Article 12(2)(a) requires in terms of risk identification through logs. However:\n1. It's somewhat narrow (focused on \"performance drift\" and \"unexpected input patterns\" rather than the full breadth of Article 79(1) risks).\n2. It doesn't address the documentation/traceability aspect — what happens after an alert? How is the response logged for traceability?\n3. The ML-based monitors themselves need to be validated — we can't have unvalidated AI making compliance-relevant decisions without that itself being a risk.\n\nBut overall, from a compliance perspective, this is a clearly aligned and valuable initiative. It directly implements what the regulation requires (automatic logging + analysis for risk detection). The proposal doesn't overreach or go off-track; it's squarely within Article 12's requirements. The fact that it's described as \"developing\" something means it commits resources, but the regulatory alignment is strong.\n\nAs a compliance officer:\n- This is not a \"nice to have\" — having automated risk detection from logs is effectively required by the combination of Articles 12(1), 12(2)(a), and Article 79 (risk management). Without it, we'd be doing manual log review which is not scalable and doesn't demonstrate adequate technical capability.\n- The \"market reputation\" angle is valid: demonstrating proactive compliance to authorities under post-market monitoring (Article 72) strengthens our position.\n- I would approve this but note that the implementation must cover the full scope of Article 12(2)(a), not just performance drift, and must include documentation workflows for alert responses.\n\nMy decision: APPROVE. This is directly aligned with regulatory requirements and supports institutional survival (avoiding non-compliance findings) and market reputation (demonstrating robust post-market monitoring).\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's well-aligned but the description is somewhat narrow in scope and doesn't address the full compliance workflow (what happens after alerts, documentation of responses, validation of the ML monitors themselves). It's a strong foundation but not a complete compliance solution on its own. I'll go with 80.\n",
