        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Forensic Interpretability for Post-Market Monitoring\" - a research project to reconstruct model decision paths using sparse logs required by Article 12, applying counterfactual analysis and attention mapping to existing log data to identify risk triggers, with publication in top-tier AI venues.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n- The core assumption is that \"sparse logs\" (as required by Article 12) contain enough information to reconstruct a model's decision path via counterfactual analysis and attention mapping. This is highly questionable. Article 12 requires logging of events like start/end times, reference databases, input data for matches, and identification of natural persons in verification. These are operational/audit logs, not model internals. Attention mapping requires access to the model's internal activations at inference time - you can't reconstruct attention from \"when the system was used\" or \"which database was queried.\"\n- Counterfactual analysis on log data is a stretch. You'd need to be able to re-run the model with modified inputs and observe different outputs, which is not what Article 12 logs capture. The logs record events (what happened), not the internal computational trace.\n- The proposal conflates two very different things: compliance logging (recording operational events) and interpretability research (understanding how a model arrived at a decision). Article 12's logging requirement is about auditability of system operation, not about capturing enough information to reverse-engineer model reasoning.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Concerns:**\n- This is framed as a \"research project\" with publication goals in \"top-tier AI venues.\" That's an