ed to know what constitutes a \"risk threshold.\"\n- The phrase \"turns compliance monitoring into a scientific study\" is slightly concerning - I don't want compliance to be treated as an experiment where we're still figuring things out. But the deliverable (probes that trigger logs) is operational, not theoretical.\n- \"Pushes the knowledge frontier of AI safety\" - this is nice for reputation but my primary concern is whether it actually satisfies the letter and spirit of Article 12.\n\n**Key question: Does this clear my bar?**\n\nMy bar as Compliance Officer: Does this proposal, if implemented, meaningfully advance our ability to comply with Article 12 in a way that protects institutional survival, ensures regulatory compliance, and supports market reputation?\n\nArticle 12(1) requires automatic recording of events. This proposal creates the detection logic (when to log) rather than the logging mechanism itself (the pipe). But it says \"trigger specific logs\" - so it's building the trigger conditions. The actual log storage/transmission would presumably already exist or be a separate system.\n\nArticle 12(2)(a) requires identifying situations that may result in risk. This is EXACTLY what drift detection toward risk thresholds does. You can't just record everything and hope for the best; you need to know when something relevant is happening.\n\nThe \"synthetic edge-case generators\" are particularly valuable from a compliance standpoint because they allow us to TEST our logging capabilities before real incidents occur. We can verify that our systems would actually catch the events Article 12 requires us to log. This is essentially a self-audit mechanism.\n\nThe integration into post-market monitoring (Article 72) is critical - without this, logs are just data sitting in a database. Integration means the logs feed into actual monitoring decisions.\n\n**Potential issues:**\n- \"Research initiative\" framing: If this is purely exploratory and doesn't commit to depl