ut as a foundational infrastructure piece, it's clearly necessary and moves in the right direction.\n\nFrom institutional survival: Without automatic logging capability, we cannot demonstrate compliance to regulators. This is table-stakes.\nFrom regulatory compliance: Directly implements Art 12(1) requirement for automatic recording. Covers parts of 12(3). Gaps exist but it's a solid foundation.\nFrom market reputation: Having a standardized, automated compliance layer demonstrates institutional maturity and readiness to deploy AI systems in the EU market.\n\nI would approve this. It's not perfect (has gaps), but it's clearly necessary infrastructure that addresses the core obligation. The promise score should reflect strong alignment with compliance needs while acknowledging some scope gaps. I'd put it around 82-85. It's very promising from my vantage point because it directly implements a mandatory technical requirement, but I'm slightly held back by the fact that it seems focused on training/deployment pipelines rather than full lifetime (including post-market operation), and doesn't explicitly address log integrity, retention, or the risk-event identification requirements of 12(2).\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
