ct ensures documentation stays current, which is what Recital 71 explicitly requires (\"kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system\")\n\nHowever, I should note some concerns:\n- Building an automated pipeline itself has upfront cost — it's not free\n- The scope of \"comprehensive reports required by Recital 71\" is broad; a Git metadata tag approach may only capture code and dataset changes but might miss training logs, testing results, risk-management documentation\n- It says \"replaces manual reporting cycles\" which is ambitious — some documentation (e.g., risk management narratives, intended purpose descriptions) won't be auto-generable from code tags alone\n\nBut the core idea is sound from a resource-allocation perspective: leveraging existing infrastructure to automate a recurring compliance burden. The upfront investment in building the pipeline should pay back quickly through reduced ongoing administrative effort. It aligns well with effective use of existing R&D infrastructure (Git repos are already there; we're adding a layer on top).\n\nThe proposal is reasonably scoped and addresses a real, recurring cost center. It doesn't require new hardware or exotic tooling — it's software engineering work that can be done by our existing team. The promise from my vantage point is high: it directly attacks the \"funding spent on paperwork rather than research\" problem while leveraging infrastructure we already have.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. It's a clear resource-allocation improvement with manageable implementation cost relative to ongoing savings. I'll approve it with a high promise score.\n\nLet me settle on a promise: From my vantage point, this is very aligned. It uses existing infrastructure (Git), reduces recurring overhead, frees researcher time. The main risk is scope creep or underestimating the build effort, but the core concept is sound and well-motivated. I'd put it at around 82-85. Not a perfect 90+ because Recital 7