h, they import and decorate.\n- Consistent data formats reduce integration bugs during prototyping (e.g., when you need to swap one experiment's logs into an audit pipeline).\n- The \"prevents reinventing the wheel\" argument is strong from a resource perspective — with scarce implementation resources, not every team can afford to build logging infrastructure.\n- Risk: if the SDK is too rigid or premature, it could slow prototyping (fighting the framework instead of using it). But a well-designed decorator-based approach should be non-intrusive.\n- The scope seems right for an internal tool — Python package, decorators, wrappers. Not trying to build a full observability platform.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Across different framework backends\" could balloon scope. If it tries to support every possible backend, it becomes a maintenance nightmare. But as an initial version targeting the lab's primary 2-3 frameworks, it's very viable.\n- The specific Article 12(3) requirements (reference database, match data for biometric verification) are hard to generically capture via decorators — you'd need domain-specific hooks. But the SDK can provide extension points for those.\n- \"Consistent data formats for auditability\" is a real engineering benefit that reduces downstream pain.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a technically sound, well-scoped internal tooling proposal. It directly addresses a real prototyping bottleneck (every project building its own logging). The decorator/wrapper pattern in Python is mature and low-risk to implement. It would accelerate time-to-compliance-prototype for new high-risk projects. The main risk is scope creep with \"different framework backends,\" but that's manageable with iterative development. From my vantage point, this is a strong enabler for fast, consistent prototyping of compliant systems.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly — maybe 78-82. It's not a 90+ because the \"across different framework backends\" ambition introduces some techni