        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2660 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Formalizing Traceability Metrics for Complex AI Systems\" — a theoretical research project that treats logging as an information theory problem, aims to define minimum sufficient logs for diagnosing high-risk failures, conducts comparative studies on failure modes, maps event types to regulatory risk categories, and publishes papers establishing the lab as a scientific leader.\n\nLet me assess this from my engineering/prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical viability concerns:**\n- This is explicitly framed as a \"theoretical research project\" — it's not building anything. It's about defining metrics, conducting comparative studies, mapping event types, and publishing papers. There's no prototype, no implementation, no system being built or improved.\n- From a development engineering standpoint, this doesn't produce a shippable artifact. It produces papers and frameworks. The actual work of implementing logging that satisfies Article 12 would still need to be done by someone else using the outputs (if they're even actionable).\n- \"Information theory problem\" framing is interesting intellectually but extremely abstract for what Article 12 actually requires. The regulation asks for specific, practical logging capabilities (start/end times, reference databases, input data, identification of natural persons). Treating this as an information theory problem risks over-intellectualizing a fairly concrete engineering requirement.\n- \"Comparative studies on failure modes in existing high-risk systems\" — this is research that requires access to other organizations' system logs and failure data, which may be difficult to obtain at scale. The practical utility of these findings for our own prototyping pipeline is questionable.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping concerns:**\n- This project does not accelerate or improve our ability to prototype AI systems with compliant logging. It's a years-long theoretical endeavor that will produce publications, not working code or design patterns we can use tomorrow.\n- The \"minimum sufficient set of logs\" framing is actually somewhat useful conceptually — if someone could define what the absolute minimum logging schema looks like for different risk categories, that would inform our implementation. But getting there through \"information theory\" and \"comparative studies on failure modes\" is a very long, uncertain path with no guaranteed engineering output.\n- The project's stated goal is to \"establish our lab as a scientific leader in defining the global standards\" — this is a positioning/branding goal, not an engineering one. It doesn't directly serve our function of building working systems quickly and well.\n\n**What it could offer (charitable reading):**\n- A theoretically grounded framework for log granularity could inform design decisions about what to log vs. what's overkill (performance impact) or underlogging (compliance risk).\n- Mapping event types to regulatory risk categories could produce a useful reference document for engineers implementing logging.\n- However, these are research outputs with uncertain timelines and uncertain applicability to our specific systems.\n\n**Verdict from my vantage point:**\nThis is fundamentally a theoretical/research project with no direct path to producing working prototypes or improving our prototyping speed/quality. It's intellectually interesting but misaligned with what my function needs. The \"information theory\" framing risks being academically elegant but practically useless for the concrete engineering task of implementing Article 12 compliance in our systems. I would reject this from a development engineering perspective — it doesn't produce artifacts I can build with, doesn't accelerate delivery, and its outputs (papers) are not what my team needs to ship compliant logging features.\n\nHowever, I'll give it a moderate promise score because the underlying question (what's the minimum sufficient log set?) is genuinely relevant to engineering decisions, even if this particular project's approach to answering it is too abstract and slow for my needs. Maybe 25-35 range? It has some conceptual relevance but no practical engineering output I can act on in any reasonable timeframe.\n\nI'll go with:\n- Rationale: Theoretical research producing papers doesn't yield implementable logging architectures or accelerate prototyping; the information-theory framing is too abstract for the concrete engineering task of implementing Article 12's specific logging requirements (timestamps, reference DBs, input data, person IDs).\n- Decision: reject\n- Promise: 28 — there's a kernel of useful thinking in \"minimum sufficient logs\" but the project as scoped won't produce anything my team can build with in any realistic timeframe.\n",
