d\" which maps to Art 12(3)(b) and (c), and \"identification of natural persons involved in verification\" which maps to Art 12(3)(d). Good alignment.\n3. However, it seems to miss Art 12(3)(a) - recording the period of each use (start/end date and time). The text says \"logs the specific reference databases used and captures the digital identity of natural persons verifying results\" but doesn't explicitly mention session duration logging. Wait, let me re-read... It says \"focuses on the precise record-keeping requirements of Article 12, paragraph three\" - so it claims to cover all of 3(a)-(d). But the specific examples given only mention (b) and (d). This is a gap but could be an oversight in the description rather than in scope.\n4. The \"mandatory gate in deployment pipeline\" is good for compliance - ensures no system goes live without the logging capability built in.\n5. From a compliance perspective, this is exactly what regulators will expect to see as evidence of Article 12(3) compliance for biometric systems. Without it, you're non-compliant by design.\n\n**Risk assessment:**\n- If we have Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric ID), this module is legally mandatory - not optional. The Act requires these systems to \"technically allow for\" these recordings.\n- Non-compliance means potential fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover.\n- Market reputation: deploying biometric AI in the EU without proper traceability would be a massive reputational hit, especially with regulators and data protection authorities watching closely.\n\n**Institutional survival lens:**\nThis is not optional for any Annex III 1(a) system we deploy. It's a hard regulatory requirement. Building this module is a compliance necessity, not an exploration of \"whether an idea might someday work.\" The resources are justified because without it, we cannot legally deploy these systems in the EU market.\n\n**Market reputation lens:**\nHaving a dedicated traceability module signals to regulators