stem from scratch; it's extending what we already have.\n\n6. Resource allocation: The upfront cost is moderate (a middleware layer, an API), but the ongoing maintenance and the fact that it covers ALL high-risk experiments means amortization works in its favor over time. For an R&D institution that will continue developing AI systems, this is a good long-term investment.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a well-scoped proposal that leverages existing infrastructure (the ML pipeline) to solve both regulatory and scientific problems simultaneously. It's the kind of \"build once, use many\" approach that makes sense for resource-constrained R&D labs. The dual purpose (compliance + reproducibility) means it isn't pure compliance overhead — researchers get tangible benefits too, which aids adoption. The main risk is scope creep (\"all high-risk AI experiments\") and whether the team can deliver the middleware without diverting too many senior engineers from actual research. But as a framework that plugs into existing infrastructure rather than replacing it, it's efficient.\n\nI'd approve this. It's resource-efficient (one integration point), leverages existing infrastructure, serves dual purposes, and scales with our portfolio of high-risk AI systems. The promise score should be relatively high — maybe 82-85. It's not a 100 because the \"effortless\" claim is optimistic and there are implementation risks around scope, but from a resource-allocation standpoint it's the right architectural approach.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 83.\n",
