        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2140 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"High-Risk System Certification Protocol\" — a mandatory institutional review process that verifies logging capabilities before any system is labeled production-ready, integrating a compliance checklist into project milestones with legal sign-off, creating an internal paper trail for external regulatory audits.\n\nLet me assess this from my domain of concern: technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\n**Rationale considerations:**\n\n1. **Technical viability**: The proposal is essentially a process/governance mechanism (checklist + sign-off), not a technical solution per se. It doesn't describe HOW to implement the logging capabilities required by Article 12(1) — it only describes when to verify they exist before production. From a development engineering standpoint, this is a gate in the workflow, not an engineering solution. It's technically trivial to implement (a checklist and a sign-off step), so viability isn't really at issue. However, it doesn't address the actual technical challenge of building automatic event recording over system lifetime.\n\n2. **Speed/quality of prototyping**: This is where I have concerns. Adding a mandatory legal sign-off gate before \"production-ready\" labeling adds process overhead. During prototyping phases, this could slow things down if not carefully scoped. However, the proposal specifically targets the \"project milestone phase\" and \"production-ready\" designation — meaning it's not necessarily blocking early prototyping iterations. It's a pre-production gate.\n\n3. **Relevance to Article 12**: The proposal addresses traceability of compliance (paper trail for audits), which aligns with Recital 71's emphasis on traceability. But it doesn't directly address the technical implementation of automatic logging. It's more of a