 approach is actually good engineering practice — it means documentation isn't a separate silo but part of the development workflow.\n\n**Quality of prototyping:**\n- Real-time documentation could improve quality because decisions are captured when they're made, reducing context loss.\n- Dataset provenance tracking is genuinely valuable for debugging and reproducibility.\n- However, if compliance checks become blocking gates at milestones, this could create bottlenecks where a well-performing prototype gets held up waiting for documentation to be \"complete enough.\"\n\n**Alignment with Article 12 / Recital 71:**\n- The proposal directly addresses the technical documentation requirement in Recital 71: \"The technical documentation should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\"\n- It also touches on recording training data, validation processes — all explicitly mentioned in the recital.\n- However, Article 12's core requirement is about automatic logging of events (logs). This proposal is more about technical documentation (which is covered under Article 11/Annex IV) rather than the automatic event logging that Article 12 specifically mandates. But Recital 71 does connect both, so it's in-scope for the broader record-keeping obligation.\n\n**My professional assessment:**\nFrom a development engineering perspective:\n- The technical solution (version-controlled vault + milestone gates) is viable and not exotic. Low risk of failure.\n- The speed impact is real but manageable if scoped to sprint-level rather than per-commit documentation.\n- The quality benefit is genuine — provenance tracking catches errors earlier.\n- The main risk is that \"mandatory\" reporting combined with compliance gates could become a bureaucratic tax on prototyping velocity, especially for exploratory work where the end state isn't known yet.\n\nThis is a solid, technically feasible proposal that addresses a real regulatory need. It's not innovative but it