tification of natural persons in verification)? The proposal seems to hand-wave these.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A standardized middleware COULD speed up prototyping by removing boilerplate logging code from each project. This is a genuine benefit for development velocity.\n- However, building a \"universal\" middleware is itself a significant engineering investment. If the lab has diverse architectures (different ML frameworks, different deployment targets), building one middleware that works across all of them could be very expensive upfront.\n- The risk: if you build this before you understand the full scope of what each system needs to log (which depends on which Annex III category it falls under), you'll need significant rework. Prototyping speed would actually SUFFER during the initial build-out period.\n- \"Without manual coding\" — in practice, even with middleware, each system will need configuration specific to its event types. The \"zero manual coding\" claim is optimistic.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n1. Scope creep risk: \"all lab experiments\" and \"universal\" is a very broad commitment of engineering resources. This is exactly the kind of proposal that sounds great but becomes a bottomless pit.\n2. Mismatch between training pipelines and operational logging: Article 12 is about runtime/inference events, not training. Integrating into \"training pipelines\" may be targeting the wrong layer.\n3. The technical approach is sound in principle (event capture middleware), but the execution scope as described (\"universal,\" \"plug-and-play,\" \"all lab experiments\") makes it a large, risky engineering bet rather than a focused prototyping accelerator.\n4. For speed of prototyping specifically: if I'm a researcher trying to prototype a new high-risk system quickly, I need logging that's relevant to MY specific Annex III category and MY specific architecture. A universal middleware might actually slow me down if it requires me to conform t