oring triggers) is a meaningful gap that existing generic ML tooling doesn't fill. The regulatory-specific layer on top of existing infrastructure could be efficient.\n- Resource concern: \"real-time technical documentation generation\" for \"model development, training, and validation\" across the full R&D phase is a substantial ongoing engineering commitment. This isn't a one-off build; it's an operational system that requires maintenance as models evolve. In a lab with funding constraints, this is a recurring cost center.\n- The proposal frames it as mitigating \"institutional failure during regulatory audits\" - which is the right incentive, but the implementation scope could be over-engineered for what the regulation actually requires at our stage of development.\n\n**Decision considerations:**\n- It's directionally correct and addresses real Article 12 obligations.\n- But from a resource-allocation standpoint, it reads as a large, sweeping infrastructure project rather than an efficient incremental addition to existing tooling. The \"real-time\" and \"entire lifecycle\" language suggests heavy engineering investment.\n- A more efficient approach might be: identify what logging/versioning we already have, add the specific Article 12 fields (start/end times of use, reference database references, input data for matches, identification of verification personnel per Annex III point 1(a)), and build a thin compliance layer on top rather than a full \"dynamic pipeline.\"\n- However, I should judge this proposal on its own merits. It does propose something that aligns with the regulation and would improve our position. The question is whether it's efficient enough given our constraints.\n\nI think this clears my bar but isn't a slam-dunk 100. It's directionally right but potentially over-scoped from an efficiency perspective. I'd approve it because:\n- It addresses genuine Article 12 obligations we must meet anyway\n- The compliance dashboard and automatic logging are