osal against these specific provisions.\n\n**Assessment from Compliance Officer vantage point:**\n\nPros:\n- A standardized schema could help ensure documentation is kept in a consistent, \"clear and comprehensive form\" as required by Recital 71.\n- Machine-readable format (JSON-LD) could facilitate regulatory audits and make it easier to verify compliance with the requirements.\n- Aligning EU requirements with scientific reporting standards is sensible for an R&D institution — it bridges two worlds.\n- \"Interchangeable between researchers and regulators\" supports the traceability goal of Recital 71.\n- Reduces administrative friction, which matters for institutional survival (cost management).\n\nCons:\n- The proposal is about documentation schema/ontology, not about the actual logging capability required by Article 12(1)-(3). It doesn't address automatic recording of events, logging of start/end times, reference databases, input data matching, or identification of natural persons.\n- It's somewhat abstract — \"develop a JSON-LD schema\" is a design task, not an implementation commitment. The actual compliance obligation under Article 12 is to technically allow for automatic logging, which requires engineering work on the AI system itself.\n- However, Recital 71 does explicitly mention \"keeping records and the availability of technical documentation\" as part of the record-keeping framework. So a standardized schema IS relevant to the broader record-keeping obligation.\n- The proposal is reasonable but somewhat generic — it's a good idea in principle but doesn't specifically address the logging requirements of Article 12(1)-(3).\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This supports the documentation/record-keeping aspect (Recital 71) but is only peripherally related to the specific logging obligations in Article 12(1)-(3).\n- It's a reasonable, low-risk initiative that would improve our posture.\n- However, it's not sufficient on its own for compliance with Art