archers don't have to think about logging during experiments, prototyping cycles are faster. You avoid the classic problem where compliance is bolted on at the end (expensive and error-prone).\n  - CON: \"Standardized hooks into base model templates\" could slow down prototyping if the template becomes too rigid. Research prototypes often need to deviate from standard pipelines quickly. If every experiment must go through a standardized logging layer, it adds overhead to the iteration loop. However, if the hooks are lightweight and non-blocking (async logging), this impact is minimal.\n  - CON: Building a \"unified\" framework that covers ALL high-risk AI projects across the lab is a significant upfront investment. During the prototyping phase, you often don't know what logging points you'll need until later. Over-standardizing too early can lock in suboptimal designs.\n  - PRO: Once built, it reduces per-project setup time significantly.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- A unified framework means consistent log formats, which improves downstream analysis and compliance audits.\n- However, \"one size fits all\" logging across diverse AI systems (NLP models, computer vision, decision-support systems) is challenging. Article 12(3) specifies different minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification) vs. other high-risk systems. A truly unified framework needs to be parameterizable per system type.\n- The proposal doesn't mention handling the specific requirements of Article 12(2)(a)-(c) or 12(3)(a)-(d). It speaks generally about \"timestamps, input data, and system events\" which maps loosely but doesn't explicitly address the identification of natural persons in verification (Art. 14(5)), reference databases, etc.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea is technically sound and a standard engineering approach to this regulatory requirement. The pattern of embedding compliance into infrastructure rather than relying on manual processes is c