        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1878\",\n      \"rationale\": \"While the direction aligns with Article 12's traceability objectives and Recital 71's call for comprehensible lifetime information, this is an open-ended R&D exploration ('semantic insight,' 'causal tracing,' a unilateral 'new standard') rather than a scoped compliance deliverable that ensures our high-risk systems technically allow automatic event logging within required timelines; committing scarce implementation resources to a project with no defined Article 12(1)–(3) delivery milestone, and whose reputation payoff ('competitive advantage', 'publish as a new standard') is contingent on multi-year research outcomes we cannot guarantee, creates an unacceptable gap in our baseline compliance posture. From a market-reputation standpoint too, unilaterally publishing a self-declared 'standard for AI accountability' risks contradicting CEN/CENELEC harmonised standards or supervisory guidance, which would damage credibility with authorities and counterparties rather than enhance it.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 42\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1878 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer whose domain is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Advanced Traceability and AI Forensics Project\" — an R&D initiative to develop next-generation logging with semantic insight into model decision-making, going beyond basic event recording toward full forensic transparency. It involves researching methods for linking low-level system logs with high-level causal tracing and interpretability tools, publishing findings as a new standard for AI accountability, positioning it as transforming regulatory constraint into competitive advantage.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance vantage point:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Article 12 requires \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This proposal goes well beyond minimum compliance toward deeper traceability.\n- Recital 71 emphasizes \"comprehensible information on how high-risk AI systems have been developed and how they perform throughout their lifetime\" and \"traceability of those systems, verify compliance with the requirements under this Regulation, as well as monitoring of their operations.\"\n- The proposal addresses Article 12(2)(a), (b), and (c) — identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operation.\n- From a market reputation standpoint, positioning as a leader in AI accountability could be valuable for the institution's standing with regulators and clients.\n- \"Transforms a regulatory constraint into a competitive advantage\" — this is good strategic framing from a compliance perspective; being ahead of requirements rather than just meeting them reduces risk of enforcement action.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal is vague: \"next-generation logging that provides semantic insight into model decision-making\" and \"full forensic transparency\" are aspirational but not specific. As a Compliance Officer, I need to know what exactly will be built and when.\n- \"Research methods for linking low-level system logs with high-level causal tracing and interpretability tools\" — this is R&D, not implementation. The lab has scarce implementation resources. This is an exploration project, not a compliance deliverable. Article 12 requires that systems \"technically allow\" for automatic recording NOW (or when the system is placed on market). A research project doesn't deliver a compliant logging capability in any defined timeframe.\n- \"We will publish these findings as a new standard for AI accountability\" — publishing a \"new standard\" is ambitious and potentially risky. If the published standard doesn't align with what regulators ultimately expect, it could create confusion or even conflict. Also, standards development is typically done through CEN/CENELEC or other recognized bodies, not unilaterally by a single R&D lab.\n- The proposal conflates \"compliance\" (meeting Article 12 requirements) with \"going beyond.\" Article 12 has specific minimums (especially paragraph 3 for Annex III point 1(a) systems). A research project into \"forensic transparency\" doesn't directly deliver those minimums. You still need the basic logging infrastructure in place.\n- The scope is very broad: \"semantic insight,\" \"causal tracing,\" \"interpretability tools\" — these are major R&D areas with uncertain timelines and outcomes. Committing scarce resources to this without a clear compliance deliverable timeline is risky from an institutional survival standpoint.\n\n**Compliance-specific assessment:**\n- Does it directly help meet Article 12? Partially — it's in the right direction (logging, traceability) but it's a research project, not a compliance implementation. The article requires that systems \"shall technically allow\" for automatic recording. This proposal doesn't say when or how it will deliver that capability.\n- Is there a risk of non-compliance if we pursue this instead of straightforward Article 12 logging? Yes — if resources are diverted to a research project while the basic compliance logging isn't implemented, that's a gap.\n- Market reputation: The \"competitive advantage\" framing is appealing but premature. If the research fails or takes years, the reputational benefit doesn't materialize, and we've spent scarce resources without meeting our baseline obligations.\n\n**Decision:** This is a well-intentioned direction but as an R&D initiative it's too vague and too far removed from actual compliance delivery to approve with my scarce implementation resources. The basic Article 12 logging requirements (automatic event recording, the specific minimums in paragraph 3) need concrete engineering projects, not open-ended research into \"semantic insight\" and \"forensic transparency.\" However, the direction is aligned with regulatory expectations and could be a valuable long-term investment IF it were scoped more tightly.\n\nI'll reject it because:\n1. It's an R&D exploration, not a compliance implementation — my function commits resources to ensure we meet obligations, not to explore future possibilities.\n2. The scope is too broad and undefined (\"next-generation,\" \"full forensic transparency,\" \"new standard\") to commit scarce resources.\n3. There's no clear timeline or deliverable that maps to Article 12 requirements.\n4. Publishing a \"new standard\" unilaterally creates regulatory risk if it diverges from what authorities expect.\n\nPromise score: The direction is right-aligned with compliance goals (traceability, post-market monitoring), and the reputation angle has merit. But the vagueness and R&D nature keep it moderate. I'd say around 40-50. It's not something I'd \"never back\" (it's in the right spirit) but it's not something I'd back \"without hesitation\" given resource constraints and compliance urgency. I'll go with 42.\n",
