nder-fund others.\n- It's a budgeting mechanism, not an implementation plan. It allocates money but doesn't tell you what to do with it or how to structure the traceability work efficiently.\n- \"Mandatory\" for ALL high-risk AI projects might be too blunt. Some early-stage exploratory projects in the pipeline may not yet be at the stage where full Article 12 compliance is needed (though if they're classified as high-risk, they presumably are).\n- It doesn't address how to leverage existing R&D infrastructure. The lab likely already has logging, version control, and documentation systems that can be repurposed for Article 12 compliance at marginal cost rather than requiring a fresh percentage allocation.\n- From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, a blanket percentage is the least efficient approach compared to project-specific scoping of traceability needs.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe instinct behind this proposal is sound — you need explicit budgeting for compliance so it doesn't quietly starve research. However, the mechanism (a fixed percentage for all projects) is crude from an efficiency standpoint. A smarter approach would be: scope traceability requirements per project based on its specific Article 12 obligations, audit existing infrastructure that can be repurposed at low marginal cost, and then budget the incremental delta rather than applying a blanket tax.\n\nThat said, as a first-order organizational intervention, it's better than doing nothing. It creates accountability and visibility for compliance costs. But I'd want to see it refined into a more nuanced model before committing implementation resources.\n\nFrom my specific vantage point (resource-allocation efficiency, scientific ambition vs. funding, effective use of existing infrastructure):\n- The proposal partially addresses resource allocation but does so in a blunt, inefficient way.\n- It protects scientific ambition in principle but the flat percentage might actually reduce efficiency by over-allocating to simple projects.\n- It ignores the opportunity to leverage existing R&D infrastructure (existing logging pipelines, documentation systems) which could dramatically reduce the actual incremental cost below any \"specific percentage.\"\n\nI'd lean toward rejecting this as stated — it's too crude and misses the key lever of repurposing existing infrastructure. But the underlying instinct is valid enough that I shouldn't give it a zero. Maybe around 35-45? It shows awareness of the problem but proposes an inefficient mechanism that doesn't leverage what we already have.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The instruction says \"approve every proposal that clears your bar and reject every proposal that does not.\" My bar as Lab Director focused on resource-allocation efficiency: does this efficiently allocate scarce resources to meet traceability requirements while protecting scientific ambition? \n\nThe flat percentage approach is NOT the most efficient allocation mechanism. It treats all high-risk projects identically regardless of their actual Article 12(3) obligations or existing infrastructure overlap. A more efficient approach would be project-specific scoping + infrastructure reuse assessment → then targeted budgeting. The proposal as stated commits resources to a blunt instrument rather than an optimized one.\n\nHowever, it does have merit as a governance mechanism — making compliance visible in the funding model is important. Without it, compliance costs become hidden overhead that management can't see or control.\n\nI'll reject it because: (1) the flat percentage is inefficient from my resource-allocation standpoint, (2) it doesn't leverage existing infrastructure (a key concern for me), and (3) as a standalone proposal, it commits resources to updating funding models without first doing the scoping work that would make the budgeting actually accurate. But I'll give it a moderate promise score because the underlying principle is sound eve