., the input data for which a search led to a match). For biometric verification systems specifically, every use must be logged with full detail.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is framed as a \"scientific project\" — research, not engineering. It doesn't specify a deliverable, a timeline, or a concrete prototype. From my vantage point, this would consume prototyping bandwidth without producing a shippable artifact.\n- The problem it addresses (log storage overhead) is real but currently manageable with existing infrastructure (time-series databases, columnar stores, tiered storage). We don't have an acute performance bottleneck that demands novel compression research right now.\n- If we need log compression for scale, we can prototype with existing techniques (delta encoding, dictionary compression, partitioning by time) in days to weeks. We don't need a \"scientific project\" to figure out how to store logs efficiently.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. It's research, not engineering — no concrete deliverable or prototype path is specified.\n2. The core tension it addresses (high-resolution monitoring vs. performance) may be premature for our current scale. We can solve this with standard infrastructure patterns before needing novel compression science.\n3. \"Semantic hashing\" and \"selective sampling\" are hand-wavy as applied to the specific heterogeneous event types in Article 12. The regulatory minimums (especially 12(3)) require specific fields to be recorded — you can't hash away \"the input data for which the search has led to a match.\"\n4. No mention of how the compressed logs would satisfy the specific per-category requirements. Legal traceability isn't a compression target; it's a hard constraint on what must be recoverable.\n\nThis feels like an academically interesting idea that doesn't map well to our immediate engineering needs. The problem is real but solvable with existing tools, and the proposed approach (semantic hashing of heterogeneous regulatory events) has unclear technical feasibility for maintaining the specific data points Article 12(3) mandates. It would be a good research publication but not a viable prototyping commitment.\n\nI'll reject this — it's too vague, too research-oriented, doesn't have a clear path to a working prototype that addresses the specific regulatory requirements, and the problem can be addressed more directly with standard engineering approaches.\n\nPromise score: Maybe 30-40. The underlying concern (log overhead at scale) is legitimate, and semantic compression could eventually help. But as proposed, it's not something I'd commit prototyping resources to.\n",
