l model architectures.\"\n- The primary goal as stated is positioning (\"thought leader in regulatory science\") and \"advancing general AI transparency research\" — this is a scientific ambition that doesn't directly serve our operational mandate.\n- Building a \"public dataset and benchmark\" requires significant engineering effort, data curation, quality assurance, and maintenance over time. Open repositories need ongoing updates.\n- The proposal conflates compliance (which we must do) with publishing research about compliance (which is optional). The resource cost of the latter could be substantial while adding little to our actual compliance posture.\n- \"Define what constitutes a sufficient log for safety verification\" — this is essentially trying to set industry standards, which is a huge undertaking and arguably better suited to regulatory bodies or consortia. We'd be spending scarce resources trying to fill a gap that the regulator (or harmonized standards) will likely address.\n- The open-source commitment creates an ongoing maintenance burden on our infrastructure and staff time.\n\nFrom my perspective as Lab Director focused on resource efficiency: This proposal uses our scarce implementation resources to pursue a thought-leadership/research goal rather than directly advancing our compliance obligations or building reusable internal infrastructure efficiently. It's scientifically interesting but misaligned with the core operational mandate. The \"positioning\" benefit is real but soft; it doesn't save us money or reduce implementation risk in a direct way.\n\nHowever, I should note that if we're already building logging capabilities, there IS some overlap. But the scope here — \"across several model architectures,\" \"public dataset and benchmark,\" \"global research community\" — goes well beyond what compliance requires. It's a significant resource commitment for external-facing research output.\n\nMy decision: reject. The promise score should reflect