on, categorisation, emotion recognition). While important, it doesn't fully address Article 12 for all high-risk systems under Annex I or other Annex III categories. However, the proposal says \"specifically for Annex III\" which is appropriate scope.\n- The phrase \"providing data on human-AI collaboration dynamics\" feels like a stretch beyond compliance — it's more of an R&D curiosity. But this doesn't hurt compliance.\n- It's described as a \"digital interface\" and \"verification portal\" — this implies a user-facing system that requires operational buy-in from the teams using the AI systems. Compliance risk if adoption is inconsistent.\n- The proposal addresses one specific sub-requirement (12(3)(d)) rather than the full logging architecture. But it's scoped appropriately as a component.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nThis directly implements a mandatory requirement for Annex III systems. Without this, we would be non-compliant with Article 12(3)(d). The accountability chain is critical — if a regulator investigates an incident, being able to show who verified what output when is essential. This protects institutional survival (avoiding enforcement action) and market reputation (demonstrating compliance maturity to customers in regulated sectors).\n\nThe proposal is well-scoped to its specific regulatory requirement. It's not overreaching into R&D territory unnecessarily. The \"human-AI collaboration dynamics\" language is slightly aspirational but doesn't detract from the core compliance function.\n\nI would approve this. It addresses a hard regulatory obligation, creates an auditable trail that protects us in enforcement scenarios, and builds trust with regulators and customers. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-85. It's clearly necessary for Annex III compliance, but it's one component of the broader logging architecture, so I'm not giving a perfect 100. There are operational adoption risks (ensuring all verifiers actually use the portal consisten