rly-stage ideation, and potentially chill ambitious projects that might straddle the \"high-risk\" boundary. The proposal doesn't address how this interacts with fundamental research exceptions (Article 2(8)) or how it handles rapidly evolving systems where classification might shift over time.\n\nThe proposal is well-intentioned from a compliance standpoint, but from my research vantage point, it's:\n- Not advancing knowledge\n- Has minimal intellectual merit as a \"research\" activity\n- Could have negative impact on scientific freedom and exploratory work by adding a regulatory gate at design phase\n- Is fundamentally an administrative/legal process masquerading as if it has research value\n\nThe cross-functional committee including \"lead researchers\" is the only thread connecting this to my domain, but being on a compliance classification committee is not a use of research resources that advances science. It's an opportunity cost — researcher time spent classifying regulatory risk rather than pursuing frontier questions.\n\nI would reject this from my vantage point. It doesn't clear the bar for knowledge advancement, intellectual merit, or scientific impact. If anything, it poses a mild threat to research agility by adding a design-phase gate.\n\nPromise score: This is not a proposal that advances science at all. From my perspective, I'd give it a very low score — maybe 5-8. It's not harmful per se (it's reasonable compliance infrastructure), but it has essentially zero connection to knowledge frontier advancement or scientific impact. The slight positive is that getting classification right might free researchers from unnecessary logging overhead on non-high-risk systems, which could marginally help research agility. But the proposal as framed is a bureaucratic process, not a research advance. I'll give it around 5-8.\n",
