r it can truly be \"transparent\" and \"without manual intervention.\"\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping concerns:**\n- Building this middleware itself would consume significant engineering resources upfront before any prototype benefits. This is a substantial infrastructure investment.\n- If it works well, it could genuinely reduce per-project compliance overhead for future prototypes — that's a real speed benefit.\n- But the risk is: you build a generic layer that's too thin to capture what Article 12 actually requires (the semantic events), and then each project still needs custom adapters anyway — at which point you've spent time building middleware AND writing per-project code, which is *slower* than just writing the logging directly.\n- The \"plugs into existing AI frameworks\" claim is vague. How does it plug in? Monkey-patching? Decorator patterns? A sidecar? Each has different implications for prototyping speed and reliability.\n\n**Quality concerns:**\n- A middleware that intercepts calls can introduce latency, instability, or subtle behavioral changes to the AI system under test — problematic for research where you need clean baselines.\n- Debugging becomes harder when there's an extra layer between your code and its behavior.\n- For a R&D lab where prototypes change rapidly (framework versions, new architectures), maintaining compatibility of the middleware is ongoing cost.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe *intent* is correct — separating compliance concerns from research code is good engineering practice. But the specific proposal as stated overpromises on technical feasibility (\"without manual intervention,\" \"transparently across different project architectures\"). The scope of Article 12's requirements, especially paragraph 3 which requires semantically rich logging tied to specific system types, makes a truly transparent, zero-intervention middleware very hard to deliver. There IS value in building shared logging infrastructure, but the proposal as