lly challenging to do automatically with high precision — false positives could be very noisy, false negatives could miss real compliance issues. This is a known hard problem in ML monitoring.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a technically viable, well-understood approach. ELK is battle-tested for this exact use case (log aggregation, anomaly detection, alerting). It directly supports multiple sub-requirements of Article 12. The main concern is that the proposal is somewhat high-level and doesn't specify the AI-specific components in enough detail to assess full technical viability. But the core architecture is sound and deployable.\n\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):\n- Technically viable: Yes, ELK is proven. The AI-telemetry tailoring adds some work but nothing revolutionary.\n- Speed of prototyping: This is an infrastructure/observability tool. It doesn't directly speed up prototyping of AI systems, but it does provide the monitoring layer that Article 12 requires, which would otherwise need to be built anyway. Having a clear, proven architecture (ELK) means less time spent figuring out \"how\" and more time on \"what specifically to monitor.\"\n- Quality: A well-implemented ELK pipeline provides high-quality, queryable, auditable logs — exactly what the regulation demands.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's technically sound, uses proven technology, directly addresses Article 12 requirements (especially 12(2)(a), (b), (c)), and doesn't add significant risk to the development timeline. The main reservation is the vagueness of \"tailored for AI telemetry\" but that's a scoping detail, not a viability issue.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's solid, proven technology applied to a clear regulatory need. Not a 90+ because it's somewhat generic and the AI-specific tailoring is underspecified (which adds prototyping risk in terms of knowing exactly what to build). But it's clearly viable and useful. Let me settle