k prototype. Building an automated pipeline that reliably maps code/data changes to EU AI Act compliance fields would take significant engineering effort.\n- However, the prototyping path is clear: you can start with a minimal version that auto-generates a changelog-style document from git commits and dataset metadata, then iteratively add more structured compliance mapping.\n- The \"metadata tags\" approach means developers need to tag their work appropriately - this introduces process overhead that could slow down prototyping speed if not carefully designed. If the tagging requirement is too burdensome, engineers will either skip it or game it, undermining the system's reliability.\n- For a lab doing rapid prototyping, adding mandatory compliance metadata tags to every commit could be seen as friction. But if done well (automated inference of what changed, minimal manual tagging), it could actually speed up audit preparation.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe concept is technically viable and the direction is right - automating documentation generation from existing MLOps infrastructure is a sensible approach. However, the framing of \"instantaneous generation\" overstates what's achievable with simple metadata tags. The real technical challenge is the semantic gap between \"code changed in file X\" and \"the system's capabilities/limitations as required by Recital 71.\" \n\nThat said, from a prototyping standpoint, this is a well-scoped infrastructure project that can be built incrementally. A v0 could just auto-generate a structured changelog linked to compliance templates within weeks. The full vision of fully automated audit-ready reports is more ambitious but the path exists.\n\nThe risk I see: if the metadata tagging scheme is poorly designed, it becomes a bottleneck for prototyping speed (engineers hate adding extra annotations). But if done right with good defaults and minimal required tags, it's a net positive.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this - it's technically feasible, incrementally buildable, and addresses a real compliance need in a way that leverages existing infrastructure. The promise score should reflect that while the full vision is ambitious, the core approach is sound and prototype-able. I'd say around 72-78 - solid technical direction with clear incremental path, but the \"instantaneous\" and fully automated aspects have real complexity that could slow delivery.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 74.\n",
