data might cover part of it.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong, well-targeted proposal that directly addresses the core mandatory requirement of Article 12. From a compliance perspective, having a unified, automated logging infrastructure across all high-risk prototypes dramatically reduces the risk of non-compliance due to individual team oversight or inconsistent implementation. The integration with existing deployment pipeline is particularly valuable because it means compliance is embedded rather than optional.\n\nHowever, I note some gaps — it doesn't explicitly address all the specific data fields required for Annex III systems, and there's no mention of log retention policies, access controls on logs (integrity), or how \"lifetime\" storage will be managed. But these are refinements that can be added to the framework specification rather than dealbreakers.\n\nThis proposal clearly clears my bar. It addresses a mandatory legal requirement in a systemic, scalable way that protects institutional compliance posture. I'd approve it.\n\nPromise score: This is very strong for compliance. The unified approach is exactly what a compliance officer would want — no system can ship without proper logging because the infrastructure enforces it. I'd give this around 85-90. The gaps (Annex III specifics, retention, access control) are real but manageable within the implementation. I'll go with 87.\n",
