or resources; it's an enabling layer that protects the research function from administrative drag.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Explicitly builds on \"existing experiment tracking software\" (e.g., MLflow, W&B, Neptune, or similar). This is exactly the kind of leverage I want to see — not throwing money at a new system when we already track experiments.\n- The templating engine component is relatively straightforward engineering work — low technical risk, well-understood pattern.\n- It creates a single source of truth for compliance documentation derived from operational data already being captured during normal R&D workflows. No additional instrumentation burden on the research teams.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Real-time\" could be overpromising if interpreted as literally every parameter change triggers a doc update — but in practice, this means continuous/auto-refresh rather than manual quarterly updates, which is reasonable.\n- The scope mentions training parameters, dataset versions, validation metrics — these are the core technical documentation elements Recital 71 calls for (algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes). It's appropriately scoped to what the regulation actually requires.\n- One risk: if our experiment tracking software doesn't capture all necessary fields (e.g., risk-management system documentation), the pipeline will have gaps. But this is a manageable integration task, not a fundamental flaw.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a high-efficiency, low-risk, well-scoped proposal that directly leverages existing infrastructure to meet an ongoing regulatory obligation. It's exactly the kind of \"make do with what you have\" approach that maximizes resource efficiency. The cost is modest (integration + templating), the benefit is continuous and compounding (saves researcher hours indefinitely), and it addresses a genuine compliance requirement rather than being speculative R&D.\n\nDecision: Approve. This