or all scenarios could balloon scope significantly.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- As a CI gate, this catches regressions — good for maintaining quality over the system lifetime (which Article 12 explicitly requires: \"over the lifetime of the system\").\n- The \"final technical gate for viability\" framing is slightly overstated — tests verify that logging works under tested conditions, but they don't prove the logging design is sufficient for all future operational scenarios. They're necessary but not sufficient for full compliance validation.\n\n**From a prototyping speed perspective:**\n- This adds process overhead. Every change to logging infrastructure requires running this suite. In early prototyping, this could slow iteration if the test suite is heavy or brittle.\n- But it also prevents technical debt accumulation in logging — which would be expensive to retrofit later.\n- The CI integration means it runs automatically, so the marginal cost after initial build is low.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a sound engineering practice. Testing your compliance-critical infrastructure is not optional if you want technical viability. The main concerns are:\n1. Scope creep — \"adversarial inputs and edge cases\" could become unbounded.\n2. Dependency on having something to test against (logging implementation).\n3. It's a verification tool, not a solution to the record-keeping problem itself.\n\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality), this is a legitimate and useful engineering artifact. It doesn't solve the core logging architecture question, but it ensures that once you build the logging system, you can verify it meets requirements. The CI integration makes it sustainable over the system's lifetime.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this — it's good engineering practice for a compliance-critical component. The promise score should reflect that it's useful but not the highest-impact investment (it's a verification tool, dependent on prior work). Maybe around 65-72. It's solid, practical, well-scoped in concept, though execution details matter.\n\nLet me settle: I'll approve at around 68. It's good practice, technically straightforward to implement, adds real value as a quality gate, but it's a supporting tool rather than a core architecture decision, and its usefulness is gated on having the logging system in place first.\n",
