ication systems (Annex III point 1(a)). The proposal seems to generalize this to all high-risk projects in CI/CD, which may over-engineer for non-biometric high-risk systems. However, having a flexible framework that can handle different logging requirements is reasonable.\n3. \"Modular\" is claimed but the architecture isn't detailed enough - how does modularity work? Plugin-based? Config-driven? This matters for prototyping speed because if each new model type requires custom middleware development, you haven't actually solved the problem.\n4. Integration into inference engine - this could introduce performance overhead. For high-throughput systems, every logged event adds latency. Need to consider async logging, batch writes, etc.\n5. The proposal is fairly high-level - it describes WHAT rather than HOW in detail. As a prototyping concern, I need to know: what's the actual codebase? What inference frameworks are we targeting (TensorRT, ONNX Runtime, PyTorch serve)? The middleware approach works differently for each.\n\n**From my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\n- The core idea is technically sound and well-aligned with Article 12 requirements\n- The modular library approach directly addresses the prototyping speed concern - researchers don't need to re-implement logging for every experiment\n- CI/CD integration ensures quality consistency\n- The middleware pattern is a proven, viable architectural choice\n- Main risk: \"tamper-proof ledger\" without specification could lead to over-engineering or under-engineering in implementation\n- The proposal is at the right abstraction level for an internal lab - detailed enough to be actionable but flexible enough to handle different model types\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's technically viable, addresses a real regulatory need, and importantly from MY perspective, it removes manual logging work from the prototyping loop which directly improves speed. The architecture is sound in 