on milestones in an R&D pipeline are a classic friction point for rapid prototyping. Requiring researchers to log data sources and validation processes \"in a central repository\" at mandatory milestones will slow down iteration cycles.\n- In early-stage prototyping, you're often iterating rapidly on hypotheses. Forcing documentation at each milestone adds overhead that can stifle exploration.\n- The proposal doesn't differentiate between the exploratory phase (where rapid iteration matters) and the production/deployment phase (where documentation is critical). A one-size-fits-all \"mandatory milestones\" approach will hurt prototyping velocity.\n- From a technical viability standpoint, a template system is low-risk to implement (it's just docs), so it won't technically fail. But it also doesn't solve the core engineering problem of automatic event logging in AI systems.\n\n**What this proposal actually does vs. what Article 12 requires:**\n- Article 12(1): Technical capability for automatic logging → This proposal is a governance policy, not a technical implementation. It doesn't address this.\n- Article 12(2): Logging capabilities for traceability of functioning → Again, this is about the system's technical logging capabilities, not human documentation.\n- Article 12(3): Specific minimum logging for Annex III point 1(a) systems → Technical requirements again.\n\nThe proposal addresses the \"documentation\" aspect mentioned in Recital 71 (technical documentation kept up to date), which is relevant but is a different obligation than Article 12's technical logging requirement. The recital does mention keeping records and technical documentation, so there IS value here — but from my engineering vantage point, this is a governance/administrative solution that doesn't address the core technical challenge of building automatic event-logging capabilities into AI systems.\n\n**My assessment:**\n- From a pure technical viability standpoint: This is technically viable (it's 