projects.\" This could be over-engineering if many of our current or near-term projects are not high-risk AI systems subject to the Act. Building infrastructure for a use case that may only apply to a subset of projects could be inefficient.\n- \"Base image of all lab environments\" — this is a strong commitment. If the framework needs updates (and it will, as regulations evolve and systems change), maintaining a base-image dependency across all environments creates operational coupling and potential update bottlenecks.\n- The proposal says engineers will \"develop a middleware layer that captures database references and natural person identifiers automatically.\" This is technically non-trivial — different AI systems interact with data in very different ways. A truly generic middleware that auto-captures the right things for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.) without significant per-system configuration may be unrealistic or require heavy per-project configuration anyway.\n- The \"reducing redundant engineering effort\" argument is strong but assumes redundancy exists and will continue to. If we only have a handful of high-risk projects, the unified framework's fixed cost might exceed the sum of individual implementations.\n\nHowever, I need to weigh this carefully. The resource-allocation efficiency angle actually cuts both ways:\n- IF we have multiple high-risk AI projects (or expect to), a shared logging layer is clearly more efficient than N separate implementations.\n- The \"base image\" integration means ongoing maintenance burden but also means new projects get compliance for free.\n- The middleware approach, if well-scoped, reduces per-project engineering time significantly.\n\nBut the risk is: this is a platform investment that requires sustained engineering resources to build AND maintain. From a Lab Director perspective worried about funding constraints and scientific ambition, committing engineers to build and maintain an internal infrastructure platform (rather than doing science) is a real opportunity cost. However, if it's done well once and serves many projects, the ROI is clear.\n\nThe proposal doesn't mention:\n- Phased rollout or scoping to actual current high-risk systems\n- Maintenance plan post-build\n- Fallback for when the middleware can't capture system-specific events\n- How it handles the \"lifetime\" requirement (Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime of the system\")\n\nI think this is a solid proposal with clear efficiency benefits, but it's somewhat ambitious in scope and could be over-engineered. From my vantage point, I'd lean toward approving it because:\n1. The core insight (one shared logging layer vs. N separate ones) is sound resource allocation.\n2. It directly addresses Article 12(1)-(3).\n3. Reduces per-project engineering overhead going forward.\n4. Integration into base images means low marginal cost for new projects.\n\nThe main risk is over-investment if the actual number of high-risk systems is small, but even then, having a standardized approach prevents future compliance gaps and rework.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderately high promise score. It's well-aligned with resource efficiency goals, though I'd want to see scoping before full build-out. Maybe 72-78 range. The proposal is strong in concept but the \"all lab environments\" scope and the assumption of universal auto-capture give me some pause about whether the engineering investment will match actual usage patterns.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 74.\n",
