nstead of \"build → manual review by compliance team → fix → rebuild,\" it becomes \"build → run tests → fix\" within the dev cycle\n  - Catches regressions when the system evolves (substantial modifications, model updates, etc.)\n- Quality benefit: automated, repeatable validation is more reliable than manual spot-checks. You can run the full suite on every build.\n- The \"before deployment\" aspect means it gates releases, which is a standard DevOps practice already.\n\n**Potential Concerns:**\n- \"Simulates user interactions\" — for some systems this could be complex (e.g., real-time streaming, multi-agent interactions). But the core concept still holds; you adapt the simulation layer per system type.\n- Scope creep risk: making it cover ALL of Article 12 across ALL high-risk system categories in one \"suite\" might be ambitious. But even a partial implementation targeting the most common logging patterns is valuable.\n- The proposal says \"a suite\" which implies breadth, but technically you could start with a narrow scope (e.g., just Annex III point 1(a) systems) and expand.\n\n**Overall from my vantage:**\nThis is a solid, technically viable engineering project. It maps cleanly to existing practices (integration testing, contract testing, CI gates). It directly serves speed of prototyping by catching logging bugs early rather than in manual review. It's not a moonshot — the technical risk is low-to-moderate. The ROI for an R&D lab building multiple high-risk AI systems is clear: every new system you build gets its logging compliance validated automatically.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should reflect that it's technically straightforward, directly serves my domain (speed/quality of prototyping), and has clear near-term value. It's not a 100 because there are some scoping complexities (generalizing across system types, keeping the simulation layer in sync with evolving systems) but it's very strong. I'd put it around 78-82.\n\nLet me settle on 80.