s is manageable — you can phase in the gates gradually.\n- The \"fails the build\" aspect is a double-edged sword for rapid prototyping. During early exploration, strict gates on logging fields that are still being designed could block progress. But the proposal says \"before it can be deployed,\" which suggests it's a deployment gate rather than a commit gate — that's more appropriate for prototyping speed.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- This is a high-leverage investment: once built, it runs on every build with zero marginal cost, ensuring compliance isn't an afterthought.\n- It reduces the \"compliance tax\" that typically hits later in development when you realize you need to retrofit logging.\n- The approach of validating against specific fields (start times, user IDs) maps directly to Article 12(3)(a)-(d), making it concrete and testable.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Maintaining the pipeline itself as requirements evolve or systems change. But this is standard CI/CD maintenance burden.\n- The \"simulate system usage\" part could be complex for AI systems where inputs are varied. You'd need representative test scenarios, not just unit-test-level simulations. This adds some engineering effort but is still very doable.\n- For the Annex III point 1(a) specific requirements (biometrics-related), the logging needs are more specific — this pipeline would need to be parameterized per system type.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage:**\nThis is technically very viable, well-scoped, and directly serves my function's concerns. It turns a regulatory requirement into an engineering artifact that can be built iteratively, tested in isolation, and integrated without disrupting the prototyping workflow (since it gates deployment, not development). It improves speed by catching issues early and improves quality by making compliance a continuous property rather than a one-time audit. This is exactly the kind of infrastructure investment I'd want to back — it's bounded in scope, uses well-known patterns, and has clear success criteria.\n\nI'd rate this highly. Maybe 82-85. It's not a 100 because:\n- The \"simulate system usage\" part for AI systems is non-trivial and could balloon in complexity\n- There's some risk of over-engineering if the test matrix becomes too broad\n- Maintenance burden as log schemas evolve\n\nBut it's clearly above my approval bar. Technically sound, well-motivated, directly serves prototyping speed/quality by shifting compliance left. I'd approve this without much hesitation.\n\nLet me settle on 84 — strong technical fit, well-bounded scope, clear engineering patterns to leverage, but the AI-system-specific test simulation adds some complexity that keeps it from a perfect score.\n",
