        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P1629) as a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, specifically Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n\nLet me analyze this proposal from my professional vantage point: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\n**Proposal P1629: Unified Compliance Logging Framework**\n\nThe proposal describes:\n- A standardized middleware layer that automatically captures event timestamps, input hashes, and system states for all high-risk prototypes\n- Ensures every execution path leaves a traceable audit trail without requiring manual code updates from developers\n- An internal Python library integrated into CI/CD pipeline to standardize log formats across different model architectures\n- Hooks into common API entry points to automate data capture\n- Removes manual burden from researchers and ensures baseline compliance without slowing down prototyping speed\n\n**Compliance Assessment:**\n\nArticle 12(1) states: \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\"\n\nThis is a mandatory technical requirement. The system MUST have automatic logging capability. This proposal directly addresses this by building automated capture infrastructure.\n\nArticle 12(2) requires logging to enable traceability appropriate to intended purpose, covering:\n(a) identifying risk situations and substantial modifications\n(b) facilitating post-market monitoring (Art 72)\n(c) monitoring operation (Art 26(5))\n\nThe proposal's \"event timestamps, input hashes, and system states\" would partially address these but the description is somewhat generic. It mentions \"traceable audit trail\" which aligns with Recital 71's emphasis on traceability.\n\nArticle 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification/verification/categorization) has specific minimum requirements:\n(a)