- Doesn't explicitly mention retention period requirements or how logs are stored/secured (though these might be covered elsewhere)\n- \"Common API entry points\" - what about systems that don't go through standard APIs? Edge cases need handling\n- The proposal is a framework/infrastructure decision - it's enabling, not a complete solution. But as a compliance matter, having the infrastructure in place is prerequisite to compliance\n\nFrom institutional survival perspective: This is critical. Without automated logging, we simply cannot comply with Art 12(1). If we rely on developers manually adding logging, we will have gaps, and during an audit or incident investigation, missing logs = non-compliance finding = potential penalties up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for the most serious violations.\n\nFrom regulatory compliance perspective: This is a foundational requirement. Building it into CI/CD means every code change is checked against logging requirements before deployment. Standardization means we can present a coherent story to regulators.\n\nFrom market reputation perspective: Having a mature, automated compliance infrastructure demonstrates organizational maturity to partners, clients, and regulators. It shows we take the AI Act seriously. Conversely, being caught without proper logging would be embarrassing and trust-destroying.\n\nThe proposal is well-scoped as an internal framework that enables compliance without being overly prescriptive about specific system requirements. It's the right level of abstraction for a middleware layer. The \"baseline compliance\" framing is appropriate - it sets up the foundation while acknowledging that individual systems may need additional logging specific to their intended purpose (Art 12(2)).\n\nMy assessment: This is strongly aligned with my function's mandate. It addresses a mandatory technical requirement in an automated, scalable way. The CI/CD integration is smart from a compliance culture perspective - it makes compliance the default rather than something that requires vigilance. I would approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is high-value infrastructure for compliance. It directly implements Art 12(1). The main gap is specificity around Annex III 1(a) requirements and retention/storage, but as a framework layer, those can be configured at the application level. I'd give it an 85-90. Let me settle on 87 - very strong fit, with minor gaps in the description regarding specific data fields required for certain high-risk categories and log retention policies.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal is quite well-aligned. The key compliance concern I have is that \"input hashes\" might not satisfy Art 12(3)(c) which requires recording the actual input data. But as a framework, it could be configured to capture full data or hashes depending on the system type. The fact that it's a configurable framework is actually an advantage. I'll go with 86.\n",
