on) is non-trivial. The scope could balloon.\n2. Article 12 specifically requires AUTOMATIC LOGGING OF EVENTS over the lifetime of the system — this proposal focuses on documentation generation from repos but doesn't clearly address the runtime logging capability (Article 12(1)-(3)). It seems to be more about the technical documentation aspect than the operational logging. However, Recital 71 does encompass both, and the proposal mentions \"experiment tracking tools\" which could partially cover this.\n3. The risk is that automated extraction from code repos produces documentation that doesn't meet the \"clear and comprehensive form\" requirement — regulators may need human-curated narratives about design choices, limitations, etc., not just hyperparameter dumps.\n4. \"Formatted regulatory reports\" — who defines the format? If the European Commission issues specific templates or standards (harmonized standards under Article 40), this tool needs to be maintainable against changing formats.\n5. The proposal conflates two things: static technical documentation (which is what it primarily addresses) and dynamic event logging (Article 12's core requirement). From a resource allocation standpoint, I need to know if this is scoped correctly or if it's trying to do too much/too little.\n\n**Resource efficiency assessment:**\n- Uses existing infrastructure (good — low capital expenditure)\n- Automates recurring work (good — reduces ongoing labor costs)\n- One-time build cost with high reuse value across multiple systems/projects (good ROI)\n- Risk of scope creep if \"formatted regulatory reports\" keeps changing (moderate concern)\n- Doesn't require new headcount for documentation (saves on personnel costs)\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding:**\n- The framing explicitly protects research velocity (\"without slowing down scientific iteration\")\n- It converts a compliance burden into an automated background process, freeing researcher time for actual science\n- This is well-aligned with my mandate\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Directly builds on version control, experiment tracking (e.g., MLflow, W&B), code repos — all things the lab already has\n- Low new procurement needs\n- Integrates into existing workflows rather than creating parallel processes\n\nOverall: This is a well-scoped proposal that addresses a genuine regulatory requirement using existing infrastructure, with clear resource-efficiency benefits. The main risk is scope ambiguity (does it cover runtime logging or just static documentation?) and the assumption that automated extraction from repos is sufficient for \"clear and comprehensive\" regulatory documentation. But as a first-phase toolchain investment, it's highly cost-effective and leverages what we already have. I'd approve this — it's one of the most resource-efficient approaches to compliance I've seen, building on existing infrastructure rather than requiring new systems or headcount.\n\nI'll give it a high promise score but not 100 because of the scope ambiguity around runtime logging vs. static documentation, and the risk that \"automated\" documentation from repos may need significant human curation for regulatory acceptability.\n",
