grity without necessarily requiring expensive blockchain-style infrastructure (could be cryptographic hashing chains).\n- It creates a baseline that all projects inherit automatically — this is efficient resource allocation because compliance becomes a property of the platform rather than an individual project task.\n\nCons / Risks:\n- Building a middleware layer that integrates with \"all lab projects\" is itself a significant engineering investment. The question is whether we can build this efficiently given our existing infrastructure, or whether it requires substantial new development.\n- \"Across all lab projects\" — if many of those aren't high-risk, we might be over-engineering the solution. However, the proposal says \"for any high-risk model we develop,\" so it's scoped correctly.\n- The tamper-evident ledger adds complexity and potentially cost. Is this strictly necessary under Article 12? The article requires logging capabilities but doesn't explicitly mandate tamper-evidence at the storage layer (though traceability implies some integrity guarantee). This might be scope creep beyond what's minimally required, consuming resources that could go elsewhere.\n- \"Without manual developer intervention\" is ambitious. In practice, ensuring the middleware correctly captures all relevant events across diverse model architectures and use cases will likely require some per-project configuration or integration work. The promise of zero overhead may not fully materialize.\n- Building a unified telemetry layer for an R&D institution with diverse projects could be complex — different models, different training frameworks, different inference stacks. The middleware needs to handle heterogeneity.\n\nHowever, the core idea is sound from a resource-allocation perspective: one shared infrastructure investment that scales across projects and reduces per-project compliance burden. This is exactly what a Lab Director should prioritize — building the platform once so every proj