sistency across projects**: For an R&D institution with diverse projects, uniform compliance reduces the risk of gaps. Regulators will look at the institution holistically; inconsistent logging practices create audit vulnerabilities.\n6. **Reduces researcher burden**: This is important for adoption. If researchers find it too burdensome, they may work around it or implement it poorly. A clean API integration means higher actual compliance rates.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n1. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention Article 12(3)(d) — \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results.\" This is a gap, though perhaps it's assumed to be covered by \"input matches\" or the modular design allows for extension.\n2. It mentions \"timestamps, reference databases, and input matches\" but doesn't explicitly call out the post-market monitoring hooks (Article 72) or the risk identification triggers (Article 79(1)). Though these might be implied in \"events relevant for\" traceability.\n3. The proposal is about a software library — it's an implementation vehicle, not a policy document. As compliance officer, I need to ensure the library actually captures what the law requires. But that's more of an engineering QA concern than a rejection reason.\n4. It doesn't address log retention duration or security of logs (though Article 12 itself focuses on recording capability; retention might be covered elsewhere in the Act).\n\nOverall assessment: This is strongly aligned with my function. A unified, mandatory logging framework directly serves regulatory compliance (Article 12), protects institutional survival (no gaps in any project's compliance), and supports market reputation (demonstrates systematic compliance posture to regulators and partners). The fact that it's a technical implementation rather than just a policy memo makes it actionable and enforceable.\n\nI would approve this with high confidence. It's one of the most directly relevant propos