        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2629\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12 mandates structured event logging (timestamps, reference databases, input data, verifier IDs) — all achievable with well-understood append-only log stores and metadata schemas on a weeks-to-months prototyping timeline. This proposal instead commits scarce implementation capacity to solving an open research problem (real-time causal tracing of internal activation patterns mapped to regulatory entries), which has no settled engineering solution, carries high latency risk under inference constraints, and offers no clear prototype milestone I can commit to delivering at quality within a reasonable window; the core compliance obligation is fully satisfiable without it.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 12\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2629 is about \"Semantic Logging for Model Interpretability\" - evolving simple event logs into semantic traces that explain WHY a specific high-risk decision was made, using causal tracing mechanisms that map internal activation patterns to regulatory log entries in real time during inference.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n1. \"Causal tracing mechanisms that map internal activation patterns to the required regulatory log entries in real time during inference\" - This is extremely ambitious. Causal tracing of neural network activations is an active research area with significant unresolved challenges. Mapping internal activation patterns to interpretable causal explanations that align with regulatory requirements is not a solved problem, especially for complex models (large language models, transformer architectures).\n2. \"In real time during inference\" - This adds a major performance constraint. Causal tracing (like attention rollout, integrated gradients, etc.) typically requires additional forward passes or gradient computations, which add latency. Doing this in real-time for high-risk systems that may have strict latency requirements is technically very challenging.\n3. The scope is extremely broad: \"evolve simple event logs into semantic traces\" - this conflates several distinct technical problems (logging infrastructure, model interpretability, causal inference, regulatory mapping).\n4. This is framed as a \"research project\" - from my perspective, committing implementation resources to a research project with high uncertainty about whether the core mechanism will work at all is risky. The fundamental question of whether we can reliably produce causal explanations for arbitrary neural network decisions in real-time is not settled science.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Concerns:**\n1. This is positioned as a research project, which means the timeline to a working prototype is highly uncertain. We could be 6-24 months from having anything that works, and even then it may only work for narrow cases.\n2. The scope is so broad that prototyping would require making choices across multiple unsolved sub-problems simultaneously.\n3. Article 12 actually requires much simpler things: automatic recording of events (logs), periods of use, reference databases, input data, identification of persons. A simple structured logging system with timestamps and metadata could satisfy the core regulatory requirements without any interpretability research.\n4. The \"semantic trace\" and \"causal tracing\" components go far beyond what Article 12 requires. The regulation asks for logs that enable traceability and monitoring - it doesn't require explaining the internal mechanism of why a model made a decision in terms of activation patterns.\n\n**What Article 12 actually requires:**\n- Automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime\n- Recording of situations presenting risk or substantial modification\n- Facilitating post-market monitoring\n- Monitoring operation\n- For Annex III point 1(a): start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of verification persons\n\nThese are all achievable with standard engineering: structured logging, timestamped event stores, metadata tracking. None of these require causal tracing or activation pattern mapping.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a technical viability standpoint, this proposal commits resources to solve an open research problem (real-time causal interpretability) as the vehicle for meeting what is essentially a straightforward logging requirement. The core regulatory obligation can be met with well-understood engineering patterns (structured event logs, append-only stores, metadata schemas). The \"causal tracing\" and \"semantic traces\" layer adds enormous technical risk without being required by the regulation.\n\nFrom a speed/quality of prototyping standpoint: I cannot prototype this quickly because the core mechanism is unsolved research. I cannot guarantee quality because there's no clear specification for what \"a causal explanation that maps to regulatory log entries\" looks like in a general, reliable way. The risk of spending 12+ months and having something that only works for narrow cases or doesn't meet latency requirements is high.\n\nThis is not a case where I'd approve it. It's technically very risky, goes well beyond what's required, and the prototyping timeline/quality are highly uncertain. However, I should acknowledge there IS value in thinking about richer logging - but this specific proposal as framed (causal tracing of activation patterns in real-time) is not something I can commit implementation resources to with confidence in technical viability or delivery speed.\n\nI'll give it a low promise score. Maybe 12-15. There's a kernel of an interesting idea (richer logs that include some reasoning context), but the specific mechanism proposed (real-time causal tracing of activation patterns) is not technically viable for production deployment on reasonable timescales, and isn't required by Article 12.\n\nLet me settle on my decision: REJECT. The technical risk is too high, the prototyping timeline is too uncertain, and the core regulatory requirement doesn't demand this level of interpretability infrastructure.\n",
