icle 12(2) requires logging capabilities that enable recording of specific event types (risks, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring). The proposal doesn't address these specific logging requirements.\n- Article 12(3) for biometric systems has very specific minimum logging requirements — not addressed here.\n- \"Automatically generates comprehensive technical documentation\" — this is ambitious and potentially risky. If the auto-generated docs are incomplete or inaccurate, that's a compliance failure worse than manual documentation (you'd be relying on an AI to produce your compliance documents).\n- The proposal says it will \"synthesize the requirements of Recital 71 into machine-readable reports\" — but Recital 71 is interpretive guidance; Article 12 is the binding text. Focusing on recitals over articles could lead to gaps in actual legal compliance.\n- No mention of data retention periods, access controls for logs (privacy concerns), or what happens when a log needs to be preserved as evidence in a regulatory investigation.\n- \"Living document that updates as models evolve\" — from a compliance standpoint, you actually need IMMUTABLE records at each point in time. A living document that overwrites could destroy evidence of past states. You need version snapshots, not just the latest state.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:** If we commit resources to building this pipeline, does it protect us? Partially. It addresses the technical documentation aspect well but leaves a gap on actual event logging (the core of Article 12). However, it's a foundational component that would be needed regardless. The question is whether approving this commits us in a way that creates false confidence about our overall Article 12 compliance while the harder logging requirements go unaddressed.\n\n**Market reputation:** Having a robust, version-controlled documentation pipeline is genuinely impressive to regulators and market partners. It signals maturity and seriousness. MLf