ance faster or slower?\n- From the funding constraints angle: developing custom semantic compression for logs is a significant R&D investment. Is the ROI justified when off-the-shelf structured logging solutions with tiered storage (hot/warm/cold) might solve 90% of the problem at 10% of the cost?\n\n**My assessment from resource-allocation efficiency perspective:**\nThe core tension: this proposal uses scarce R&D resources to explore a novel approach to a problem that may be solvable with existing, well-understood infrastructure (structured logging, time-series databases, tiered storage, data lifecycle management). The regulatory requirement is for specific, discrete event records — not for \"summaries\" of events. The risk of non-compliance from using compressed/summarized logs in place of actual event-level records is high.\n\nHowever, there IS a legitimate efficiency question about managing log volume over system lifetimes (which can be 10+ years). But the proposal as framed is a research exploration, not an implementation plan. It doesn't address the immediate compliance need. And its proposed solution (semantic compression replacing raw data) may conflict with the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3) which mandate very concrete data points (start/end times, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of natural persons).\n\nI think I should reject this. The proposal:\n1. Addresses a real problem (log storage over time) but proposes a solution that risks non-compliance with the specific requirements of Art 12(3).\n2. Is framed as R&D exploration rather than implementation — it commits scarce resources to \"explore\" without clear path to compliance.\n3. The efficiency gains are speculative; standard tiered storage and structured logging likely handle most cases adequately.\n4. Replacing raw data with summaries creates an unacceptable compliance risk for a high-risk AI system subject to regulatory scrutiny.\n\nPromise score: I'd give th