essment from my vantage point:**\nThis proposal aligns well with resource-allocation efficiency (leverages existing Git infrastructure, avoids duplicate work), respects funding constraints (low capital cost, mainly process change), and makes effective use of existing R&D infrastructure (version control is already there). It's a practical, proportionate response to Recital 71 that doesn't require massive new investment. The main risk is workflow friction from mandatory templates, but this is manageable and far cheaper than non-compliance.\n\nI'd approve this. It's efficient, uses existing infrastructure, and addresses the regulatory requirement without demanding disproportionate resources.\n\nPromise score: From my vantage point, this is a strong fit. It's not a moonshot — it's practical infrastructure that we largely already have, plus process discipline. I'd rate it around 78-82. Let me settle on 80. It's very well-aligned with efficiency and existing infrastructure use, but the \"mandatory templates\" element introduces some workflow overhead that could be a drag if not carefully designed, and there's an ongoing governance cost to maintain template quality and compliance.\n\nLet me write my response now.\n",
