 The requirement says \"identification of the natural persons\" - a hashed identifier linked to an internal HR database with access controls would likely satisfy both the AI Act (traceability) and GDPR (data minimization) without needing ZKPs. The proposal jumps to the most complex solution before establishing that simpler approaches don't work.\n- This is an \"investigation into using\" language - it's exploratory, not implementation. From a resource-allocation standpoint, committing scarce R&D resources to investigate cryptographic methods for what is essentially a logging metadata question feels misallocated. Our infrastructure engineers could probably handle this with existing tooling (encrypted fields, access-controlled databases) without needing ZKP research.\n- The \"validation against GDPR data minimization\" step is primarily a legal task, not an R&D task. We'd be spending engineering time to produce a deliverable that our DPO or external privacy counsel would need to interpret anyway.\n- From effective use of existing R&D infrastructure: do we have ZKP expertise in-house? If not, this becomes either hiring (expensive) or outsourcing (defeats the purpose). If we DO have it, is that team's time better spent on our core AI research where it differentiates us?\n- The scope is somewhat unclear - \"prototype an authentication layer\" is a significant engineering effort for what might be a minor compliance metadata field.\n\nHowever, I should note: the proposal does identify a real tension and a real requirement. The question is whether this is best solved by R&D investment or by legal/compliance process with standard engineering support. From my vantage point of resource efficiency, I lean toward rejecting because:\n1. The solution space is likely well-trodden (hashed IDs, tokenized references) and doesn't require novel ZKP research\n2. It's an \"investigation\" - exploratory spend without a clear deliverable tied to our core mission\n3. The regulatory interpretation risk means we might build the wrong thing\n4. Our existing infrastructure (standard encrypted logging, access controls) likely already handles most of this\n\nI'll reject it but give it a moderate promise score since the underlying problem is real and there's some value in having a thoughtful approach to the AI Act/GDPR tension - just not at R&D resource-commitment level. Maybe around 35-40. It identifies a real issue but proposes an over-engineered, exploratory solution that isn't well-matched to our resource constraints or existing capabilities.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise score 38.\n",
