ally\n- It's a meta-proposal about money allocation, not about engineering solutions\n- The \"Compliance Engineering line item\" is vague - it doesn't specify what technical work would be funded\n- Pursuing EU grants is slow and uncertain; it doesn't guarantee funding when you need it\n\n4. Assessment: This proposal is fundamentally outside my professional domain of concern. I care about whether a technical solution will work, whether we can prototype it quickly, whether the architecture is sound. A budget restructuring proposal tells me nothing about technical viability or prototyping speed/quality. It's an administrative decision that might indirectly affect whether engineers have time to do compliance work, but it doesn't constitute a technical proposal at all.\n\nHowever, I should consider: does having dedicated funding for compliance engineering make the technical implementation more viable? In a sense, yes - if engineers are pulled off R&D to handle logging requirements, both suffer. Dedicated resources could help. But this is extremely indirect and the proposal itself doesn't contain any technical content that I can assess for viability or prototyping potential.\n\nI think from my vantage point, this is largely irrelevant - it's a financial/administrative proposal, not a technical one. It doesn't address how logging will be implemented technically, what the architecture looks like, whether the approach to automatic event recording is feasible, etc. I would reject it as outside my domain of professional concern for technical screening. It's not that it's bad - it might be a sensible administrative measure - but from the perspective of \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping,\" this proposal contains no technical content to evaluate.\n\nMy decision: reject (it's not a technical proposal, it doesn't address implementation viability or prototyping)\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe 15-20. It acknowledges that compliance work needs resources (which is true and indirectly supports technical implementation), but it contains zero technical substance from my perspective. It's not harmful, just irrelevant to my domain.\n\nLet me settle on a promise of around 15 - it has a kernel of relevance (ensuring engineers have dedicated time/resources for compliance work does affect whether we can actually implement the logging technically) but it's so far removed from actual technical content that I can barely assess it.\n",
