vs custom C++)? The generality claim needs to be realistic.\n- \"System state changes by default\" for AI systems can be very complex - model weights changing, data drift, feature distributions shifting. Capturing this meaningfully is non-trivial and may not fit neatly into a generic middleware.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe core idea is technically sound and well-motivated. A shared logging library reduces per-project effort and ensures consistency. The main risks are: (1) the upfront build cost, (2) generality across very different system types, and (3) whether \"default\" capture actually covers what Article 12(3) requires for specific categories or if it's just a partial solution.\n\nBut from a pure prototyping speed/quality angle, having this in place IS a net positive. It removes a recurring task from every project. The technical approach (middleware, standardized API, base template integration) is conventional and feasible. I'd say this clears the bar for approval - it's technically viable, it improves prototyping speed by removing repetitive work, and it improves quality through consistency.\n\nMy promise score: This is solid but not revolutionary. It's a well-understood pattern applied to a specific regulatory need. The main uncertainty is scope (how much can be truly unified vs. how much needs per-project customization) and the upfront cost. I'd put this around 72-78. Let me settle on 74.\n",
