e tax on each one.\n- The scope is well-bounded: \"production-bound systems\" only, not every experimental model. This shows awareness of where the regulatory obligation actually falls (Article 12 applies to high-risk AI systems, i.e., deployed ones).\n- Using MLflow (open source) rather than a commercial SaaS reduces ongoing license costs.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- MLflow is likely already in or near the lab's MLOps stack. The incremental cost is the \"immutable backend\" layer, not a full platform rebuild.\n- It integrates with existing workflows rather than imposing a new parallel process.\n- A centralized layer means one implementation serves all teams/projects, avoiding fragmented per-team compliance solutions (which would be far more expensive and harder to audit).\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Immutable backend\" — what does this actually cost operationally? Immutable storage has retention implications and can become a cost center over time. But compared to the alternative of manual documentation across dozens of teams, it's still efficient.\n- The proposal says it \"satisfies traceability requirements\" — I need to be careful here. Article 12 is specifically about *logging* (automatic recording of events), not just provenance/lineage tracking. Provenance tracking (dataset → model version → hyperparameters) is more aligned with the technical documentation requirements in Article 11 and Recital 71, while Article 12(2)-(3) requires specific event logging (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, verification personnel). So this proposal addresses part of the compliance picture but may not fully cover Article 12's logging obligations.\n- However, from a resource-allocation standpoint, even if it doesn't cover 100% of Article 12, the provenance/lineage layer is a prerequisite infrastructure that makes the full logging system tractable. Without knowing which model version ran which inference, you can't a