ing wrapper around model APIs IS efficient use of engineering resources if scoped correctly. The problem is the over-scope and the unrealistic auto-documentation claim.\n\nFrom a resource-allocation perspective: I would want to approve the narrow version (log timestamps, DB versions, user IDs, IO pairs via a lightweight API wrapper) but reject the broad \"generate technical documentation\" and \"immutable ledger\" components as scope creep that will consume disproportionate resources for marginal compliance gain. The proposal as written bundles too much and overpromises on the documentation generation part.\n\nGiven that I must judge it strictly on its own merits as written: the proposal as stated is over-scoped, makes an unrealistic claim about auto-generating Recital 71 documentation from a logging wrapper, and proposes building new infrastructure without leveraging existing tools. This would consume significant engineering resources for a solution that doesn't fully deliver what it claims (the documentation part) while duplicating capabilities we likely already have (basic logging).\n\nI'm leaning toward reject with a moderate promise score - the core idea has merit but as written it's over-scoped and makes claims I can't back. Maybe 40-50? The idea of an API wrapper for automatic event capture is solid and efficient, but the full package as described would be a resource misallocation due to scope creep and the unrealistic documentation automation component.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, with promise around 42. The core logging wrapper idea is genuinely useful and resource-efficient IF scoped properly, but as written it bundles in over-ambitious components (auto-doc generation, immutable ledger) that would consume disproportionate resources relative to compliance benefit, and fails to leverage existing infrastructure.\n",
