t among many. Over-investing here at the expense of actual scientific development could be misallocation.\n- Building a new protocol specifically for documentation sync, even integrated into VCS, still represents a non-trivial engineering investment. For an R&D lab with funding constraints, the question is whether this can be done incrementally using existing tooling (e.g., CI/CD pipeline steps that auto-generate docs) rather than as a \"new protocol.\"\n- The proposal says \"real-time\" which suggests continuous monitoring and generation overhead. A batch approach (sync at release milestones or PR merge) might achieve the same compliance outcome with less resource expenditure.\n\n**From my vantage point:**\n- Resource allocation: Using existing VCS infrastructure is good, but \"real-time\" adds cost. Could be achieved more cheaply.\n- Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints: The proposal diverts engineering resources to documentation tooling. While necessary for compliance, the \"real-time\" framing suggests over-engineering relative to what's needed.\n- Existing R&D infrastructure: Leveraging VCS is smart and efficient. This is a point in favor.\n\nOverall assessment: The core idea (integrating doc generation into existing VCS/CI pipelines) is sound and resource-efficient because it reuses existing infrastructure. However, the \"real-time\" framing and the creation of a \"new protocol\" suggest more investment than strictly necessary. The regulation requires documentation to be kept up-to-date appropriately — not necessarily in real-time. A more measured approach (e.g., automated doc generation at merge time or release) would achieve compliance with less resource commitment.\n\nI think this is borderline. The core concept of integrating into VCS is good and efficient. But the \"real-time\" ambition and \"new protocol\" framing suggest it might over-commit resources relative to what's actually needed. From a pure resource-efficiency standpoint, I'd lean toward 