        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1793\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises Recital 71's requirement that technical documentation be kept up to date throughout the system lifetime by automating version-controlled linkage between model artefacts, training data, validation reports, and risk assessments; from a compliance standpoint, this eliminates the most common audit-failure vector (stale or inconsistent documentation) and creates an inherent change-history trail that satisfies traceability expectations. It is well-scoped to the documentation-maintenance obligation and uses standard MLOps infrastructure (Git + model registry), which increases implementation reliability without requiring bespoke tooling.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 88\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1793 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, specifically Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is about a \"Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline\" - an automated system for maintaining up-to-date technical documentation as required by Recital 71. It involves version-controlled documentation synced with Git repositories and model registries, automatically linking model versions to training data, validation reports, and risk assessments.\n\nLet me assess this from a compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses Recital 71's requirement that \"technical documentation should be kept up to date appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system\"\n- Automating documentation reduces the risk of gaps or errors in records - critical for audit readiness\n- Links model versions to training data, validation reports, and risk assessments - this is exactly what Recital 71 calls for: \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system\"\n- \"Always audit-ready\" is a key compliance concern - regulatory auditors (national market surveillance authorities) can request documentation at any time\n- Version control provides traceability, which is explicitly mentioned in Recital 71: \"enable traceability of those systems\"\n- Reduces human error in documentation, which is a common source of non-compliance findings\n\n**Considerations/Concerns:**\n- The proposal focuses on technical documentation (Recital 71) rather than the logging requirements (Article 12(1)-(3)). However, Article 12 and Recital 71 are closely related - the recital is the purpose statement for Article 12, and both deal with record-keeping. The technical documentation pipeline supports the broader record-keeping obligation.\n- It doesn't specifically address the automatic logging of events (Article 12(1)) or the specific logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (Article 12(3)). But it's not claiming to be a complete solution - it's addressing the documentation maintenance aspect.\n- The \"syncs directly with Git repositories and model registries\" approach is practical and aligns with modern MLOps practices. This makes it more likely to actually work in practice rather than being a theoretical framework.\n- For an R&D institution, this is particularly important because models evolve rapidly - manual documentation would quickly become stale.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nThis proposal directly supports compliance with:\n- Recital 71 (up-to-date technical documentation)\n- Article 12(1) indirectly (by ensuring the documentation of logging capabilities is maintained)\n- Article 43 (technical documentation requirements for high-risk AI systems, which references similar content)\n- General traceability requirements\n\nFrom a compliance officer's perspective, this is a strong proposal. It addresses a specific regulatory requirement with a practical, implementable solution. The automation aspect reduces ongoing compliance burden and risk of non-compliance due to human error. Version control provides an audit trail of documentation changes itself.\n\nThe main limitation is that it doesn't cover the full scope of Article 12 (specifically the automatic event logging in 12(1) and the specific Annex III 1(a) requirements in 12(3)), but as a component of the overall compliance program, it's highly valuable and clearly within scope.\n\nI would approve this proposal. It's directly relevant to my domain, addresses a clear regulatory obligation, and proposes a practical implementation path. The promise score should be high - maybe 85-90. It's very strong but not perfect because it only covers the documentation maintenance aspect of Article 12/Recital 71, not the full logging requirements. But as a standalone proposal, it's highly relevant and well-scoped.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 88.\n",
