s formal processes to iterate quickly. If the training creates friction or adds bureaucratic overhead, it could actually slow down prototyping rather than speed it up.\n- \"Streamlined\" is doing a lot of work in that description. The actual implementation (designing effective checklists, getting buy-in from research teams who value autonomy) is non-trivial and the ROI is hard to measure.\n- However, the core insight - that designing with logging/traceability in mind during architecture decisions saves significant rework later - is technically sound and directly impacts my domain. A system architected without proper event emission points requires invasive refactoring later, which slows down prototyping cycles dramatically.\n\n**Net assessment:** The technical principle behind this is sound and relevant to my domain (designing for observability from the start speeds up development and reduces rework). However, as a \"training program\" it's more of an organizational/process intervention than a technical solution I can directly evaluate for viability. It doesn't produce deliverables in the traditional engineering sense. The risk is that it becomes a bureaucratic overhead that slows prototyping rather than enabling it. But the core idea - bake traceability into design phase - is something I would advocate for technically.\n\nI think this clears my bar at a moderate level. The principle is correct and would improve prototyping quality (systems designed with proper logging from the start are better-engineered), but the delivery mechanism (training/checklists) has execution risk that could undermine its benefits. I'll approve it but give a moderate promise score since it's not a direct technical solution and the ROI depends heavily on organizational adoption.\n\nLet me settle: I'd say approve with a promise around 55-62. The principle is right, it would help, but it's not a direct technical fix and has significant execution risk in an R&D culture that values speed over formality.\n",
