ology innovation.\n- \"Mapping experimental hyperparameters, dataset hashes, and validation results into the EU AI Act's prescribed reporting format\" — this is essentially an ETL/data pipeline project with a regulatory output template. The intellectual content is low; it's engineering plumbing.\n- The claim that this \"preserves intellectual merit by creating a permanent and searchable archive of research evolution\" is somewhat overreaching. Simply dumping hyperparameters and dataset hashes into a reporting format doesn't capture the *intellectual* reasoning, hypotheses tested, dead ends explored, or scientific insights gained. A true record of research evolution would include narrative reasoning, failed approaches with explanations, and analytical commentary — not just structured metadata.\n- The \"living record\" framing is somewhat marketing language for what is essentially automated report generation from existing data sources.\n- It doesn't advance the knowledge frontier in any meaningful way. It doesn't enable new experiments, new methodologies, or new scientific understanding. It organizes and formats existing information for regulatory purposes.\n- The broader scientific impact is minimal — this is a compliance tool specific to EU regulation, not something that would be adopted by the broader research community as a methodological advancement.\n\n**Verdict from my vantage point:**\nThis is primarily a regulatory compliance engineering project dressed up in language about \"intellectual merit\" and \"research evolution.\" While there's a thin kernel of legitimate research-value (keeping structured records of experiments), the proposal as stated doesn't meaningfully advance the knowledge frontier, doesn't contribute to broader scientific impact beyond one regulatory context, and the intellectual merit argument is somewhat superficial. It's useful infrastructure but not a research contribution in any meaningful sense.\n\nI would lean toward reject. The promise score should be low — maybe 15-25. There's a small sliver of value (structured experiment tracking) but it's wrapped in compliance language and doesn't genuinely advance scientific understanding. I'll say 20. It's not nothing, but from my vantage point, this is plumbing, not research advancement.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 18.\n",
