specific requirements (reference database, input data for matches, verification persons).\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Impact:**\n- This is a cross-cutting infrastructure project. It will require significant upfront engineering investment before any researcher sees benefit. The \"baseline compliance for every prototype\" framing means it becomes a blocking dependency — you can't ship a high-risk prototype without the telemetry layer being wired in.\n- However, the \"asynchronous\" design is key: if done right (fire-and-forget event emission from the model serving pipeline), the latency impact on prototyping iterations is minimal. A researcher's experiment loop shouldn't be slowed by logging.\n- The risk is scope creep: \"across all lab models\" could become a never-ending integration project as new architectures appear. The modularity claim helps, but in practice each new architecture needs adapter development.\n- For a prototyping team, the biggest value is that once built, every new prototype automatically gets logging without per-project effort. This reduces compliance overhead from O(n) to O(1) marginal cost per project.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- \"Immutable ledger\" adds operational complexity (no deletion for retention policy compliance, storage growth management). For a research lab that iterates quickly and discards experiments, this could become a data management burden.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level — it's an architecture vision rather than a concrete implementation plan. But as a \"proposal\" to commit resources toward building, the core idea (async event telemetry with structured fields into append-only storage) is technically sound and well-scoped enough to begin prototyping.\n- It doesn't address retention periods, data minimization, or how it interfaces with the post-market monitoring requirements specifically. But those are refinements, not blockers for initial implementation.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe core technical concept is sound and feasible. The async design protects prototyping speed. The \"baseline compliance\" framing is good — it sets a floor rather than trying to be a complete solution. The main risk is the \"across all lab models\" scope being too ambitious for initial delivery, but that's a project management concern rather than a technical viability concern. I can see this being built in a reasonable timeframe with a small team.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. It's technically viable, it directly addresses Article 12(1) and partially 12(2), and the async design means it won't slow down prototyping significantly once the initial infrastructure is in place. The promise score: I think around 72-78. It's a solid engineering project with clear value, but \"across all lab models\" and \"immutable ledger\" introduce operational risks that could slow things down. Not a 90+ because the scope is ambitious and the immutability requirement creates friction for a research environment.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 74.\n",
