        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen a single proposal (P2808) from my vantage point as the Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through that lens:\n\n**Proposal P2808: Unified High-Risk Logging Framework**\n- A standardized internal software library for automatic event recording across all high-risk AI projects\n- Creates consistent data format for logs meeting Article 12 requirements\n- Engineering team builds a modular API researchers must integrate into model pipelines\n- Captures timestamps, reference databases, and input matches automatically\n- Mandatory dependency for all high-risk deployments\n- Aims for uniform compliance while reducing manual burden on individual researchers\n\n**My vantage point assessment:**\n\nResource-allocation efficiency:\n- Building a single unified library is more resource-efficient than having each research team build their own logging solution. This avoids duplicated effort across projects.\n- However, it requires upfront engineering investment (building the modular API) before any project can deploy. This is a fixed cost that must be amortized across multiple high-risk projects to be efficient.\n- The \"mandatory dependency\" aspect could create bottlenecks if the library isn't ready or has bugs — all high-risk deployments are blocked.\n\nBalance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:\n- A shared infrastructure piece (the library) is a one-time investment that scales across projects. This is good for funding-constrained labs.\n- The \"modular API\" approach means researchers don't need to deeply understand compliance requirements — they just integrate the API. This lets them focus on science.\n- However, making it \"mandatory\" could add friction to research timelines. If a researcher wants to deploy a model quickly for a grant deadline or publication, having to integrate with this framework adds overhead.\n- The upfront cost of building and maintaining this library competes with direct scientific work in the budget.\n\nEffective use of existing R&D infrastructure:\n- A shared library leverages engineering resources centrally rather than duplicating them across teams. Good.\n- But it requires a dedicated team to maintain, update, and evolve the library as regulations change or new model architectures emerge. This is ongoing cost.\n- The \"modular\" aspect suggests flexibility for different pipeline types, which respects existing diverse infrastructure.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The proposal assumes all high-risk projects will need this — reasonable given Article 12(1) mandates automatic logging.\n2. Building a shared framework is resource-efficient IF there are enough high-risk projects to amortize the cost. If only 1-2 projects qualify, it's overkill vs. just building custom logging per project.\n3. The \"mandatory dependency\" could slow down scientific iteration. In R&D, you often need to tinker quickly. A rigid mandatory integration could frustrate researchers and create compliance overhead that slows science.\n4. However, the alternative (each team builds their own) is worse for resource efficiency long-term.\n5. The proposal doesn't mention maintenance costs, versioning strategy, or what happens when a new model architecture makes the existing API awkward to integrate.\n6. From a funding constraint perspective: this is a sunk-cost infrastructure investment that doesn't directly produce scientific output. It's necessary compliance overhead, but it competes with research budgets.\n\n**Decision:** This is a reasonable and well-motivated proposal from an efficiency standpoint. A shared logging framework IS the resource-efficient approach vs. per-project solutions. The modular API design