ficiency standpoint, I need to ask: does the lab already have capacity? Can this be handled by augmenting existing engineering work rather than spinning up a full research project?\n- The tension between traceability (Article 12) and privacy is real but perhaps overblown in this framing. For most Annex III systems, the \"natural persons involved in verification\" are likely internal staff whose identification can be handled through access control and role-based logging without needing SMPC-level solutions. Differential privacy on logs that need to record specific events (start/end times, input data matches) is technically challenging because DP adds noise — but Article 12 requires recording *specific* events, not aggregate statistics. Adding noise to \"who verified this\" or \"what time the system was used\" could actually violate the precision requirements of Article 12(3).\n- SMPC for logging is extremely resource-intensive in practice. Running SMPC over every log event would be a massive computational overhead. This might not scale for systems that need to log continuously over their lifetime (Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime\").\n- The proposal conflates two different problems: (1) ensuring logs meet Article 12's specific requirements, and (2) protecting personal data in those logs under GDPR. Problem (1) is an engineering/compliance task; problem (2) can be largely addressed through standard data governance (access controls, encryption at rest, retention policies, pseudonymization). A full \"R&D project\" exploring DP and SMPC seems like overkill for what might be a configuration and policy problem.\n- Resource efficiency: if we already have engineers building the logging infrastructure to meet Article 12, adding a privacy layer is an incremental task. Spinning up a separate \"research project\" with its own timeline, publications, experiments adds overhead.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core insight — that there's a tension between detailed traceability and