oach (new R&D project with DP/SMPC) is a resource-inefficient way to solve what is largely an engineering-and-policy problem. The lab's scarce resources would be better deployed on actually building the Article 12 logging infrastructure and then applying standard privacy-by-design measures.\n\nBut wait — let me reconsider. Is there genuine scientific novelty here that justifies R&D investment? DP applied to log streams is an active research area. SMPC for specific verification workflows might have novel applications. If the lab has existing infrastructure in cryptographic methods, the marginal cost might be lower than it appears. The \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" criterion matters — if we already have a crypto/privacy research group, this could slot in with minimal incremental cost.\n\nStill, I think the framing as a standalone project exploring DP and SMPC for logging is over-scoped. The practical compliance need doesn't require that level of cryptographic sophistication for most Annex III systems. I'll reject but give a moderate promise score to acknowledge the underlying tension is real and a narrower version could be worthwhile.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, with a promise score around 35-40. The problem identification is good (maybe 60/100 on that axis), but the solution prescription is resource-inefficient and potentially over-engineered for what's needed. I'd rather see this as a design constraint within the logging build, not a separate research project.\n",
