ing what you already have. This is relatively low incremental cost compared to the actual engineering work of implementing logging. The audit itself is primarily an analytical/administrative exercise that can leverage existing documentation and project descriptions.\n\n**Strengths from my perspective:**\n- Preventive: catching compliance issues early is far cheaper than retrofitting or facing penalties later\n- Triage function: it creates a clear picture of where resources are actually needed for Article 12 implementation vs. where they're not\n- \"Flagged for modification\" and \"pivot away\" shows resource-awareness — you can't do everything, so prioritize\n- Establishes a registry which is foundational infrastructure for ongoing compliance (not just a one-time cost)\n- The audit itself is relatively low-cost compared to the engineering work it informs\n\n**Weaknesses from my perspective:**\n- \"Comprehensive review of ALL current research trajectories\" could be scope creep — in a large R&D lab, this could consume significant time if done without clear boundaries\n- \"Mandate retrofitting\" is stated as a given but doesn't address the cost of that retrofitting or whether it's proportionate to the scientific value\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level; the actual resource commitment isn't specified\n- Risk of creating bureaucratic overhead that slows research throughput\n- \"Pivot away from projects where logging is technically unfeasible\" — this could be a significant opportunity cost if those projects are scientifically valuable\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is fundamentally a good first step. You cannot efficiently allocate resources for Article 12 compliance without first knowing which of your systems are in scope. The audit/classification function is the prerequisite to all other work. It's relatively low-cost (analytical work by compliance team + project leads confirming their system characteristics) relative to the downstream engineering it informs. The