entation is auto-generated from actual system metadata, it's less likely to diverge from reality. This supports \"verifying compliance with the requirements under this Regulation.\"\n- Integration into CI/CD means every release cycle automatically updates documentation, which aligns with \"throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\"\n\n**Concerns/limitations from a compliance perspective:**\n- The proposal focuses on technical metadata (hyperparameters, dataset hashes, validation results). Article 12 and Recital 71 require more: risk-management system documentation, general characteristics, capabilities AND limitations, intended purpose descriptions. An automated pipeline extracting hyperparameters won't generate narrative risk assessments or limitation analyses. So it's a partial solution.\n- The proposal conflates technical documentation with logging (Article 12(1)-(3)). Article 12 is specifically about automatic recording of events (logs) over the system's lifetime, not just generating static documentation reports. The \"structured reports\" approach may miss the continuous event-stream logging requirement. Logs need to capture operational events in real-time during use, not just training metadata.\n- However, the proposal does say it extracts from \"version control and training metadata\" which is more about development-phase documentation than operational logs. Article 12(3)(a) requires recording start/end times of each USE - that's operational, not developmental.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- Having automated documentation reduces the risk of non-compliance at audit time (no more \"we forgot to update section 4.2 after the last model retraining\"). This is a genuine compliance improvement.\n- It demonstrates good faith and systematic approach to regulators, which helps market reputation.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\n- A structured, automated documentation pipeline signals maturity and seriousness about AI governance to clien