periments, models, and architectures vary enormously) could be resource-intensive upfront. The word \"universal\" is ambitious.\n- Maintenance burden: In an R&D lab, pipelines change rapidly as researchers iterate on models. A standardized library needs ongoing maintenance to keep pace with evolving experimental setups. This is a long-term resource commitment.\n- Integration friction: Forcing all diverse research pipelines through a single API could create bottlenecks or slow down experimentation if the library doesn't fit every use case cleanly. In an R&D context, flexibility matters — researchers need to iterate quickly. A rigid \"by default\" logging layer might add friction to exploratory work.\n- The proposal says \"all high-risk AI pipelines\" but in an R&D institution, it's often unclear which systems will ultimately be classified as high-risk until they reach deployment. Over-engineering for a universal solution before the scope is clear could waste resources.\n\nHowever, from my specific vantage point:\n- Resource-allocation efficiency: This is clearly the most efficient approach versus N separate implementations. Strong positive.\n- Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints: By offloading compliance logging to infrastructure, researchers can focus on science. The one-time (plus maintenance) investment saves recurring researcher time. Positive.\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure: It integrates INTO existing pipelines rather than adding parallel systems. Good fit.\n\nThe main risk is the \"universal\" scope and the upfront development cost in a diverse R&D environment. But even accounting for that, the alternative (each team building their own) is clearly worse from a resource perspective. The proposal is well-aligned with my domain concerns.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly — maybe 78-82. It's efficient, it uses infrastructure well, it frees researchers to focus on science. But I'm not giving 90+ because \"universal\" across dive