tations), rather than requiring full rework. This is fiscally prudent.\n- Risk: over-engineering. If we build an overly elaborate framework before fully understanding all logging requirements across our portfolio of high-risk systems, we waste resources. But the \"modular\" and \"standardized APIs\" framing suggests a pragmatic, incremental approach rather than a big-bang solution.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal explicitly targets integration into existing training and inference loops via decorators — this means it layers onto what we already have rather than requiring parallel systems. Good use of existing infrastructure.\n- \"Centralized database\" for logs is reasonable — we likely already have data infrastructure that can host or connect to such a store, avoiding new capital expenditure.\n- The framework approach means as we develop new high-risk AI systems, they plug into the same logging layer — no reinvention.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Who builds and maintains this? If it's an engineering team, that's a real resource commitment. But given the cross-cutting nature of Article 12 compliance (applies to all high-risk systems), this is one of those cases where centralization clearly beats distributed effort.\n- \"Standardized\" could be a constraint if different research domains have genuinely different logging needs. But Article 12(3) specifies minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems, and Article 12(2) gives a general framework — so the core is well-defined enough to standardize while allowing modular extensions.\n- The proposal doesn't specify cost or timeline, but as a screening decision, I'm evaluating the concept's merit from my vantage point, not doing a full business case.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a high-leverage infrastructure investment that directly serves regulatory compliance while protecting researcher productivity. It uses existing pipelines (decorators on training/inference loops) rather than requiring new parallel systems. The modular design keeps future adaptation costs low. From a resource-allocation standpoint, this is one of the few proposals where the upfront cost is clearly amortized across all current and future high-risk projects in the lab. I'd approve it with confidence.\n\nPromise score: This aligns very well with my core concerns — efficiency (one framework serves all), protecting scientific capacity (reduces manual burden on researchers), leveraging existing infrastructure (decorators on existing loops, centralized DB). The main risk is execution quality and scope creep in the \"framework\" building phase, but conceptually this is strong. I'd put it around 82-85. It's not a 95+ because there's inherent risk in over-engineering compliance tooling before the regulatory landscape fully settles (implementing regulations may clarify specifics), and \"unified framework\" projects can sometimes balloon in scope. But it's clearly the right architectural approach for our lab.\n",
