        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2996\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This directly operationalises Recital 71's explicit obligation that technical documentation 'should be kept up to date…throughout the lifetime of the AI system' by binding it to version-controlled, auto-synchronised records rather than static artefacts—a material reduction in compliance-drift risk and inspection exposure. The main reservation is that the proposal focuses on development-phase documentation (training data, algorithms, validation) and does not explicitly cover Article 12(1)–(3)'s operational event-logging requirement, nor does it specify human review gates before auto-generated content enters the compliance registry; however, as a scoped pipeline for the technical-documentation stream, it is squarely within my mandate and materially lowers the probability of a market-surveillance finding under Article 72/79.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain concerns are institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2996 is about creating an \"Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline\" — a systemic workflow to automatically generate and synchronize technical documentation regarding training data, algorithms, and validation processes. It integrates version control with dynamic templates so codebase changes update the compliance registry in real time, creating a verifiable audit trail of development, while minimizing manual administrative overhead and satisfying the requirement to keep documentation up to date throughout the system lifetime.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance vantage point:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Article 12 and Recital 71 explicitly require that \"The technical documentation should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" This proposal directly addresses this requirement.\n- The Act requires documentation on \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system\" — the pipeline covers these elements.\n- A verifiable audit trail is crucial for regulatory inspections (Article 79 - non-compliance situations, Article 72 - post-market monitoring). If a regulator comes asking \"show me how this changed over time,\" having an automated, version-controlled trail is far superior to manual documentation.\n- Reducing manual overhead reduces the risk of human error in compliance documentation — a key concern for institutional survival.\n- \"Living records\" vs static documents aligns with the continuous nature of the obligation throughout the system lifetime.\n\n**Concerns/Risks:**\n- The proposal says it addresses technical documentation (training data, algorithms, validation) but doesn't explicitly mention Article 12's specific logging requirements (automatic recording of events/logs over the lifetime). It's more about development-phase documentation than operational logging. However, Recital 71 does tie both together under \"record-keeping.\"\n- \"Real time\" updates to a compliance registry — there's a risk that real-time auto-generation might produce incomplete or inaccurate documentation if the codebase changes are complex. A compliance officer would want human review gates. The proposal doesn't mention any validation or sign-off step for the generated documentation.\n- The scope seems focused on development-phase documentation (training data, algorithms, validation processes) rather than the full Article 12 logging requirement about operational events. But it does say \"compliance registry\" which could be broader.\n- Integration with version control is sensible and traceable — this supports auditability.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- If we don't have up-to-date technical documentation, we face non-compliance penalties under the AI Act (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, up to €15M or 3% for other violations). Automated generation reduces the risk of falling behind.\n- Market reputation: demonstrating a robust compliance infrastructure is a competitive advantage when selling to EU enterprise clients who will demand AI Act compliance documentation.\n\n**From regulatory compliance perspective:**\n- Directly addresses Recital 71's requirement for up-to-date technical documentation.\n- Creates traceability that supports Article 72 (post-market monitoring) and Article 79 (non-compliance identification).\n- The audit trail is verifiable, which is what regulators will demand during market surveillance.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\n- A mature compliance infrastructure signals to customers and partners that the