he operational/deployment phase logging requirements (Article 12(2)(b) post-market monitoring, Article 26(5) monitoring of operation).\n- It mentions \"inputs and system states\" but doesn't specifically address the detailed requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification): recording period of each use, reference database, input data matching results, identification of natural persons in verification. The proposal is generic enough that it might not cover these specific fields.\n- \"Internal API wrapper\" — this is an implementation detail that's reasonable but I need to ensure the logs are retained for the appropriate duration and are accessible for regulatory inspections (Article 12 also implies traceability for notified bodies and authorities).\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention data retention periods, access controls for regulators, or log immutability/integrity. These are important for compliance but might be assumed as part of a \"compliance framework.\"\n\n**Overall assessment from Compliance Officer perspective:**\nThis is fundamentally aligned with what Article 12 requires. Building an automated, unified logging layer that captures events by default across all high-risk systems is exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that reduces regulatory risk. The \"by default\" aspect is crucial — it means compliance doesn't depend on individual researcher diligence. This protects institutional survival (avoiding non-compliance penalties), supports market reputation (demonstrating robust compliance infrastructure to customers and regulators), and is a core operational requirement rather than an optional nice-to-have.\n\nThe concerns about Annex III specific fields and deployment-phase coverage are implementation details that would need to be addressed during development, but the fundamental architecture proposed is correct and necessary. I would approve this as it directly addresses the core legal obligation in A