en-ended research problem.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Custom script\" — how robust will this be across different projects, model types, and team conventions? If our researchers use heterogeneous stacks, the mapping might need significant maintenance. But MLflow/DVC already standardize a lot of this.\n- Recital 71 asks for more than just hyperparameters and dataset versions: it also requires documentation on \"relevant risk-management system\" and \"capabilities and limitations.\" The proposal focuses on technical extraction but may understate the need for human-authored narrative sections (risk management, intended purpose, known limitations). However, this is a partial solution to a larger problem — and as a Lab Director, I care about whether it moves the needle efficiently.\n- The resource commitment here seems modest: one or two engineers, leveraging existing tools, producing a pipeline that runs automatically. Compared to building entire logging infrastructure from scratch (Article 12(1)), this is a relatively low-cost intervention with high leverage.\n\n**Resource-allocation lens:**\n- This is a high-leverage, bounded-scope project. It converts recurring researcher time (documentation) into a one-time engineering investment plus minimal maintenance. For a lab constrained by funding and competing research priorities, preserving researcher hours for actual science is critical.\n- Using MLflow/DVC means we're not building new infrastructure — we're extending what we likely already use or can adopt cheaply. This aligns with \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\"\n- The risk is scope creep: if the \"custom script\" becomes a sprawling mapping engine trying to handle every edge case, it could consume more resources than anticipated. But the core value (automating extraction of standard training metadata) is clear and bounded.\n\n**Decision:** This clears my bar. It's efficient, leverages existing tooling, has bounded scope, addresses a genuine recurring cost, a