for biometric systems - reference databases, input data matches, verification personnel IDs), the \"mapping\" might be more complex than \"lightweight\" suggests.\n\n**Risks/Concerns from my vantage:**\n- The upfront cost of building and maintaining a universal middleware team is non-trivial. If we only have 2-3 high-risk systems, does the abstraction pay off? But even then, it's better than each project doing it separately.\n- \"Universal\" is an ambitious word. In practice, different system types (biometric verification under Annex III(1)(a) vs. other high-risk categories) have different logging requirements per Article 12(3). The middleware needs to handle these variations without becoming a one-size-fits-all compromise.\n- Maintenance burden: regulatory requirements evolve; the middleware team becomes a single point of dependency for compliance across all projects.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong, resource-efficient proposal from my perspective. It directly addresses the \"every project reinvents logging\" problem, leverages existing infrastructure (telemetry), and keeps research teams unblocked. The main risk is scope creep on the middleware itself and whether \"lightweight\" holds up against the specific granular requirements of Article 12(3) for certain system types. But the core logic - centralize compliance plumbing so scientists do science - is exactly right from a resource-allocation standpoint.\n\nI'd approve this. It's efficient, leverages existing infrastructure, and frees up project teams. My promise score would be high but not perfect - maybe 78-82 range. The \"universal\" claim introduces some risk of over-engineering or under-specification for edge cases in Article 12(3), and the maintenance cost of a dedicated middleware team is ongoing. But the fundamental approach is sound.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 80.\n",
