        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2054\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly targets the resource-duplication problem I'm most concerned about: if each of our N high-risk projects independently engineers its own logging stack, we burn aggregate budget on redundant tools that may still fall short of Article 12's technical requirements. Centralising a thin middleware layer over compute clusters we already operate converts a linear (per-project) compliance cost into a largely fixed one, and the 'by default' capture design means no individual team must divert research headcount to solve traceability from scratch. My main reservation is that building and maintaining this service itself demands sustained engineering effort that competes with active science, and that 'entire lifecycle / every execution' risks over-scoping beyond what Article 12(3) actually mandates for most of our system categories — but those are scoping-management issues, not fundamental flaws in the resource logic.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 80\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen proposal P2054 as the Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through my lens:\n\n**Proposal P2054: \"Unified Telemetry and Traceability Layer\"**\n\nThe proposal suggests building a centralized middleware service that:\n- Provides a standardized API and database system\n- Automatically captures mandated logs across all high-risk AI projects throughout their entire lifecycle\n- Integrates with existing compute clusters\n- Records timestamps, inputs, and user IDs by default for every execution\n- Prevents individual research teams from wasting limited funding on building redundant, fragmented logging tools that may not meet legal standards\n\n**From my vantage point (resource-allocation efficiency, scientific ambition vs. funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure):**\n\nArguments FOR:\n1. **Resource allocation efficiency**: This is directly aligned with my core concern. By centralizing the logging/traceability infrastructure, we avoid N research teams each spending budget on building their own fragmented solutions. This is a classic case where a shared service saves aggregate resources. The proposal explicitly frames this as preventing \"wasting limited funding on building redundant, fragmented logging tools.\"\n2. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: It proposes integrating with \"existing compute clusters\" rather than requiring new hardware procurement. This leverages what we already have.\n3. **Compliance at scale**: Article 12 requires automatic recording over the lifetime of the system. A unified layer ensures all projects get this capability without each team having to solve it independently. The technical documentation requirement (Recital 71) also benefits from a single, consistent data store.\n4. **Scalability**: As we develop more high-risk AI systems, the marginal cost of adding another project to the logging infrastructure drops significantly with a unified layer vs. building point solutions each time.\n\nArguments AGAINST / Concerns:\n1. **Upfront investment**: Building a centralized middleware service is itself a significant resource commitment. It requires engineering time, maintenance, and potentially new skill sets (database administration, API design) that may not currently exist in the lab.\n2. **Scientific ambition concern**: There's a risk that building this infrastructure diverts senior engineers away from research projects. However, the proposal frames it as integrating with existing clusters, suggesting it's an add-on rather than a replacement for research compute.\n3. **\"By default for every execution\"**: This could create overhead or performance concerns for compute-intensive research workloads. But logging timestamps and user IDs is relatively lightweight.\n4. **Scope creep risk**: \"Throughout their entire lifecycle\" is broad. The actual Article 12 requirements are specific (period of use, reference databases, input data for matches, natural person identification). A unified layer that captures more than needed could be over-engineered.\n5. **Single point of failure / maintenance burden**: Centralizing creates a dependency. If the middleware goes down, all projects lose logging capability. But this is manageable with good engineering practices.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis proposal directly addresses my core mandate: preventing resource waste from duplicated efforts and leveraging existing infrastructure efficiently. The framing is sound — rather than each of our N research teams burning through their budgets building bespoke logging solutions (some that may not meet Article 12 standards), we invest once in a shared service. The integration with existing compute clusters shows awareness of not requiring new capital expenditure on hardware.\n\nThe main risk is the upfront engineering cost and whether it diverts too many resources from active research. But given that compliance is mandatory (not optional) for all high-risk systems, and that fragmented approaches would cost MORE in aggregate, the unified approach is clearly more efficient from a resource-allocation standpoint.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a well-motivated infrastructure investment that directly serves efficiency goals. I'd approve it with a solid promise score — maybe 78-82. It's very aligned with my mandate but not a 100 because of the upfront cost and the risk of over-building beyond what Article 12 strictly requires, plus maintenance burden. But it's clearly one I would back.\n\nLet me settle on a promise score: I'll go with 80. It's strongly aligned with resource efficiency and infrastructure reuse, but there are legitimate concerns about upfront engineering investment and potential scope bloat that keep it from being a \"without hesitation\" backing.\n",
