        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2329\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Early classification is a prerequisite I cannot function without: if we do not know by the design phase whether a system triggers Article 12's logging mandate, we risk shipping a non-compliant product and facing enforcement action or market withdrawal. The core logic—gate expensive logging engineering behind a verified high-risk determination—is sound from both a resource-allocation and an institutional-survival standpoint. However, a *monthly* batch cadence is a genuine liability: R&D projects can cross the deployment threshold between meetings, leaving us in a window where a system is live with no automatic-event-logging capability built in, which is a per-se violation of Article 12(1) and exposes the lab to fines under Article 99. The two-person board (one researcher + compliance officer) is also thin for the nuanced Annex III category analysis (e.g., biometric verification vs. mere quality-control screening), though as a starting protocol it is proportionate.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 73\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to act as a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. I need to screen proposal P2329 from my professional vantage point: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n\"High-Risk Classification Triage Framework. A streamlined internal protocol to determine early in the R&D cycle whether a system falls under Article 12's high-risk mandate. We will establish a monthly cross-functional review board consisting of one lead researcher and the compliance officer to categorize new projects. This ensures we only allocate expensive logging resources to projects that actually require them legally.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point as Compliance Officer:\n\n**Positive aspects:**\n- Early classification is critical for compliance. If you don't know whether your system is high-risk, you can't ensure Article 12 obligations are met in time. This is a genuine compliance gap that needs addressing.\n- The proposal directly supports the regulatory requirement by ensuring logging capabilities are built in from the start rather than retrofitted.\n- It's resource-efficient — not every project will be high-risk, and building full logging infrastructure for non-high-risk systems wastes resources.\n- Cross-functional involvement (researcher + compliance) is sensible because classification often requires understanding both the technical nature of the system AND the regulatory criteria (Annex III categories).\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Monthly\" review cadence could be too slow. If a project starts and is deployed before the next monthly board, you've already missed the window to build logging in. The EU AI Act doesn't give you a grace period — if your system is high-risk and goes live without proper logging, that's a non-compliance event with potential penalties.\n- \"One lead researcher and the compliance officer\" — this is a very thin governance structure. Classification under Annex III (biometrics, critical infrastructure, education, employment, essential services, law enforcement, migration, justice) requires nuanced legal judgment. A single researcher + one compliance officer might not catch edge cases or novel applications. But for an R&D institution that may have limited staff, this could be proportionate.\n- The phrase \"whether a system falls under Article 12's high-risk mandate\" is slightly imprecise — Article 12 applies to ALL high-risk systems (those in Annex I product safety and Annex III standalone). The classification isn't really about \"Article 12's mandate\" specifically but about whether the system is high-risk under Articles 6, 87-91. This is a minor wording issue but shows some imprecision.\n- \"Streamlined\" could be a red flag for compliance — overly streamlined processes risk missing classification triggers. However, the goal of efficiency is legitimate.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- Getting this right early prevents costly retrofits and regulatory exposure.\n- A clear triage process means we're not accidentally deploying non-compliant systems.\n- This protects market reputation — if a regulator finds we deployed a high-risk system without proper logging because we \"didn't classify it yet,\" that's bad.\n\n**From regulatory compliance perspective:**\n- The EU AI Act requires you to know what your obligations are BEFORE deployment. This framework supports that.\n- However, the monthly cadence is a real concern. Projects can move fast in R&D.\n- The board composition (2 people) might be insufficient for complex classification questions, but it's a starting point.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\n- Having a documented internal process shows regulators and customers that we take compliance seriously.\n- It demonstrates organizational maturity.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a genuinely useful proposal from my vantage point. The core idea — early classification to ensure proper logging resources are allocated — is sound and directly supports Article 12 compliance. The main weakness is the monthly cadence (should be per-project-trigger rather than batch) and the thin governance structure, but these are implementation details that can be refined. The proposal addresses a real compliance gap and protects institutional survival by ensuring we don't accidentally deploy non-compliant systems.\n\nI would approve this with moderate-to-high promise. It's directionally correct and addresses a genuine need from my perspective. The monthly cadence is concerning but the overall framework is valuable. I'd say around 72-78 for promise — it's solid but has refinements needed (cadence, governance depth).\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 74.\n",
