        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2644\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core technical challenge—designing reliable semantic probe layers that capture uncertainty, out-of-distribution encounters, and failure-mode signatures in deployed high-risk systems—is genuinely at the knowledge frontier of uncertainty quantification and interpretability research, and the resulting corpus of labelled risk-state transitions across multiple deployments could constitute a scientifically valuable dataset for studying real-world AI robustness. However, the proposal is underdeveloped as a research programme: it does not articulate which specific OOD/uncertainty methods would be probed, what hypotheses about failure-mode structure are being tested, or how the 'semantic markers' differ from existing confidence-thresholding approaches; it reads primarily as an engineering deliverable with a research justification appended rather than a clearly framed investigation with expected scientific contributions beyond compliance.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 67\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P2644 from the perspective of a Researcher at an R&D institution focused on \"the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact.\"\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**Proposal P2644: Semantic Event Logging for Risk Detection**\n\nThe proposal describes developing custom probe layers that record high-level semantic markers (model uncertainty, failure modes) rather than just simple system events. It focuses on Article 12(2)(a) - identifying situations where the AI presents a risk or undergoes substantial modification. Key elements:\n- Records model uncertainty and out-of-distribution inputs\n- Custom probe layers that trigger detailed logs in low-confidence states\n- Implemented as a shared library for all high-risk projects\n- Satisfies Article 12 while providing \"a rich dataset for studying AI robustness and reliability\"\n\nNow let me assess from the research/knowledge frontier/intellectual merit/broader scientific impact perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The idea of semantic-level logging (uncertainty, OOD detection) goes beyond trivial event logging. This is genuinely at the knowledge frontier - understanding when an AI system \"doesn't know\" it's failing is a deep research problem in uncertainty quantification, out-of-distribution detection, and model interpretability.\n- Developing custom probe layers for triggering logs based on confidence states touches on active areas of ML research (epistemic vs aleatoric uncertainty, OOD detection methods).\n- The \"rich dataset for studying AI robustness and reliability\" is a genuine scientific contribution - having systematic records of when models encounter uncertain or OOD situations across multiple high-risk deployments could be valuable for the broader community.\n- As a shared library, it creates infrastructure that standardizes how uncertainty/failure modes are captured, which has methodological value.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat vague on what \"custom probe layers\" actually means technically. OOD detection and uncertainty quantification are hard problems with no single settled solution. The proposal doesn't specify which methods or approaches would be used.\n- The connection between \"semantic markers of model uncertainty\" and the regulatory requirement in 12(2)(a) is somewhat loose. Article 12(2)(a) asks for identifying situations that \"may result in\" risk - this is broader than just low-confidence states. A model could be highly confident but wrong (overconfident failure modes). The proposal narrows the scope to \"low-confidence states or out-of-distribution inputs,\" which might miss some risk scenarios.\n- The \"broader scientific impact\" claim about providing a dataset for studying AI robustness is somewhat secondary - it's essentially saying compliance work will generate useful data as a byproduct. This isn't wrong, but the primary motivation seems to be regulatory compliance with a research upside tacked on.\n- However, from my vantage point (research merit and knowledge frontier), the core technical challenge of developing reliable uncertainty/failure-mode detectors that can trigger meaningful semantic logs IS a legitimate and intellectually meritorious research direction. It sits at the intersection of uncertainty quantification, interpretability, and system monitoring - all active and important areas.\n\n**Intellectual Merit Assessment:**\nThe proposal touches on genuinely open research questions: How do you reliably detect when an AI system is in a \"risky\" state? What are good semantic markers for failure modes that go beyond raw confidence scores? How do you design probe layers that add minimal overhead while capturing meaningful information? These are non-trivial problems.\n\nHowever, the proposal as stated is more of an engineering implementation plan (\"we will implement these as a shared library\") than a research program. The \"researchers will develop\" framing is thin - it doesn't articulate what specific scientific questions would be investigated or what hypotheses would be tested. It reads more like \"build OOD detection into our logging pipeline\" with a research justification appended.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\nThe dataset angle has potential, but it's contingent on the system being deployed across multiple high-risk projects AND the semantic markers being meaningful enough to study from. If the probe layers are crude (e.g., just thresholding confidence scores), the scientific value is limited. If they're sophisticated (e.g., ensemble disagreement, energy-based OOD detection, feature-space anomaly detection), the dataset could be genuinely valuable for understanding failure modes in deployed systems.\n\n**My Decision:**\nFrom my research perspective, this proposal has moderate intellectual merit. The core problem (semantic-level risk/failure detection) is at the knowledge frontier and scientifically interesting. However, as proposed, it's somewhat underdeveloped as a research program - it reads more like an engineering deliverable with a research justification. The \"rich dataset for studying AI robustness\" is a nice byproduct but not clearly articulated as a primary research contribution.\n\nI think this clears my bar for approval because:\n1. The technical problem (reliable semantic failure-mode detection in deployed AI) is genuinely at the frontier\n2. It has potential broader scientific impact through the dataset and methodology\n3. As a shared library, it standardizes an approach that others could build upon\n\nBut I won't give it a very high promise score because:\n1. The research program isn't well-specified (what specific methods? what hypotheses?)\n2. The scope is narrow (focused on 12(2)(a) specifically)\n3. The scientific contribution is somewhat derivative of existing OOD/uncertainty literature unless the probe layer design introduces novel methodology