institution has systematic record-keeping. This is strong for market reputation.\n6. **\"Required dependency for all new projects\":** This is a critical governance mechanism — it ensures no new high-risk system escapes the logging requirement. This is exactly what a compliance officer wants: prevention over detection.\n\nPotential concerns:\n1. **Scope limitation:** The proposal mentions \"high-risk AI prototypes\" but Article 12 applies to the lifetime of the system, not just prototypes. Need to ensure this extends through deployment and post-market monitoring (Article 72). However, the text says \"over the lifetime of the system\" in context, so it may be implicitly included.\n2. **Article 12(3)(d) - identification of natural persons:** The proposal mentions timestamps, database versions, and input hashes but doesn't explicitly mention recording identification of natural persons involved in verification (Article 14(5)). This is a gap for Annex III 1(a) systems specifically. However, the framework is described as modular, so this could be an additional module.\n3. **Resource commitment:** As a compliance officer, I need to consider whether committing resources to build a full software library is proportionate vs. simply requiring existing tools. But given that we're an R&D institution developing multiple AI systems, the one-time investment pays off in consistency and audit-readiness.\n4. **\"Existing training pipelines\":** The proposal focuses on training pipeline integration, but Article 12 logging extends beyond training to operational/deployment events (post-market monitoring, operation monitoring under Art 26(5)). The framework needs to extend to runtime/operational logging too.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong compliance-aligned proposal. It directly implements the core technical requirement of Article 12 in an institutional, systematic way. From my vantage point:\n- **Institutional survival:** Reduces regulatory breach risk by making compliance struc