tutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation), this is a high-value investment because:\n- It reduces the probability of documentation gaps being found in regulatory inspections\n- It demonstrates systematic compliance culture to regulators and market participants\n- It scales with the institution's R&D output without proportional increase in compliance staff\n\nThe main risk I'd flag is ensuring completeness — that \"automated\" doesn't mean \"incomplete automation.\" But as a proposal to commit resources toward, it clearly clears my bar. I would approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with my core concerns. It's not 100 because there are implementation risks (real-time reliability, completeness of captured data points, coverage of all Article 12 requirements vs. just documentation) and it doesn't explicitly address the event-logging within the system itself (Article 12(1)-(3)). But from a compliance infrastructure standpoint, this is highly promising. I'd say around 82-85.\n",
