cts ship creates a bottleneck. However, if scoped tightly (just the logging middleware + schema), it could be delivered in a sprint or two.\n4. The proposal is somewhat generic — it doesn't specify what \"events\" means concretely for different AI system types (biometric vs. medical vs. employment). Article 12(3) has specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems that go beyond generic logging.\n\nFrom my vantage point: The idea has merit but the framing suggests it might be over-engineered for what's actually needed during prototyping. A lighter instrumentation layer would serve the same compliance purpose with less friction. The \"unified framework\" ambition could become a platform-team bottleneck that slows down the very projects we're trying to support.\n\nI'd lean toward approving because the core concept (shared logging library, consistent schema) is technically viable and would genuinely reduce per-project prototyping overhead once built. But my confidence isn't at 100 because of the over-engineering risk and the \"intercept system calls\" framing that suggests a heavier implementation than necessary.\n\nDecision: I'll approve it — the core idea is sound, technically feasible, and would serve prototyping speed well if scoped correctly. But promise score should reflect the risks around scope creep and the potentially overengineered implementation approach. Maybe 62-70 range. The idea is right but the execution as described has friction points for rapid prototyping.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 65.\n",
