posal specifically targets Article 12(3)(b), which applies to Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, categorization, emotion recognition). This narrows the scope considerably - these systems typically have well-defined reference databases (e.g., a biometrics DB) that are bounded in size.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- For prototyping: A basic version is quite achievable. You can start with a simple approach: at each inference, compute a hash of the relevant database partition and store it in the log. This could be prototyped in days to a couple of weeks.\n- The \"Git-like versioning\" part adds complexity but isn't strictly necessary for v1. A content-addressed blob store (or even just storing DB checksums + timestamps) achieves the regulatory goal.\n- Integration into existing data pipelines is straightforward - it's essentially adding a hook at inference time to record a database state identifier.\n- The scope is well-defined and bounded, which makes prototyping tractable.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n1. \"Git-like versioning\" is somewhat over-engineered for what's needed. You don't need full diff tracking; you need an identifiable reference point. A hash + timestamp + metadata record suffices. Calling it \"Git-like\" might lead the team down a rabbit hole of implementing proper VCS semantics.\n2. Storage cost for truly immutable, permanent records could be significant over years of operation, but this is more of an ops/infrastructure concern than a prototyping blocker.\n3. Real-time hashing overhead: need to benchmark whether computing DB hashes on every inference call is acceptable latency-wise. For bounded reference databases (biometrics), this should be fine.\n4. The proposal is narrowly scoped to 12(3)(b), which is good for prototyping - it's a concrete, testable deliverable.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a technically viable, well-scoped proposal that directly addresses a specific regulatory requirement (Article 12(3)(b)