sal focuses on \"presence\" of logging capabilities rather than their functional adequacy. This is a gap - you can have logging code present but misconfigured or insufficient to meet the Article 12(2) requirements about traceability appropriate to intended purpose.\n3. However, as a first layer / gate, it's still valuable. It's not meant to be the sole compliance mechanism but rather an early warning system.\n4. The \"checklist-based\" nature is somewhat reductive for what Article 12 actually demands - the logging requirements are context-dependent (they depend on the intended purpose of the system per Art. 12(2)), so a static checklist may not fully capture this nuance.\n\n**My assessment as Compliance Officer:**\nThis is a genuinely useful compliance infrastructure investment. It directly operationalizes Article 12(1) at an enforcement point (production deployment). Even if imperfect, it creates:\n- A verifiable record that the institution systematically checks for logging compliance\n- Early detection of gaps before they become audit findings\n- Reduced risk of market reputation damage from shipping non-compliant systems\n- Institutional survival protection by preventing regulatory penalties\n\nThe limitation is that it's a necessary but not sufficient control. But as one layer in a compliance program, it's clearly valuable. It doesn't over-promise - it says \"verify the presence\" which is honest about its scope. It won't replace full functional testing of logging adequacy, but it's a good first gate.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's directly relevant to Article 12 compliance, reduces regulatory risk, and is a reasonable use of implementation resources given that CI/CD pipeline modifications are relatively contained engineering work. The scope is appropriately bounded (it's a presence check, not a full functional validation).\n\nPromise score: This is solidly useful but has the limitation of being a presence-check rather than a functional-adequacy check. It addresses the most basic requirement (Art 12(1) - logging must exist and function) but doesn't fully address Art 12(2) adequacy or Art 12(3) specific minimums for Annex III systems. I'd say around 72-78. It's clearly worth doing, it directly maps to the regulation, and it protects against the most obvious compliance failure mode. But it's not a complete solution and its \"checklist-based\" nature has limitations for context-dependent requirements.\n\nI'll go with 74.\n",
