ort. The recurring nature of compliance work makes automation particularly cost-effective compared to manual approaches.\n\n**Arguments against / concerns:**\n1. \"Dynamically links code commits, hyperparameters, and dataset updates\" - this sounds ambitious. Getting true dynamic linking working reliably across all our systems (different frameworks, different data pipelines, different model architectures) could be a significant engineering investment upfront. The question is whether the setup cost is proportionate to what we save.\n2. \"Auto-generate comprehensive technical reports\" - there's a risk that auto-generated documents don't meet the \"clear and comprehensive form\" requirement of Recital 71. Regulatory documentation often requires human judgment in explaining design choices, trade-offs, and contextual reasoning. Pure automation might produce technically accurate but contextually shallow documents. This could lead to rework or audit failures.\n3. The scope is broad - linking ALL code commits, hyperparameters, AND dataset updates into one pipeline is a substantial engineering project. For a lab with limited resources, this could consume significant engineering bandwidth before delivering value.\n4. \"Specialized documentation tools\" - what does that cost? Licensing? Maintenance? This adds to the infrastructure stack we need to maintain.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound and addresses a real, recurring cost driver in AI R&D labs subject to EU AI Act compliance. The integration with existing CI/CD infrastructure is smart - it leverages what we already have rather than building parallel systems. The resource savings from eliminating manual retrospective documentation are substantial and recurring, which makes the upfront investment recoverable over time.\n\nHowever, I need to be careful about scope creep. The proposal as stated is quite broad (\"comprehensive technical reports,\" \"single source of truth\"). In practice, the highest-ROI piece wou