sk\" in the formal sense\n- The requirement that logs meet Annex III requirements BEFORE testing is problematic — how do you know if your logging is adequate before you've actually tested the system? You might discover during testing that certain events need to be logged that weren't anticipated.\n\nHowever, I should also consider: having a clear spec of what logging needs to look like BEFORE you start prototyping IS valuable from an engineering standpoint. It prevents rework. If I know upfront what fields need to be captured (start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, verification person IDs), I can design the logging infrastructure into the architecture rather than bolting it on later. That's technically sound and could actually save time in the long run.\n\nBut the proposal as written is primarily a governance/process mechanism, not a technical solution. It doesn't address:\n- What the actual logging architecture looks like\n- How to implement automatic event recording (Article 12(1))\n- The technical design of log storage, retention, and retrieval\n- How to ensure logs are \"comprehensible\" as Recital 71 requires\n- Performance implications of comprehensive logging on system speed\n\nFrom my engineering vantage point:\n- The idea of front-loading compliance requirements into the design phase is reasonable (shift-left testing/compliance)\n- But a pure checklist sign-off gate without technical substance doesn't help me build better systems faster\n- It adds process overhead without providing technical guidance\n- The \"before any system moves to testing\" timing is counterproductive for prototyping — I need to iterate and test to discover what logging events actually matter\n\nI think this is a borderline case. The spirit (define requirements early) is good, but the mechanism (bureaucratic gate before testing) conflicts with agile prototyping. From my vantage point specifically focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping, this proposal:\n- Doesn't address technical implementation\n- Adds process friction to the prototyping cycle\n- The \"checklist\" approach may be too rigid for evolving systems\n- But having requirements defined early is technically sound\n\nI'll lean toward a low-to-moderate promise score. It's not harmful per se, but it doesn't advance my core concerns (technical viability of solutions, speed/quality of prototyping). It's more of a governance/compliance proposal that happens to touch on my domain tangentially. The actual technical work I'd need to do isn't addressed here.\n\nDecision: reject — from my vantage point, this is a process/governance mechanism that adds friction without providing the technical substance needed for viable, fast prototyping. It doesn't help me build better logging systems; it just gates their release. The actual engineering problem (how to implement compliant logging) remains unaddressed.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 25-30. There's some value in front-loading requirements definition, but the mechanism as proposed conflicts with iterative prototyping and doesn't address technical implementation. It's not something I'd \"never back\" (it's not harmful), but it's not compelling from my perspective either.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 28.\n",
