e. It doesn't push the knowledge frontier in any novel way, and its intellectual merit is moderate at best. The broader impact would be limited to the regulatory compliance community rather than the wider AI research community.\n\nHowever, I could see arguments for approval if one values:\n- Creating a labeled dataset of risk events (this IS a contribution to the field)\n- The meta-monitoring question is genuinely open and important\n\nBut the proposal as written doesn't frame these as research questions to be investigated — it frames them as engineering deliverables. From a pure \"knowledge frontier\" perspective, this is borderline. I'll lean toward reject with a moderate promise score, because there IS some intellectual substance but it's not framed or positioned to advance the frontier meaningfully.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The creation of a labeled dataset of risk events for AI systems is genuinely valuable for science — we currently lack such datasets. And the question \"can secondary models reliably detect drift in primary AI system logs?\" is an open empirical question with real uncertainty. That does have research merit. But it's not clearly positioned as a research investigation; it's positioned as building a compliance tool.\n\nI'll go with reject, promise around 35-40. There's some scientific value buried here, but the proposal doesn't clear the bar for \"advancing the knowledge frontier\" from my vantage point. It's applied work that happens to touch on interesting questions without actually investigating them as open problems.\n",
