        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2699 is about \"Research into Low-Overhead Traceability Mechanisms\" — specifically, it's a scientific research project to minimize computational performance impact of high-frequency event logging in real-time, high-risk AI systems, testing sampling and compression techniques for logs while maintaining regulatory utility without slowing inference speeds.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The problem is real: Article 12(1) requires \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" For high-frequency, real-time AI systems (e.g., those doing continuous inference at millisecond latencies), naive logging can indeed add significant overhead. This is a legitimate engineering concern.\n- However, the proposal frames this as a \"scientific project\" aimed at \"novel publication opportunity in the field of systems research.\" That framing is concerning from my vantage point. As a development engineer focused on prototyping speed and quality, I need solutions that work NOW for our specific systems, not open-ended research projects with uncertain timelines.\n- The technical approaches mentioned (sampling and compression) are well-understood engineering techniques. There's no fundamental research gap here — this is applied engineering optimization, not basic science. Log sampling, delta encoding, ring buffers, async write-behind logging, etc., are all established patterns.\n- \"Maintain regulatory utility\" is a key constraint but it's somewhat vague. What constitutes \"regulatory utility\" for logs under Article 12? The regulation requires specific data points (especially for Annex III point 1(a) systems: start/end times, reference databases, input data, person IDs). You can't arbitrarily sample away required fields.\n- For our actual high-risk AI systems, the logging overhead is likely manageable with standard engineering practices (async logging, batched writes, structured logging frameworks). The performance impact of appending a log entry to a buffer is typically negligible compared to model inference time.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is framed as a research project, which by nature has open-ended timelines and uncertain deliverables. From my perspective, I need concrete, implementable solutions I can prototype quickly. A \"research into\" project doesn't give me that.\n- The proposal doesn't specify which systems we're targeting, what the current performance bottlenecks actually are, or what baseline logging approach exists. It's too abstract to be actionable for prototyping.\n- However, if there IS a genuine performance bottleneck from logging in one of our specific real-time systems, then targeted optimization work would be valuable. But that would be an engineering task, not a \"scientific project\" aimed at publications.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe underlying concern is valid (logging shouldn't degrade real-time inference), but the proposal as framed is:\n1. Too research-oriented and publication-driven for my prototyping-focused role\n2. The problem space is well-trodden engineering territory, not requiring a \"scientific project\"\n3. Lacks specificity about which systems, what current bottlenecks exist\n4. The \"sampling\" approach raises compliance concerns — you can't sample away required log fields under Article 12(3)\n5. From a speed-of-prototyping standpoint, this would consume engineering time on an open-ended research question rather than delivering a working logging solution\n\nI think this should be rejected from my vantage point. The promise score: the underlying problem is real but the framing as a research/publication project misaligns with what I need (fast, concrete prototyping solutions). I'd give it maybe 25-30. There's some merit to