es researcher burden), but the execution as described is overly ambitious for our resource constraints and doesn't adequately consider existing infrastructure. Building a custom unified framework from scratch is more expensive than adapting/layering onto what we already have. However, I do think SOME form of standardization is necessary — we can't let each project solve this independently.\n\nThe question is: does it clear my bar? My bar is: does this proposal represent an efficient use of our scarce implementation resources to meet compliance requirements while preserving scientific capacity? \n\nI think the proposal has a valid core insight but is over-scoped and potentially inefficient in its proposed approach (custom development vs. leveraging existing infrastructure). It commits significant platform engineering resources for what could be achieved with less by adapting existing tools. However, it's not wrong — standardization IS needed, and having a single point of compliance maintenance IS valuable.\n\nI'll lean toward approve but with moderate promise, because the core direction is right (standardize, automate, reduce researcher burden) even if the specific implementation path described might be suboptimal from a resource perspective. Actually, let me reconsider — \"approve means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it.\" If I approve this as written, we commit to building a custom unified framework integrated into ALL pipelines. That's a large commitment. But rejecting it entirely would mean each project handles its own logging, which is less efficient in aggregate.\n\nI think I'll approve it because:\n- The standardization principle is correct and necessary\n- Automation reduces ongoing researcher time (which IS our most scarce resource)\n- A single maintenance point for regulatory updates is efficient long-term\n- The scope, while large, addresses a genuine compliance requirement we must meet anyway\n\nBut my promise score should 