ation mechanism tied to actual logging volumes or system lifetimes, this could underfund and become a source of friction later. But as an initial structural proposal, the vagueness might be acceptable — it sets up the governance framework first.\n2. The term \"subsidize\" is slightly odd for internal reallocation — you're not subsidizing, you're earmarking. Minor language issue but suggests maybe the framing isn't fully thought through.\n3. It only addresses storage and compute. Article 12 also requires maintaining technical documentation (Recital 71), which involves personnel costs, version control systems, document management infrastructure. This proposal is narrow in scope — it's a good first piece but doesn't cover the full compliance footprint.\n4. \"High-durability storage needed for lifetime logs\" — this is well-identified as a real need. Lifetime logging means data grows indefinitely and must be retained. This is a legitimate, non-negotiable infrastructure requirement that will compound over time.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a genuinely good resource-allocation intervention. It protects the research mission by isolating compliance costs into their own line item. It uses existing budget (reallocation) rather than asking for new money, which makes it more likely to actually get implemented. The risk of scope creep or underfunding exists, but as a structural first step, it's sound governance. It directly serves my concern about balancing scientific ambition with funding constraints by ensuring that one doesn't silently consume the other.\n\nI'd approve this. It's not a flashy proposal, it's not ambitious in a research sense, but from a resource-allocation and infrastructure management standpoint, it's exactly the kind of boring-but-essential governance mechanism that keeps an R&D lab functional under regulatory constraints. The promise score: I think this is genuinely useful and well-targeted. Not a 100 because it's narrow (only s