d framework across ALL high-risk projects could become a bottleneck if different model architectures (e.g., LLMs vs. traditional ML pipelines) need very different logging semantics.\n- However, the proposal says \"middleware or decorators\" which suggests it's designed to be composable and not overly prescriptive about internals.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n\nPros:\n- The core value proposition is exactly right for my domain: removing repetitive compliance boilerplate from every prototyping cycle. If I can spin up a new high-risk model iteration without re-implementing logging, that's a direct speed gain.\n- A consistent API means less cognitive overhead - engineers know how to instrument their code once and it works everywhere.\n- Automated capture (via decorators/middleware) vs. manual implementation is strictly faster for prototyping.\n\nCons:\n- Initial development of the framework itself has upfront cost, but that's amortized across all future projects.\n- If the framework becomes a bottleneck or requires waiting on the framework team for changes to support new logging needs, it could actually slow prototyping in edge cases.\n- But this is a standard tradeoff with shared infrastructure and is manageable.\n\n**Regulatory Fit:**\n- Article 12(1) requires \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events\" - this directly addresses that.\n- Article 12(3)(a)-(d) specifies minimum logging for Annex III point 1(a) systems: start/end times, reference database, input data matches, person identification. The proposal covers start/end times and reference DB versions explicitly. Input data hashes cover part of (c). Person identification (d) is harder to automate generically but could be handled as an API call.\n- The \"regulation-compliant structured format\" output addresses the traceability requirement in Recital 71.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound, well-scoped engineering solution that directly serves my domain's con