nput data for which search led to a match — not explicitly addressed\n- (d) identification of natural persons in verification per Article 14(5) — NOT addressed\n\n**Recital 71:** Requires comprehensible information on development, performance throughout lifetime, traceability, compliance verification, monitoring. Technical documentation kept up to date. Automatic recording via logs over lifetime.\n\nNow from my Compliance Officer vantage point:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses the core mandate of Article 12(1) — automatic logging\n- Standardized approach means consistency across all high-risk systems, reducing compliance gaps\n- Centralized secure repository supports traceability and audit readiness\n- Removes manual burden from developers → less likelihood of human error or omission in logging\n- \"Over the lifetime\" is addressed by streaming to a centralized repo (persistent)\n- Protects institutional survival: if regulators come knocking, having consistent, automatic logs across all systems dramatically reduces compliance risk\n- Market reputation: demonstrates proactive, systematic approach to regulatory compliance\n\n**Concerns/Gaps:**\n- The proposal focuses on \"prototypes\" — but Article 12 applies to high-risk AI *systems* in the market. Prototyping is one phase; the regulation requires logging over the *lifetime* of the system. Does this middleware extend to deployed systems? The text says \"all high-risk AI prototypes\" which might be too narrow.\n- The specific data points mentioned (start times, end times, input references) cover part of 12(3)(a) and (b) but don't explicitly address match data (12(3)(c)) or verifier identification (12(3)(d)).\n- \"Input references\" is vague — does this capture the reference database (12(3)(b))? Does it capture the actual input data that led to a match (12(3)(c))?\n- No mention of how logs are retained, their format for regulatory submission, or access controls for regulators.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly 