eability concerns (Recital 71) and data integrity — a regulator will want to know logs haven't been tampered with.\n- Removes manual burden from researchers — this reduces the risk of inconsistent or incomplete logging, which is a major compliance failure mode.\n- Consistency across projects means if one project needs an audit, all projects have uniform documentation trails.\n\nPotential gaps/concerns:\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input data, and system state\" but doesn't explicitly mention all the Para 2(a)-(c) event types (risk situations, substantial modifications, post-market monitoring events). However, this could be addressed in implementation specifications.\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems specifically, it needs to capture reference databases, match results, and verifier identities — the proposal is somewhat generic but \"system state\" could encompass these if properly configured per project type.\n- The lifetime requirement: \"over the lifetime of the system\" — the immutable database addresses this, but what about retention periods? Not explicitly mentioned but implied by immutability.\n- \"Technically allow for\" (Art 12(1)) — the middleware approach means the logging is built into the infrastructure, so it's technically inherent to the system. Good.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This is a foundational infrastructure investment that directly implements the core technical requirement of Article 12(1).\n- It creates institutional consistency, which is critical for demonstrating systemic compliance rather than ad-hoc per-project compliance.\n- The immutable database supports regulatory inspections and audits — if a regulator comes asking for logs, we can produce them from a single trusted source.\n- Removing manual burden reduces human error in logging, which is where most compliance failures happen.\n- Market reputation: having a robust, standardized logging infrastructure demonstrates to customers and partners that the institution takes 