t wouldn't satisfy the full Article 12 + Recital 71 documentation requirements because those require describing training data provenance, validation methodologies, risk management decisions, etc.\n\nThe question is: does this clear my bar? The proposal as written promises \"real-time\" compliance documentation \"without manual entry.\" That's not technically viable for the full scope. But a scoped-down version would be. However, I'm judging the proposal AS STATED, and as stated it overpromises significantly on technical feasibility.\n\nI'll reject it because:\n- The full scope claimed (automatic, real-time, no-manual-entry compliance documentation) is not technically viable - much of what's required simply doesn't exist in machine-readable form in code/logs.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, you can quickly build the easy parts but they don't deliver the promised value, creating a misleading prototype that doesn't represent the actual solution path.\n- The proposal would consume resources chasing automation for content that fundamentally requires human expert input (describing why certain algorithmic choices were made, documenting training data collection decisions, explaining risk management rationale).\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 25-30. There's a real but narrow kernel of useful engineering here (auto-populating structured fields from configs), and it addresses a real pain point. But the overall framing is technically overpromising for the scope implied, which would misdirect scarce resources.\n",
