ity requirements in Article 12(2) — being able to identify what data was used, when models changed, etc.\n\n**Institutional survival:** If a regulator audits us and finds our documentation is stale or incomplete (as static PDFs often are), that's a compliance failure with potential fines up to 7% of global turnover. A living system reduces this risk significantly. The alert system for documentation gaps is particularly valuable — it means we catch non-compliance before an auditor does.\n\n**Regulatory compliance:** This directly addresses the \"up to date throughout the lifetime\" requirement. Static documents inevitably become outdated as models are retrained, datasets change, capabilities shift. A dynamic system that pulls metadata from code and version control ensures documentation reflects actual state. The template-based approach also helps ensure all required elements under Recital 71 are covered systematically.\n\n**Market reputation:** Being able to demonstrate to partners, clients, and regulators that we have a robust, continuously-updated documentation framework is a strong market signal. It shows maturity and seriousness about compliance.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal focuses on the development phase (\"during development\"). Article 12 requires logging over the \"lifetime\" of the system — this includes post-market operation. Does the dynamic documentation extend to deployment? The text says \"over the lifetime of the system\" in the regulation but the proposal emphasizes \"during development.\" This is a gap, but not fatal — the framework could be extended.\n- It replaces static PDFs with a dynamic database. Regulators may still expect certain formal documents (like the technical documentation file under Article 17/Annex IV). The dynamic system should be able to generate those artifacts on demand. But this is an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw.\n- \"Alerts when documentation gaps occur\" — excellent for c