apture what's needed supports this\n\n**Weaknesses/Considerations:**\n- The scope seems focused on technical verification of the logging mechanism itself, which is necessary but perhaps not sufficient for full compliance (you also need to maintain the logs over lifetime, ensure they're accessible to authorities, etc.)\n- \"Adversarial testing framework\" could be resource-intensive - but as a Compliance Officer, I care about whether it actually proves compliance, and this seems like a reasonable approach\n- The phrase \"guarantees technical viability by proving the system is actually compliant before it leaves the lab\" is slightly overconfident - no test can fully \"guarantee\" compliance, but it significantly reduces risk\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\nThis is highly relevant. Article 12 is a mandatory requirement for high-risk AI systems. If our systems don't have functioning automatic logging, we're non-compliant from day one of deployment. Having an automated validation suite that stress-tests these capabilities before release:\n- Reduces the risk of deploying a non-compliant system (institutional survival)\n- Creates documented evidence of compliance verification (regulatory compliance - can show regulators \"we verified this\")\n- Strengthens market positioning by demonstrating rigorous internal quality assurance for regulatory requirements (market reputation)\n\nThe specific mention of biometric-style systems (Annex III point 1(a)) shows it's targeting the most tightly regulated category with explicit minimum logging requirements.\n\nThis is a strong, directly relevant proposal from my compliance perspective. I'd approve this with high confidence. The promise score should be high - maybe 82-88. It's not a perfect solution (it validates technical capability of logs but doesn't address the full lifecycle record-keeping obligation), but as a focused unit test for the logging mechanism, it's exactly what a compliance function would want to see in the pipeline before systems go live.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 84. It's very strong and directly addresses my domain, but I'll note that \"guarantees\" is slightly overpromising language, and it covers only the technical verification aspect of Article 12 rather than the full record-keeping obligation (which also includes maintaining documentation up to date throughout lifetime).\n",
