eady-made logging layer means new projects don't have to reinvent it. This is genuinely helpful for prototyping velocity.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Positive: Researchers get compliance \"for free\" — they just import the middleware and their logs are structured correctly. This reduces friction in prototyping cycles.\n- Positive: Standardized schema means downstream tools (compliance dashboards, audit queries) can work uniformly across projects.\n- Negative/Risk: If the framework is too rigid or opinionated, it could slow down prototyping when a new use case doesn't fit neatly into the \"standard\" event types. The \"universal API\" ambition could lead to scope creep or a bloated abstraction that takes longer to build than per-project logging would for early prototypes.\n- Negative: Building and maintaining this shared library is itself an engineering commitment. If it's not built right, every project inherits the technical debt.\n- The proposal says \"ensuring absolute regulatory compliance\" — this is a strong claim. In practice, whether the logs actually satisfy Article 12 depends on what events are deemed \"relevant for\" each specific system's intended purpose (Art 12(2)). A one-size-fits-all schema might over-log some systems and under-log others.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a solid engineering pattern that aligns well with my domain concerns. The core idea — decouple logging from model logic via middleware, use standardized schemas — is technically viable and would genuinely speed up prototyping by removing per-project compliance plumbing. The main risks are (1) over-generalization (\"universal API\" for very different system types) and (2) the scope of \"events to log\" being determined too early or too rigidly in a fixed schema.\n\nBut these are manageable design challenges, not fundamental viability issues. The pattern is proven (AOP, decorators, interceptors). The ROI for prototyping speed is clear: every new high-risk prototype skips