rage) to a regulatory context.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:** The broader impact is compliance and operational efficiency within the lab. It doesn't contribute to open science, doesn't advance the field of AI, doesn't create new knowledge that other researchers could build upon in a scientific sense. Its \"impact\" is regulatory compliance for one institution. One could argue it indirectly frees researcher time (less manual documentation), but this is an operational benefit, not a scientific impact.\n\n**My assessment:** From my vantage point as a Researcher concerned with the knowledge frontier and intellectual merit, this proposal is fundamentally an engineering/compliance project disguised in research language. It does not advance AI science. It does not produce novel insights into how models work, learn, or reason. The \"research\" framing (\"reducing administrative burden on researchers pursuing scientific breakthroughs\") is a nice justification but the actual work described is building logging infrastructure. This would be better handled by an engineering/IT/compliance team rather than consuming research resources.\n\nHowever, I should consider: does it have ANY merit from my vantage point? \n- Standardized API wrappers could have minor reproducibility benefits (knowing exactly what hyperparameters were used), but this is well-solved in the field (MLflow, Weights & Biases, etc.)\n- Tamper-evident logging for research integrity has some appeal but isn't a research frontier question\n- The \"automatic\" aspect reduces researcher time on documentation, which could theoretically free more time for actual research — but this is a second-order effect and the proposal doesn't address whether existing tools (MLflow, DVC, etc.) would suffice\n\nI think this should be rejected from my vantage point. It's not a research project; it's an infrastructure/compliance project. The promise score should reflect that from my perspective (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit