\"Without requiring manual calls from the researcher\" - this directly addresses prototyping friction. Researchers can focus on their actual work rather than compliance plumbing.\n- Standardization across projects means: faster onboarding for new team members, fewer bugs (consistent implementation), easier cross-project comparison.\n- However, a risk: if the middleware is too rigid or adds latency to inference pipelines, it could slow prototyping. But \"lightweight wrapper\" suggests they've considered this.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage:**\n- Scope creep risk: trying to serve ALL high-risk AI projects with ONE library might lead to either over-engineering (trying to handle every edge case) or under-engineering (not covering specific domain needs). But the proposal says \"lightweight wrapper\" so it seems scoped appropriately.\n- Maintenance burden: who maintains this middleware? If it becomes a bottleneck, prototyping speed suffers. But this is an organizational concern more than a technical viability one.\n- The proposal mentions \"start/end times and input metadata by default\" - this covers Art 12(3)(a) and partially (c). For the reference database (b) and person identification (d), those would need additional hooks, but that's project-specific configuration rather than middleware redesign.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom a development engineering perspective focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality, this is a strong proposal. The pattern is well-understood, technically straightforward to implement, and the primary benefit (removing manual logging burden) directly accelerates prototyping while ensuring compliance. It's a \"boring\" solution in the best sense - not flashy but reliably effective. The technical risk is low. The speed-of-prototyping benefit is high because it eliminates repetitive compliance work from every project.\n\nThe main reservation: \"designed to be integrated into all high-risk AI projects\" could be ambitious