e our compliance cheaper or faster? Probably not significantly — we'd still need to implement logging for OUR systems regardless of what industry standards look like.\n- \"Publishing detailed white papers\" is a communications/marketing activity, not an R&D infrastructure activity. It doesn't leverage existing R&D infrastructure; it creates new overhead.\n- The scope is very broad: \"various high-risk AI domains.\" This means we'd be categorizing events for biometric systems, critical infrastructure, employment, education, etc. — far beyond what our lab actually builds. This is a massive scope creep that doesn't align with effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n- The primary value proposition (\"positions our lab as a global leader\") is reputational/strategic, not operational. It doesn't directly reduce the cost or complexity of OUR compliance. A consortium effort is where consensus-building, politics, and coordination eat up resources without producing engineering deliverables for us.\n- From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint: we need to implement Article 12 logging for our own systems. That's bounded and necessary. Leading an industry-wide standards body is unbounded, politically complex, and the ROI for our actual compliance is unclear at best.\n\n**My assessment:** This proposal sounds prestigious but is fundamentally misaligned with efficient use of scarce R&D resources. It asks us to take on consortium leadership (coordination overhead, multi-stakeholder negotiation), produce white papers (communications work, not engineering), and categorize events across \"various high-risk AI domains\" (scope far beyond our actual systems). The value is primarily reputational. While there's a small kernel of truth that sharing templates could help peers (and indirectly create a cleaner ecosystem we operate in), the cost-benefit from my resource-allocation perspective is poor. We should implement Article 12 for our own systems efficiently and perhaps contr