e fields is unlikely to cover all cases.\n  3. Two engineers building this - is that enough? It depends on scope. If it's truly a thin wrapper, maybe. But if you need to handle different system types, different logging requirements per risk category, retention policies, integrity guarantees (tamper-proofing), etc., two engineers might be stretched.\n  4. The \"automatic\" aspect is key - the regulation says systems shall \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" A library that requires developers to call specific API points is semi-automatic but still requires integration effort per project. True automation would require deeper integration or instrumentation.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n- Pros: If it works, researchers don't rebuild logging from scratch each time. This speeds up prototyping for future projects. Standardized APIs reduce cognitive load on researchers who aren't compliance experts.\n- Cons: \n  - Building this library itself is a non-trivial project that takes engineering resources away from actual AI R&D work. Two engineers dedicated to this is an opportunity cost.\n  - The \"wrapper around existing telemetry\" approach risks being brittle - if the underlying tools change, the wrapper breaks. Also, different projects may use different telemetry stacks, making a single wrapper hard to maintain.\n  - The requirement scope in Article 12 is broad (events relevant for risk identification, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring). A fixed API might not capture what's \"relevant\" for every intended purpose. You'd need configurability that adds complexity.\n  - For prototyping speed specifically: researchers building prototypes often iterate quickly and may not want to integrate a compliance library during early stages. They might bolt it on later, at which point the \"automatic\" benefit is reduced because you're retrofitting.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThe general direction (shared library, standardization) is 