ulation already tells us WHAT to log for Annex III 1(a) systems specifically, and gives functional requirements for other high-risk systems. We don't need a research project to tell us that; we need engineering implementation.\n- Even for non-Annex III 1(a) high-risk systems, the functional requirements in Article 12(2)(a)-(c) are sufficiently specific to guide engineering design. You can prototype logging architectures against those requirements without first conducting \"empirical studies on optimal traceability metrics.\"\n- The framing of \"shifting from passive compliance to leading the scientific discourse\" is a marketing/PR narrative, not an engineering deliverable. From my seat, I care about: can we build the logging pipeline that satisfies Article 12? Does it integrate cleanly with our model serving infrastructure? What's the latency overhead? How do we handle retention, access control, and format standards? This proposal doesn't address any of those.\n- \"Detecting edge-case anomalies\" is a much broader problem than what Article 12 requires. Article 12 is about record-keeping for traceability, not about building an anomaly detection system. Conflating these two would scope-creep the project and dilute focus.\n\n**What it gets right (marginally):**\n- There IS a legitimate engineering question of \"what granularity of logging strikes the right balance between observability utility and performance/storage cost.\" But that's an implementation detail I can resolve during prototyping, not something requiring a \"dedicated research project with empirical studies.\"\n- Understanding which log events correlate with failures is useful for tuning alerting, but again, that's operational engineering, not fundamental research.\n\n**My assessment:** This proposal is fundamentally misaligned with what Article 12 requires of us as implementers. It substitutes a long-term, open-ended research program for the concrete engineering task of building compliant logging infrastructure. From my vantage point on technical viability and prototyping speed, this doesn't give me a buildable specification, it introduces uncertainty into an area where the regulation already provides sufficient guidance, and it commits resources to an activity with unclear deliverables and timeline. It's not technically non-viable — you CAN do empirical studies of logging granularity — but it's not viable as a path to meeting Article 12 in any reasonable timeframe, and it doesn't accelerate or improve our prototyping capability for compliance.\n\nI'll reject this. Promise score: maybe 8-12? The underlying question (what log granularity is appropriate) is real but solvable during normal engineering work without a \"dedicated research project.\" It's not harmful, just misdirected and too slow/uncertain for my function's needs. I'd say around 10.\n",
