        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2644 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain concerns are: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is \"Semantic Event Logging for Risk Detection\" — an advanced logging layer that records high-level semantic markers of model uncertainty and failure modes rather than simple system events, focused on Article 12(2)(a).\n\nLet me assess this carefully:\n\n**What the regulation actually requires (Article 12):**\n- Art. 12(1): High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\n- Art. 12(2): Logging capabilities shall enable recording of events relevant for: (a) identifying situations that may result in presenting a risk or substantial modification; (b) facilitating post-market monitoring; (c) monitoring operation per Art. 26(5).\n- Art. 12(3): For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically, minimum logging includes start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, and identification of natural persons in verification.\n\n**Assessment from Compliance Officer's vantage point:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses Article 12(2)(a) — identifying situations that may result in the system presenting a risk. This is squarely within the regulatory requirement.\n- The focus on \"situations that may result in a high-risk AI system presenting a risk\" aligns with what Art. 79(1) covers (serious incidents, malfunctions).\n- Implementing as a shared library across all high-risk projects is operationally sensible for compliance — it creates a uniform standard that auditors can verify consistently.\n- Recording model uncertainty and failure modes is a more sophisticated approach than simple event logging, which could demonstrate to regulators that the institution goes beyond minimum compliance (favorable for market reputation)