eristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" Also mentions technical documentation kept up to date throughout lifetime.\n- The proposal's capture of hyperparameters, dataset versions, execution logs aligns well with training/testing/validation process documentation.\n\n**My professional assessment from a Compliance Officer's vantage point:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly supports Article 12(1) — automatic recording of events/logs. This is a \"shall\" requirement, so having automation built into the infrastructure reduces compliance risk.\n- Supports Recital 71's emphasis on traceability and documentation of training/testing/validation processes.\n- Automating this removes reliance on individual researchers to manually maintain records, which is a significant compliance gap in practice. Manual record-keeping is where institutions fail audits.\n- \"Treats traceability as a first-class citizen\" — good institutional culture signal for regulators.\n- Improves reproducibility, which supports the technical documentation requirements under Article 11 (which feeds into Article 12's traceability purpose).\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal is framed around R&D experiments and training scripts. Article 12 primarily concerns the HIGH-RISK AI SYSTEM in operation (deployment lifetime), not just the development phase. There's a gap between \"capturing metadata during training\" and \"the system technically allowing automatic recording of events over its operational lifetime.\"\n- It doesn't explicitly address the specific logging requirements in Art. 12(3) for Annex III systems (period of use, reference database, input data matches, verification persons).\n- The proposal is a development-phase tool; it needs to be complemented by deployment-phase logging infrastructure.\n- However, as a foundation, it's directionally correct and addresses 