ndational infrastructure - once built, it serves all projects. The question is whether it should be built as a dedicated project or evolved incrementally.\n- WORM architecture adds complexity to the prototyping workflow. During rapid iteration/prototyping, you might want to discard logs or iterate on documentation quickly. A strict WORM system could slow down experimentation cycles. You'd need a \"sandbox\" mode for development vs. \"production\" mode for deployment.\n- CI/CD integration is actually good - it means logging archiving becomes automatic rather than manual, reducing friction in the prototyping cycle.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The proposal focuses on storage and integrity but doesn't address the harder technical challenge: what events to log (Article 12(2) requirements about identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, etc.). That's an application-layer problem requiring per-system instrumentation.\n2. \"Centralized\" could become a single point of failure or bottleneck in a large R&D lab with multiple teams prototyping different systems.\n3. WORM is good for compliance but potentially at odds with the iterative nature of R&D prototyping. During development, you want to experiment freely; locking down records early adds friction.\n4. The proposal doesn't specify retention periods, access patterns, or how it handles the \"up to date\" requirement from Recital 71 (documentation needs updates over lifetime).\n5. From a pure prototyping speed perspective, this is infrastructure that serves compliance rather than directly enabling AI development. It's necessary but not sufficient - and building it as a monolithic centralized system might be over-engineering for early-stage prototypes.\n\n**Positive aspects:**\n- Technically sound and mature approach\n- WORM genuinely addresses the \"lifetime\" retention requirement\n- CI/CD integration means it becomes part of normal workflow, reducing overhead\n- Prevents tampering which