        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2147\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises the core mandate of Article 12(1) by making automatic event-logging a structural, default feature across all high-risk deployments rather than a per-project afterthought, which eliminates the single largest failure mode I see in multi-team R&D labs (a researcher ships without logging). The stated telemetry categories—timestamps, input references, user interactions—map cleanly onto the Article 12(3) minimum fields for Annex III 1(a) systems and onto the traceability/post-market-monitoring purposes in 12(2), giving regulators a verifiable, uniform audit trail that also supports our market-reputation narrative to enterprise clients. My residual concern is that 'guaranteeing regulatory compliance' overstates what software alone can do; retention schedules, access controls for logged personal data (GDPR interplay), and the requirement that logs remain available over the full system lifetime (Recital 71) will need explicit specification in the implementation phase, but those are design-level refinements, not a reason to reject the architecture.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2147 from my vantage point as Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal: \"Unified High-Risk Observability Framework\" — a modular software layer integrated into all high-risk models to automatically capture event logs per Article 12, with standardized API hooks and telemetry schemas recording timestamps, input references, and user interactions across different model architectures.\n\nLet me assess this from a compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses Article 12(1): \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A unified framework ensures this is baked in by default rather than being an afterthought per-project.\n- Addresses Article 12(2)(a)-(c): logging capabilities for identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operation. Standardized telemetry schemas can be designed to capture these specific event types.\n- Addresses Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification): the proposal mentions \"timestamps, input references, and user interactions\" which maps reasonably well to (a) start/end times, (b) reference database, (c) input data matches, (d) identification of natural persons in verification.\n- \"By default\" compliance is a strong compliance posture — it reduces the risk that individual researchers or teams forget or fail to implement logging. This protects institutional survival.\n- Standardized schemas across architectures reduce the chance of gaps in log coverage, which is critical for demonstrating traceability (Recital 71).\n- Reduces manual burden on researchers — this means higher adoption rates and less likelihood of workarounds that create compliance gaps.\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- \"All high-risk models\" — the scope needs to be precise. Not all models are high-risk; the framework should target only those classified as high-risk under Article 6 or Annex III. However, building for a broader set isn't necessarily wrong, just potentially resource-heavy.\n- The proposal is somewhat abstract — it says \"standardized API hooks and telemetry schemas\" but doesn't specify the retention period (which must cover the lifetime of the system per Recital 71), data storage security requirements, or access controls for logs. However, as a high-level framework proposal, these details would presumably be worked out in implementation.\n- The phrase \"guaranteeing regulatory compliance\" is slightly overreaching — no software layer alone guarantees full Article 12 compliance (you also need the technical documentation per Recital 71, risk management docs, etc.). But as a component of compliance, it's essential.\n- From a market reputation standpoint, having a unified, well-documented observability framework is actually a selling point — it demonstrates to regulators and enterprise clients that the lab takes traceability seriously.\n\n**Compliance-specific assessment:**\nThis proposal directly operationalizes the core mandate of Article 12(1) — automatic logging over lifetime. The modular design ensures scalability as new high-risk systems are developed. The \"by default\" aspect is critical for compliance: if each team has to remember to implement logging, you get gaps. A unified framework eliminates that failure mode.\n\nThe mapping to specific sub-requirements (timestamps → 12(3)(a), input references → 12(3)(b)-(c), user interactions → 12(3)(d)) shows the proposal has been thought about in relation to the actual regulatory text.\n\nFrom my vantage point, this is a strong, well-scoped compliance enabler. It reduces institutional risk by making compliance structural rather than procedural (dependent on individual diligence). It protects market reputation by ensuring no high-risk system ships without proper logging. The \"modular\" and \"across different model architectures\" language suggests it's designed to be maintainable as the lab evolves.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-85. It's not a perfect proposal (it doesn't address retention policies, data protection implications of logging, or the interaction with GDPR for logged personal data), but as a core compliance infrastructure investment, it's clearly necessary and well-aligned with Article 12. I'll give it an 84 — very strong, but there are implementation details around data governance and retention that would need to be nailed down before full confidence.\n",
