ting and validation processes\" plus documentation on risk-management systems. The proposal captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, and validation results - that's a good subset but not necessarily the complete picture. Still, it's a meaningful chunk.\n- The cost seems proportional to the benefit: you're automating something that would otherwise consume ongoing engineering hours indefinitely.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Explicitly builds on \"existing experiment tracking tools\" - this is exactly the kind of leverage I'd want.\n- The custom generator is an addition, but a relatively thin one if it's just mapping existing metadata to a structured format.\n- This doesn't require new hardware or major infrastructure investment.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Custom generator\" could balloon in scope if not tightly constrained. Who maintains it? What happens when experiment tracking tools evolve?\n- The proposal mentions Recital 71 specifically, which is good scoping, but I need to be aware that Article 12's logging requirements (automatic recording of events over lifetime) are somewhat separate from the documentation pipeline described here. This proposal addresses the technical documentation side more than the runtime logging side. That said, it's still a valid and useful component.\n- The claim \"removes the manual burden\" might be slightly overstated - there will likely still be human review needed for accuracy and completeness of compliance documents. But even 70% reduction in manual work is valuable.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my resource-allocation perspective, this is a strong proposal. It:\n1. Leverages existing infrastructure (experiment tracking)\n2. Has bounded scope (mapping to Recital 71 requirements)\n3. Frees up scarce engineering resources for actual R&D\n4. Produces ongoing value (compliance documentation is continuous, not one-time)\n5. Doesn't require major new funding - it's an integration project\n\nI would approve this. It's efficient, well-scoped, and directly addresses a compliance requirement while freeing up human capital. The promise score should be high but not 100 because there are execution risks (scope creep on the \"custom generator,\" maintenance burden, the fact that it doesn't fully cover all of Article 12/Recital 71).\n\nI'll give it around 82-85. It's a clear win from my vantage point with some residual risk in execution.\n",
