ojects**: One shared API means consistent compliance, fewer edge cases, easier maintenance over time. As the AI system lifecycle continues (Article 12 requires logs \"over the lifetime of the system\"), having a unified layer is more maintainable than scattered custom implementations.\n\nConcerns/Potential issues:\n1. **Upfront investment**: Building this module requires engineering resources upfront. However, if we have multiple high-risk AI projects (which seems likely for an R&D institution), the amortization makes sense quickly. The cost of NOT having this is every team building their own logging, which compounds over time.\n2. **Scope creep risk**: \"All high-risk AI projects\" — need to make sure this doesn't balloon into trying to log everything about everything. But the proposal seems appropriately scoped to Article 12 requirements (timestamps, data references, session durations).\n3. **Integration with existing pipelines**: The fact that it's designed to integrate INTO our development pipeline rather than being bolted on separately is good for adoption and doesn't disrupt ongoing research workflows.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a highly aligned proposal with my domain concerns. It directly addresses resource efficiency (centralization, no duplicate work), leverages existing infrastructure (compute clusters), protects scientific ambition (researchers don't write boilerplate logging code), and has clear scope tied to a specific regulatory requirement. The ROI is straightforward — one build, many projects benefit, reduced long-term maintenance burden compared to N custom implementations.\n\nI would approve this with high confidence. It's exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that makes sense when you have multiple regulated systems in development. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-88. It's not a perfect 100 because there's execution risk (building it well, getting adoption across teams, maintaining it over time), but from my vantage point t