es regulatory risk compared to ad-hoc per-project implementations. It also protects market reputation by demonstrating that the institution takes compliance seriously at a structural level. The \"mandatory\" aspect is crucial - as Compliance Officer, I need assurance that no team can opt out.\n\nThe main gap I see is that it doesn't explicitly address 12(2)(a) (risk identification events) and the comprehensibility requirement from Recital 71. But as a framework/library, these would be implementable details within it. The core architecture described is sound and directly responsive to the regulation.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score because it's fundamentally aligned with what Article 12 requires and addresses systemic compliance risk effectively.\n",
