ty of prototyping:**\n- This fits naturally into existing CI/CD workflows. No new infrastructure needed beyond standard test harnesses.\n- It accelerates development by catching gaps early (shift-left), which is exactly what speeds up prototyping - you don't spend weeks fixing logging issues discovered late in the cycle.\n- The \"compliance unit test\" framing is good because it decomposes a large regulatory requirement into small, testable units.\n- However, building this suite has non-trivial upfront cost: you need to enumerate all required log fields per system type, create fixtures for each scenario, and maintain the tests as systems evolve. This is ongoing maintenance work.\n- For prototyping speed specifically: if the logging infrastructure isn't built yet, you can prototype the test suite in parallel with the logging implementation (TDD approach), which actually accelerates delivery because it forces clarity on what \"done\" means for logging.\n\n**Quality:**\n- Catches gaps early = higher quality deliverables\n- Automated regression testing ensures that refactors don't break compliance-relevant logging\n- The stress-test angle (simulating failure modes) adds depth beyond simple field-presence checks\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Simulates failure modes\" could balloon scope if not carefully bounded. Simulating every possible Article 79(1) risk scenario is a huge undertaking. But the proposal says \"designed to verify that system logs actually capture all events required\" - so it's testing log completeness, not simulating every possible real-world risk event. That's more tractable.\n- The CI/CD integration means these tests run frequently. If they're slow (especially stress tests), they could slow down the development cycle. Need to ensure fast execution or separate into unit-level (fast) and integration-level (slower, less frequent) tiers.\n- Maintenance burden: as systems evolve, the test suite must evolve too. This is a real cost but manageable if well-architected.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a technically sound, well-scoped engineering proposal that directly addresses Article 12 compliance through standard software engineering practices. It's implementable with existing tools and patterns. It improves prototyping speed by shifting compliance verification left in the development cycle. The main risk is scope creep on \"simulating failure modes\" but the core idea of log-field validation via automated tests is straightforward.\n\nI would approve this. Promise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's solid, well-motivated, technically feasible, and aligns with how we should build things anyway (test-driven compliance). The slight deduction is because the \"stress test\" and \"simulates failure modes\" language could lead to over-engineering or scope issues if not carefully bounded, and the maintenance burden in a fast-moving R&D environment is non-trivial. But it's clearly worth doing.\n\nLet me settle on 80.\n",
