nst a schema.\n- It prevents technical debt accumulation, which is a real cost multiplier in R&D environments where teams move on to new projects.\n\nCons/Concerns:\n- Building the test suite itself requires initial investment of engineering time. For a lab with many different prototype types, the test scripts need to be adaptable. However, this is a one-time (or incremental) cost vs. recurring rework costs.\n- The \"specific event scenarios\" part could get complex if the lab develops very diverse AI systems. But for high-risk systems specifically subject to Article 12, the scope is bounded.\n- There's a risk of false confidence — automated checks verify format/structure but may not catch semantic issues in logging (e.g., wrong database reference that still looks structurally correct). However, this is a limitation of any automation and doesn't negate the value.\n\nFrom my resource-allocation efficiency perspective:\n- This is a high-leverage investment: relatively modest upfront engineering cost, recurring savings on every prototype that would otherwise need late-stage rework.\n- It uses existing infrastructure (CI/CD) rather than requiring new tools or teams.\n- It directly addresses the \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" concern.\n- The ROI is clear and quantifiable: fewer rework cycles, less technical debt, faster time-to-compliance for each system.\n\nThis seems like a strong proposal from my vantage point. It's not about scientific ambition per se — it's an operational efficiency tool that happens to be driven by regulatory requirements. But the resource savings are real and substantial.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-85. It's clearly valuable, but I'm not giving it a 100 because:\n- Building comprehensive test scenarios for all Article 12 event types across diverse AI systems is non-trivial work.\n- There's maintenance burden as the regulation or interpretation evolves.\n- It addresses one article of compliance (Article 12) and doesn't help with other requirements, though that's not its stated scope.\n- The initial build cost needs to be weighed against current project timelines — if the lab is already stretched thin on engineering resources, this competes with active R&D work.\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 83.\n",
