        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P2945) from my vantage point as Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n**P2945: Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline**\n\nThe proposal describes a living documentation system that:\n- Synchronizes technical specifications and training data logs with version control systems (Git)\n- Transforms static reports into real-time audit trails\n- Deploys automated tooling to generate compliance reports from Git history and experiment tracking platforms like MLflow\n- Maps R&D changes to specific requirements in Recital 71\n- Maintains up-to-date records for regulatory audits without slowing down research pace\n\nNow let me assess this from my vantage point (resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure):\n\n**Strengths from my perspective:**\n1. **Leverages existing infrastructure**: It explicitly builds on tools the lab already uses — Git for version control, MLflow for experiment tracking. This is a major resource-efficiency win. We don't need to procure new expensive platforms; we're automating the mapping between what we already generate and what the Act requires.\n2. **Reduces recurring compliance overhead**: Instead of periodically producing static documentation (which consumes scientist time and requires dedicated compliance staff), this creates an automated pipeline. Over the lifetime of a high-risk AI system, Recital 71 explicitly says \"The technical documentation should be kept up to date.\" Manual upkeep is expensive; automation amortizes the initial build cost across many future audit events.\n3. **Preserves research throughput**: The proposal explicitly frames itself as not slowing down scientists. From my resource-allocation view, compliance work that pulls researchers away from their core R&D is a tax on our output. Automating the mapping reduces this tax.\n4. **Directly addresses Article 12(1) and Recital 71**: The \"automatic recording of events\" and \"kept up to date throughout the lifetime\" requirements are exactly what an automated pipeline tied to version control would handle.\n\n**Weaknesses / concerns from my perspective:**\n1. **Initial build cost is non-trivial**: Setting up automated tooling that maps Git history and MLflow metadata to specific regulatory requirements in Recital 71 is not a weekend project. It requires domain expertise (understanding the Act's granular requirements) AND engineering skill (building the pipeline, maintaining it across framework updates). This is an upfront investment.\n2. **Maintenance burden**: As the lab evolves its ML stack, adds new frameworks, or changes experiment tracking practices, the pipeline needs updating. Who owns that? If it falls to a small compliance team, it could become a bottleneck. If it falls to research engineers, it's a recurring tax.\n3. **Scope creep risk**: \"Map R&D changes to the specific requirements listed in Recital 71\" — Recital 71 is broad (general characteristics, capabilities, limitations, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes, risk-management system). Building complete automated mapping for ALL of that from Git + MLflow alone may not be sufficient; some compliance artifacts (e.g., risk-management documentation) don't naturally live in version control. The proposal might overpromise what can be generated automatically.\n4. **Doesn't fully address Article 12(3)**: For Annex III point 1(a) systems, specific logging requirements (period of use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification) are runtime operational logs, not R&D documentation. The proposal seems focused on the development/documentation side (Recital 71) rather than the operational logging side (Article 12(3)). This is a gap, though it's not necessarily this proposal's job to fill everything.\n5. **Funding constraint**: In a constrained budget environment, committing engineering resources to build this pipeline competes with other R&D priorities. However, the ROI argument is strong if we have multiple high-risk systems in development — the pipeline amortizes across all of them.\n\n**Net assessment:**\nThis is a well-conceived proposal from a resource-efficiency standpoint. It leverages existing tools (Git, MLflow) rather than requiring new procurement. The upfront cost is real but bounded, and the recurring savings (not needing to manually regenerate compliance documentation for every audit, not pulling scientists off research tasks periodically) compound over time. The main risk is scope creep and the maintenance question, but those are manageable with proper ownership assignment.\n\nThe proposal directly serves my domain concerns:\n- Resource-allocation efficiency: ✓ (automation reduces recurring manual effort)\n- Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints: ✓ (uses existing infra, amortizes cost)\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure: ✓✓ (explicitly builds on Git + MLflow)\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-85. It's not a perfect solution (doesn't cover operational logging fully, has maintenance risk), but from my vantage point it's exactly the kind of proposal that makes regulatory compliance a byproduct of normal R&D workflow rather than a separate expensive exercise.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 84.\n",
