ts are clearly defined in 12(3)(a)-(d), making prototyping targets concrete.\n- \"Immutable audit log\" - while technically simple to implement (append-only with cryptographic hashing), there's a subtle question about what \"immutable\" means operationally. Do we need tamper-evidence for regulators? That adds some complexity but is still well-trodden territory.\n- Speed of prototyping: This is a bounded, well-understood component. You could prototype this in days to a couple weeks. It's not an ML research problem - it's data engineering with clear requirements. The decoupling from the primary model means you can iterate on the AI system without waiting for logging infrastructure changes.\n- Quality: The separation of concerns (dedicated data layer vs. embedded logging) is architecturally clean and will produce higher-quality code because the team can focus purely on compliance requirements rather than retrofitting logging into model code.\n\nOverall, from a technical viability and prototyping speed perspective, this is a very strong proposal. It's well-scoped, technically straightforward, uses established patterns, clearly maps to specific regulatory text (12(3)), and the architectural decision to decouple it from the primary AI models preserves iteration velocity. The main risk is scope creep (what about non-Annex III high-risk systems under Article 12(1) and 12(2)?), but as stated, this proposal explicitly targets Annex III, which is fine - you can address other categories separately.\n\nI'd rate this highly. Maybe 85-90. It's not a 100 because:\n- The \"immutable\" requirement needs more specification (what level of tamper-evidence? Retention period? Who has write access during development/testing?)\n- There's an implicit assumption that the reference database versioning is already solved or will be simple - for large ML datasets, pinning exact versions can have significant storage implications.\n- \"Without compromising performance\" is a claim that needs valida