ameters are not the same as operational event logs (when was the system invoked, what inputs were processed, what outputs were generated, who verified results). This proposal conflates development documentation with operational logging.\n\nAs a compliance officer: I see value in this for the technical documentation side but it doesn't adequately address Article 12's core requirement of automatic event logging during operation. The proposal is misaligned with the specific article being addressed. It's more about Recital 71's \"technical documentation\" than Article 12's \"automatic recording of events (logs).\"\n\nI'll lean toward reject or a low promise score. Actually, let me think again...\n\nThe proposal does say it uses \"training logs\" which are a form of log. And \"version control metadata\" provides temporal traceability. But the focus is clearly on development-phase documentation generation rather than operational lifetime logging.\n\nDecision: I'll reject this. While it addresses some adjacent documentation needs, it doesn't meaningfully implement Article 12's requirement for automatic event recording over the system's full lifetime. It's a documentation generator for the prototyping phase, not a compliance logging architecture. The specific gaps (no operational logs, no post-market monitoring support, no Annex III 1(a) fields, no substantial modification detection triggers) are too significant from a regulatory survival standpoint.\n\nPromise score: Maybe 35-40. It's not worthless - the MLflow integration concept is reusable and the automation principle is sound. But as an Article 12 compliance measure, it's insufficiently scoped.\n",
