 — that's a red flag from my perspective. I need prototyping speed and quality. A research project into novel compression techniques for neural activations is speculative and slow. The engineering problem actually needed (async event logging with timestamps, database references, input data capture) is well-understood and can be prototyped quickly with existing patterns.\n- However, if the system genuinely needs to log decision paths (e.g., which features were salient in a biometric match), there IS a technical challenge in doing this efficiently at inference time without adding latency that breaks real-time requirements.\n- The \"publication-worthy contribution\" framing is a distraction from the engineering goal. I care about shipping working prototypes, not papers.\n\n**Key concerns:**\n1. Over-engineering risk: The proposal solves a harder problem than what Article 12 actually requires. You can meet the regulation with structured event logging (timestamps, input references, verification IDs) without needing to compress and asynchronously log full activation tensors.\n2. Research vs. engineering: \"A research project\" signals uncertainty about technical viability. For prototyping speed, I want known patterns applied to a new domain, not open research questions.\n3. The real engineering challenge (meeting Art 12(3) logging requirements in a biometric system with acceptable latency) is solvable with standard async queue + structured serialization + periodic flush patterns. You don't need novel compression of neural activations for this.\n4. That said, if the lab's systems DO need to log decision paths for explainability (which supports Art 12(2)(a) - identifying risk situations), then efficient representation of \"why did the model decide X\" is a legitimate engineering problem. But that's different from logging raw high-dimensional activations.\n\n**Verdict:**\nThe proposal addresses a real tension (comprehensive recording vs. performance), but it significantly over-scopes the regulatory requirement and frames it as an open research question when the core engineering solution (async structured event logging) is well-established. From my prototyping-speed vantage, this would slow us down: we'd be in a research loop rather than shipping a working log pipeline that meets Article 12. The compression of neural activations is not required by the regulation — what's required is recording specific events (start/end times, database reference, input data for matches, verifier IDs).\n\nI'll reject this but give it moderate promise because the underlying tension is real and some elements (async logging in inference pipelines) are useful engineering even if the full research framing is not.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 30-35. The problem space has some validity but the approach (research project, novel compression of activations) misaligns with what's actually needed for regulatory compliance and would slow prototyping.\n",
