idelines evolve or Annex III categories shift).\n- The proposal says \"guaranteeing essential event logs are captured consistently\" — but the quality of what gets logged matters. A decorator that just wraps function calls may capture timing events (start/end) but might not naturally capture all the Article 12(3) specific requirements like \"the reference database against which input data has been checked\" or \"input data for which the search has led to a match.\" Those are domain-specific and may need more than a generic decorator.\n- Over-abstraction risk: if the SDK is too rigid, it might constrain experimental flexibility. If too flexible, compliance gaps emerge.\n- Maintenance burden as regulations evolve (implementing acts, harmonized standards).\n\n**Net assessment from my vantage:**\nThis is a strong proposal for resource efficiency. The one-time investment in a well-designed compliance SDK pays dividends across every high-risk project the lab undertakes. It directly serves \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" because it layers onto existing Python codebases without requiring new hardware or platforms. It protects scientific ambition by making compliance nearly invisible to researchers.\n\nThe main risk is that the scope might be underestimated — a generic decorator may not cover all Article 12 requirements, especially the specific ones in 12(3) for biometric systems. But even as a partial solution covering logging of use periods (12(3)(a)) and general event capture (Article 12(1)-(2)), it's valuable infrastructure.\n\nI'd rate this highly from my perspective. It's the kind of \"build once, use many times\" investment that maximizes ROI on compliance spending. I'll approve with a high promise score. Maybe 82-85? The slight deduction is because I need to ensure the SDK scope actually covers all Article 12 requirements and isn't just a thin wrapper that gives false comfort. Also, the maintenance cost as regulations evolve needs budgeting.\n\nLet m