ite papers, regulatory engagement, collaboration) without directly producing compliant systems.\n- \"Lead the consensus building process\" is ambitious but high-risk from a resource perspective. Consensus processes are slow, politically complex, and may never yield concrete results that benefit us specifically.\n- The output (white papers, proposals to regulators) doesn't directly translate into our AI systems being more efficient or compliant. It's upstream influence work.\n- From a resource-allocation standpoint, we could spend those same resources directly implementing Article 12 compliance in our systems, which is what actually gets us to market and meets the regulatory deadline.\n- \"Scientific AI\" as a category may not even be clearly mapped to Annex III high-risk categories. If our systems aren't clearly \"high-risk\" under the Act, this whole exercise may be misdirected.\n- The proposal doesn't specify how this connects to our existing R&D infrastructure or what concrete deliverables we'd produce within our funding cycle.\n- The framing of \"leading consensus building\" is a significant resource commitment with uncertain ROI. We don't know if other stakeholders will buy in, if regulators will adopt our proposals, or if the timeline aligns with when we need compliance.\n\nFrom my specific vantage point (resource efficiency, funding constraints, infrastructure use):\n- This is an expensive, open-ended project with diffuse outputs. The resources required for white papers, regulatory engagement, and \"consensus building\" are substantial but don't directly produce a compliant system.\n- There's no clear link to how this uses or improves existing R&D infrastructure. It's a new activity that doesn't leverage what we already have.\n- The ROI is speculative: maybe shaping standards saves us money later, but the cost now is certain and the benefit is uncertain and delayed.\n- However, there IS a legitimate concern: if Article 12's requirements are vague for our class of systems, implementing without clarity could lead to over-engineering (wasting resources) or under-engineering (non-compliance risk). A focused effort to clarify requirements for OUR specific system class might be valuable — but that's different from \"leading consensus building\" and authoring white papers.\n\nMy assessment: The proposal as stated is too broad, too advocacy-focused, and too resource-intensive relative to its direct impact on our lab's compliance and operational efficiency. It's a strategic/lobbying play rather than an implementation or infrastructure improvement. From my vantage point of making the best use of scarce R&D resources and existing infrastructure, I would lean toward rejecting it in this form. The core need (understanding what logging is appropriate for our systems) could be met more efficiently through internal analysis and targeted regulatory Q&A rather than a full consensus-building campaign with white papers.\n\nHowever, there's a kernel of value: ensuring we understand the scope of Article 12 obligations for our specific AI system class before investing heavily in implementation. But that's a much smaller, more focused task than what's proposed here.\n\nDecision: Reject. The proposal is too diffuse, advocacy-heavy, and resource-intensive relative to its direct contribution to our compliance and operational efficiency. It doesn't leverage existing infrastructure and the ROI is speculative.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 25-30. There's a legitimate strategic interest in understanding regulatory expectations, but the form (leading consensus building, white papers) is poorly matched to my resource-efficiency mandate.\n",
