input data matches for Annex III point 1(a), etc.) and (b) research provenance/scientific documentation. The compliance requirements are specific and bounded; \"capturing intellectual merit\" is unbounded.\n3. However, integrating version control into orchestration is a reasonable infrastructure investment that serves both scientific rigor AND regulatory compliance - dual use of resources.\n4. The scope seems manageable: it's essentially \"add structured logging to our existing training pipeline.\" This is not building a new system from scratch; it's augmenting what we already have.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\n- The core idea (integrate version control and structured logging into the orchestration layer) is a reasonable, bounded infrastructure investment\n- It leverages existing R&D infrastructure (the orchestration layer) rather than requiring new parallel systems\n- The \"intellectual merit\" language is problematic - it suggests scope beyond what's needed for compliance and could lead to over-engineering\n- However, the fundamental architecture described (version control + timestamped logs in orchestration) is efficient use of existing infrastructure\n- It addresses ongoing labor costs by automating documentation - good ROI\n\n**Decision:** The core proposal is sound from an efficiency standpoint. Integrating logging into the existing orchestration layer is a natural, low-friction addition that serves dual purposes (compliance + research reproducibility). The \"intellectual merit\" language is a red flag for scope creep but doesn't fundamentally change the architecture being proposed. I think this clears my bar as a reasonable, efficient use of resources. It's not revolutionary but it's practical and addresses a real need with minimal additional infrastructure cost.\n\nI'll approve it but give it a moderate-to-high promise score because while it's solid, the \"intellectual merit\" framing introduces some ambiguity about scope, and I'd want to see tighter scoping before full implementation commitment. But the core architecture is sound and efficient.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 72-78. It's practical, leverages existing infrastructure, addresses a real need efficiently. The main deduction is for the vague \"intellectual merit\" framing that could lead to scope creep, and the fact that it doesn't specifically address Article 12(3)'s specific logging requirements (start/end times, reference database, input data matches) which are more prescriptive than what's described.\n",
