y have. The \"default feature rather than an afterthought\" framing means we're not building a separate compliance layer; we're embedding it into the workflow scientists already use.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage:**\n- Scope creep: \"diverse model architectures\" is vague and could balloon. But the core idea of timestamps, input metadata, user IDs across CI/CD deployments is well-scoped enough to start.\n- Maintenance burden: A centralized library needs ongoing maintenance as models evolve. This is a recurring cost. But again, one team maintaining one library vs. N teams maintaining N ad-hoc solutions.\n- The proposal doesn't specifically address the Annex III point 1(a) requirements (biometric matching logs with reference databases, specific input data matches, natural person verification IDs). It's more general. However, as a framework/foundation, it could accommodate those specifics.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a high-leverage infrastructure investment that directly addresses Article 12(1) and 12(2) requirements using existing CI/CD pipelines. It's the kind of \"build once, use everywhere\" proposal that maximizes resource efficiency. The main risk is scope creep in the \"diverse model architectures\" part, but the core concept is sound and aligned with efficient resource allocation. I'd approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is a strong fit for my domain. It uses existing infrastructure (CI/CD), creates a reusable asset across all projects, minimizes per-project engineering burden on scientists, and addresses the regulation head-on without requiring massive new funding. I'd say around 82-85. Not a perfect 90+ because of the \"diverse model architectures\" vagueness and ongoing maintenance cost, but clearly approve-worthy.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
