aluable and complementary.\n- It mentions \"identifying specific risk situations described in Article 79(1)\" - this is good alignment with Article 12(2)(a).\n- \"Alerts administrators when a substantial modification is detected\" - this addresses the traceability requirement.\n- The proposal focuses on Articles 12(2)(a) and (b), but what about 12(2)(c) regarding monitoring under Article 26(5)? It seems to cover this broadly with \"anomalies from all deployed systems.\"\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems, the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3) aren't explicitly addressed here - but that's more about what gets logged vs. how logs are monitored.\n\n**From a compliance officer's perspective:**\n- This is directly aligned with regulatory obligations. Articles 72 and 12 together require providers to monitor their systems post-deployment, and having a centralized dashboard that does this in real-time is a strong compliance measure.\n- Market reputation: Having an active monitoring system demonstrates regulatory diligence to market surveillance authorities. It shows we're not just technically compliant but operationally proactive.\n- Institutional survival: If a serious incident occurs (Article 73), having evidence of active post-market monitoring would be critical in demonstrating we fulfilled our obligations. The dashboard provides that evidentiary trail.\n- Practical: Using existing ELK stack reduces implementation cost and time-to-value.\n\n**Potential gaps:**\n- It's a monitoring/visualization tool, not the logging mechanism itself. But the proposal says \"parses logs\" implying the logging is handled elsewhere. As long as the underlying log generation (Article 12(1)) exists or is being built separately, this dashboard complements it well.\n- The scope seems limited to \"all deployed systems\" which is good for comprehensive coverage.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong compliance-aligned proposal that directly supports multiple Article 12 requirement