rchers who are focused on model development, not regulatory plumbing.\n- However, there's a risk: if the framework is too rigid or has performance overhead, it could slow down prototyping iterations (e.g., logging every intermediate tensor during rapid experimentation adds I/O). The \"lightweight\" qualifier helps but needs validation.\n- Building a shared library introduces a dependency - if the framework team is slow to respond to edge cases or new requirements, individual teams get blocked. This is a classic centralized-infrastructure tradeoff.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a solid, technically achievable proposal that directly addresses a real engineering concern (compliance overhead slowing prototyping). The approach of wrapping existing infrastructure rather than building new is smart from a resource perspective. The main risks are around the \"universal\" scope potentially being too ambitious for v1, and the shared dependency creating bottlenecks. But as an initial framework with composable primitives, this is very implementable.\n\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality), this clears my bar. It's a well-scoped engineering solution to a regulatory requirement that would otherwise impose per-team implementation costs. The technical approach is standard and proven. I'd approve it.\n\nPromise score: This is very aligned with my concerns. Technical feasibility is high, prototyping velocity impact is positive (removes per-team burden), and the approach leverages existing infrastructure. I'd give this around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because \"universal\" across all high-risk AI system types is ambitious and might need iteration, and there's some risk of the framework becoming a bottleneck or being too prescriptive for certain prototyping workflows. But it's clearly above my approval threshold.\n",
