lows prototyping. A standardized library that slots into project templates would genuinely accelerate the start of new projects.\n- However, there's a risk: building this framework itself is a substantial engineering investment. The question is whether the abstraction will be correct from the start or whether it'll need rework as requirements evolve (the EU AI Act is relatively new and guidance is still emerging).\n- \"Integrated into standard project templates\" - good idea, but if the template is wrong or too rigid early on, it could actually slow prototyping rather than accelerate it. Developers might fight against a logging framework that doesn't fit their specific experimental workflow.\n- The risk of over-engineering: in early prototyping/experimentation, you often want minimal overhead. A heavy middleware layer intercepting everything might add latency and complexity to what should be quick experiments. You'd want the logging to be opt-in or lightweight during experimentation phases and full during deployment/validation phases.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Tamper-evident\" adds engineering burden. For internal R&D prototyping, this is overkill. The requirement is for deployed high-risk systems, not for every experiment. Building tamper-evidence into the base framework means even quick prototypes carry that overhead.\n- The scope creep risk: trying to cover ALL of Article 12's requirements (including Annex III point 1(a) specifics like biometric matching logs, verification person IDs) in one unified library is ambitious. Those are domain-specific and may not fit a generic middleware pattern well.\n- However, the core idea - standardized API calls for logging that integrate into project templates - is sound and would reduce per-project engineering time.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality), this is a genuinely useful infrastructure investment IF scoped correctly. The risk is over-engineering it to try to cover 