nto heterogeneous model architectures (transformers, tabular models, multi-modal systems) will require per-project adaptation even with a good library.\n- The claim that it \"maintains prototyping speed\" needs scrutiny: the initial development of this library itself takes engineering time away from actual prototyping. There's a trade-off. If you have 10+ high-risk AI projects, amortization works. If you have 2-3, building a cross-project library might be over-engineering.\n\n**Regulatory alignment (Article 12 specifically):**\n- Article 12(1): \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events\" - yes, this addresses that directly.\n- Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c): The proposal mentions \"events relevant for\" identifying risk situations and post-market monitoring somewhat generically. It doesn't specifically address how it captures the types of events listed in (a)-(c) or the Annex III point 1(a) specific requirements (start/end times, reference database, input data matches, natural person identification).\n- The proposal is somewhat generic - \"timestamps and system states\" doesn't clearly map to all the specific data points Article 12(3) requires.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a prototyping speed perspective, this is a solid architectural idea with real value IF implemented correctly. A shared logging library reduces per-project compliance overhead significantly. The technical approach (middleware interception + immutable/append-only storage) is viable and well-understood. \n\nHowever, concerns:\n1. Building it as a \"cross-project\" standard before understanding all use cases risks either being too rigid or too generic.\n2. The scope of what gets logged needs to be carefully designed per Article 12's specific requirements - the proposal is vague on this.\n3. There's a risk of over-engineering for early prototypes - you might want lighter-weight logging during rapid iteration phases and fuller compliance logging at deployment.\n4. \"Immutable database\" adds 