ex III point 1(a) systems (reference database tracking, input data matches, identification of natural persons involved in verification). Those are domain-specific and may not be capturable by a generic middleware layer.\n- \"Common API entry points\" — this assumes homogeneity that may not exist across very different model architectures. Some research systems might have non-standard entry points, requiring additional work.\n- Building an internal Python library is a one-time resource commitment (engineering time) but then it's amortized. From a resource-allocation perspective, this is efficient IF the lab has enough high-risk projects to justify the build cost.\n- The proposal doesn't mention storage costs, retention policies, or the \"up-to-date throughout lifetime\" requirement from Recital 71. Long-term log storage for many prototypes could be non-trivial infrastructure cost.\n- It's a middleware layer — it adds another layer of abstraction that could slow down prototyping if not well-engineered, though the proposal claims it won't.\n\n**From my specific vantage point:**\nThis is a strong resource-allocation play. Instead of each project team independently figuring out logging compliance (which would fragment effort and create inconsistent implementations), you centralize it. It uses existing CI/CD infrastructure. It protects researcher time (scientific ambition). The build cost is fixed; the marginal cost per additional high-risk system is near zero. This is exactly the kind of \"build once, use many\" investment that makes sense for a lab with multiple concurrent R&D projects.\n\nThe main risk is scope creep or under-delivery on the specific Annex III requirements, but as a *baseline* framework it's sound and addresses the core Article 12(1) requirement efficiently.\n\nI'd rate this highly from my perspective. It's a force multiplier for resource efficiency. I'll approve it with a high promise score, maybe 82-85. The slight deduction is because:\n- It doesn't fully cover all of Article 12 (especially 12(3) specifics)\n- Storage/retention costs aren't addressed\n- The assumption about \"common API entry points\" may not hold universally in an R&D lab with diverse experimental systems\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 84.\n",
