nternal governance, not just regulatory compliance - dual-use value.\n\nCons / Concerns:\n1. Building a custom \"Dynamic Technical Documentation Engine\" is itself a significant software engineering investment. We're an R&D lab, not a software house. Diverting engineers to build this tool competes with actual research projects.\n2. The scope is potentially very large - \"transforms raw development data into regulatory-ready reports\" could be a massive undertaking if done properly for all AI systems in the portfolio.\n3. There's a risk of over-engineering: we might spend months building an elegant system when a simpler approach (e.g., structured templates + CI hooks) would suffice.\n4. The \"living document\" concept sounds good but maintenance of the synchronization itself becomes an ongoing resource drain.\n5. It doesn't address Article 12(3) specifically for biometric systems - it's more about Recital 71 technical documentation than the specific logging requirements in Article 12. Though Recital 71 is relevant context.\n6. The proposal is somewhat vague on implementation specifics - \"pulls metadata from Git and training logs\" is a starting point but the full scope of what constitutes \"regulatory-ready reports\" under the AI Act is substantial.\n\nFrom my resource-allocation perspective:\n- The core insight (use existing VCS and training infrastructure to generate compliance documentation) is sound and efficient\n- However, building a dedicated \"engine\" for this is a significant commitment of engineering resources\n- A more pragmatic approach might be: configure CI/CD pipelines with documentation-generation steps, use existing documentation tools (Sphinx, MkDocs) with templates that map to AI Act requirements, and add automated metadata extraction as a step in the build process. This would achieve 80% of the value at maybe 30-40% of the cost.\n- The proposal as stated (\"We will implement a system\") sounds like building something bespoke rather than configuring existing tools.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says it \"synchronizes version control systems with the required technical documentation\" and \"pulls metadata from Git and training logs.\" This could be interpreted as either:\n(a) Building a completely new custom platform - high cost\n(b) Configuring existing DevOps infrastructure (Git hooks, CI/CD pipelines, documentation generators) to produce compliance outputs - moderate cost\n\nThe language is somewhat ambiguous but leans toward building something. \"An internal tool\" and \"We will implement a system\" suggest development work.\n\nFrom my vantage point: The concept is aligned with efficient resource use IF implemented leanly (leveraging existing infrastructure). But as proposed, it risks becoming a significant engineering project that diverts resources from core R&D. The key question is whether the scope is well-bounded or open-ended.\n\nI think this is a promising direction but needs scoping discipline. I'd approve it with some reservations about implementation approach - but my job here is to screen on merits, not to add conditions. Does it clear my bar?\n\nMy bar: Is this a good use of scarce R&D resources? Does it leverage existing infrastructure efficiently? Does it balance compliance necessity against research throughput?\n\nI think the answer is: The CONCEPT clears the bar (it's efficient in principle - uses existing data, automates repetitive documentation work, doesn't disrupt workflow). But the SCOPE as stated risks being too large. However, I can approve the proposal and note that implementation should be phased/lean. Actually, I'm supposed to judge on its merits. The core idea is sound: don't build new compliance infrastructure from scratch; derive it from what you already have for research.\n\nI'll approve with a moderate-high promise score. It's well-aligned with my concerns but has execution risk in terms of resource commitment if over-scoped. I'd say 72-78 range. Let me settle