es EU AI Act obligations seriously - not as an afterthought.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n1. Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics) requires very specific logging: start/end times of use, reference databases, input data matched, identification of natural persons in verification. Markdown templates in version control don't naturally capture runtime operational logs - they're development-time documentation, not runtime event logging. This proposal addresses the technical documentation aspect but NOT the automatic recording requirement (Article 12(1)).\n2. The \"mandatory\" update at every major commit could create compliance gaps if researchers skip or provide superficial entries. There's no enforcement mechanism described beyond \"mandatory.\"\n3. Version control alone doesn't guarantee immutability - force pushes, rebasing, etc. can alter history. For regulatory traceability, you'd want additional safeguards (signed commits, append-only storage, etc.).\n4. The proposal focuses on documentation templates but Article 12's core requirement is about \"automatic recording of events\" - the system itself must technically log operations. Documentation templates are a complement, not a substitute for the logging infrastructure.\n5. However, the Recital explicitly calls for technical documentation to be kept up to date, and this proposal directly addresses that need.\n\n**Overall assessment from Compliance Officer's vantage point:**\nThis is a solid supporting measure for the technical documentation requirements referenced in Recital 71 and implicitly supported by Article 12 (the \"availability of technical documentation\" mentioned in the recital). It doesn't fully satisfy Article 12(1)'s automatic logging requirement, but it addresses the documentation side that regulators will demand. From institutional survival: having this in place means we can produce compliant documentation quickly and systematically. From market reputation: it shows embedded compliance culture.\n\nThe key limitation is that it's a partial solution - it handles documentation but not runtime logging. But as a standalone proposal, it's clearly within scope of what Article 12 and Recital 71 demand regarding technical documentation being kept up to date throughout the system's lifetime. It's a necessary component (though not sufficient alone for full compliance).\n\nI'd rate this moderately high - it addresses real compliance needs identified in the regulation, but it's incomplete as a standalone measure for Article 12. I'll approve it because it clearly moves us toward compliance on the documentation side, and the compliance officer would want to see this implemented. It reduces regulatory risk and supports institutional survival by making compliance demonstrable.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 70-75. It's genuinely useful and aligned with the regulation's documentation requirements, but it doesn't cover the automatic logging aspect which is the core of Article 12(1). Also, \"mandatory\" without enforcement mechanisms is a concern for a compliance officer. Let me settle on 72.\n",
