aceability is redirected only where legally required.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The \"classification by risk level\" step itself has costs. Who maintains the classifier? What happens when a project's risk profile changes mid-lifecycle (e.g., a research prototype transitions into a product)? You need ongoing re-assessment, not just onboarding.\n- The proposal says \"automatically enables specific logging modules\" — but what are those modules? If they don't already exist in the infrastructure, you still have to build them. The triage system doesn't eliminate the engineering work; it just gates it. However, gating is still valuable because it prevents speculative pre-building.\n- There's a risk of under-classification: if a project that should be high-risk gets misclassified as low-risk, we'd face regulatory non-compliance. This is a legal/compliance risk that could be very expensive (fines, reputational damage). The proposal doesn't address validation or audit of the classification itself.\n- \"Onboarding checklist\" suggests a one-time gate at project start. But Article 12 requires logging \"over the lifetime of the system.\" If a system's intended purpose changes mid-lifecycle and it becomes high-risk, you'd need to retrofit. The proposal is somewhat static.\n- However, from a pure resource-efficiency standpoint, even an imperfect triage that catches 90% of cases correctly saves enormous resources compared to \"log everything at maximum fidelity for every project.\"\n\n**From my specific vantage point:**\nThis is squarely in my wheelhouse. The core thesis — apply strict regulatory constraints only where legally required, don't burden exploratory research with production-grade compliance infrastructure — is exactly the resource-allocation principle I'd advocate. The CI/CD integration leverages existing infrastructure rather than creating parallel governance processes. The onboarding checklist is a low-cost mechanism compared to building full logging for everything.\n\nThe main concern is that the classification step needs its own maintenance and validation, and the \"automatic\" trigger could create a false sense of security. But these are implementation details, not fundamental flaws in the concept.\n\nI think this is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses resource efficiency, uses existing infrastructure (CI/CD), and protects scientific ambition by preventing compliance overhead from strangling exploratory work. I'd approve it, though I'd want to see more detail on the classification mechanism and lifecycle re-assessment.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's well-aligned with my domain concerns, practical in its use of existing infrastructure, but has some gaps (classification maintenance, mid-lifecycle changes) that would need addressing before full implementation. I'll go with 80.\n",
