sion control provides traceability of who changed what and when - supports the traceability purpose in Recital 71.\n4. Real-time compliance review before changes go live reduces the risk of shipping a system whose documentation no longer reflects its actual behavior.\n5. This is an implementation resource commitment that directly mitigates regulatory risk. If we don't have this, and a model iteration happens without doc update, we're in violation during any audit window.\n\nConcerns/Weaknesses:\n1. The proposal mentions \"major change\" as the trigger - but the regulation requires documentation to be current throughout the lifetime. What counts as \"major\"? This threshold needs precise definition. A minor dataset adjustment could still affect compliance claims. However, this is an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw in the concept.\n2. \"Real time\" review by compliance officers could be a bottleneck - if every model iteration requires human sign-off before deployment, R&D velocity drops significantly. But from my vantage point (compliance), that's actually appropriate gatekeeping.\n3. The proposal doesn't explicitly address Article 12(1) logging requirements (automatic event recording). It focuses on documentation rather than operational logs. However, it's complementary - the technical file describes the system while logs capture its operation. This isn't a disqualifier; the proposal is about documentation synchronization specifically.\n4. \"Git-integrated\" is a good technical choice for version control and audit trails, but the implementation details matter. Still, the concept is sound.\n\n**Market reputation angle:** Having a demonstrable, automated documentation pipeline that ensures regulatory files are always current would be a strong signal to customers, partners, and regulators that we take compliance seriously. This enhances market position.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:** Without this, rapid R&D cycles will inevitably create documentation