ucture where it's not legally required.\n- The claim that it \"guarantees technical compliance by default\" is inaccurate and potentially dangerous — it could create false confidence. Article 12(3) has very specific logging requirements for biometric systems that generic I/O capture won't satisfy.\n- Building custom middleware across all model versions is a significant, ongoing engineering commitment that competes with scientific development for the same people.\n- The proposal doesn't mention leveraging existing observability tools or ML experiment tracking infrastructure, which suggests it may not be using existing R&D infrastructure effectively.\n\nThat said, the core idea — automate what can be automated to free researcher time and reduce compliance burden — is sound and aligned with resource efficiency. The problem is more in scope (too broad), approach (build vs. adapt), and overpromising (guaranteeing compliance).\n\nI'm leaning toward reject because:\n- It doesn't address whether existing infrastructure can be leveraged first\n- \"All R&D pipelines\" is a misallocation of resources for non-high-risk work\n- Building custom middleware from scratch when off-the-shelf or semi-off-the-shelf solutions exist is not resource-efficient\n- The overconfident compliance claim could mask gaps in actual Article 12(3) requirements\n\nBut it's not a terrible idea — the direction is right, just the execution plan as described is not optimal. I'd give it a moderate promise score because the underlying principle is valid but the specific proposal as written has significant inefficiencies and scope problems from my vantage point.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, with a promise of maybe 35-40. The direction is right but the approach is not resource-efficient (builds new rather than adapting existing), over-scoped (all pipelines vs. only high-risk systems), and makes overconfident compliance claims that could mislead leadership into thinking one middleware layer solves everything wh