d by \"high-fidelity event tracing... in real time across all high-risk models\" is enormous relative to what's actually mandated\n\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal is too ambitious and poorly scoped for a lab with constrained resources. It risks over-committing implementation resources to a research vision that goes well beyond compliance needs, without clear phasing or return criteria. The core insight (use logs for research) is sound but the execution plan as described would burn significant resources on infrastructure far exceeding what Article 12 demands.\n\nI would reject this proposal as written — not because the underlying idea is bad, but because it over-commits resources to an unphased, all-or-nothing build that conflates compliance minimums with research maximums. A better proposal would separate: (a) implement the Article 12 logging requirements efficiently, and (b) separately evaluate whether a subset of those logs can support specific interpretability research questions with bounded resource commitments.\n\nPromise score: The idea has some merit from my perspective — leveraging compliance infrastructure for research is efficient in principle. But the execution as described would be resource-prohibitive and poorly scoped. I'd say around 25-30. It's not zero because the underlying logic of dual-use (compliance data → research) is sound, but the proposal as written would waste resources if approved without significant restructuring.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 28.\n",
