ng capabilities.\n- The balance between scientific ambition and funding: This doesn't advance science at all, but it also doesn't require major new funding — it's an operational protocol. It uses existing compliance resources.\n- Effective use of infrastructure: Quarterly stress tests on logs is a reasonable use of existing test environments. It doesn't require new hardware or major software investment.\n- The \"before any high-risk system is deployed\" gate could create delays in the R&D pipeline, which from a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint is a concern — it adds a serial dependency to deployment. But this is a legal requirement anyway (the system must technically allow for logging), so the audit just verifies what should already be there.\n- The proposal is defensive/reactive rather than enabling research. It protects against regulatory risk but doesn't enable new capabilities or advance scientific goals.\n\nFrom my vantage point: This is a reasonable, narrowly-scoped operational protocol that uses existing resources (compliance team, test environments) without requiring significant new investment. It's not glamorous from a scientific ambition standpoint, but it's efficient in its resource use — quarterly checks on an already-built system are low-cost. The main concern is the \"mandatory\" gate before deployment adding process overhead, and the recurring quarterly cost. But these are modest relative to the lab's overall budget.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. It's a sensible operational control that doesn't consume disproportionate resources, leverages existing infrastructure (compliance team, test environments), and is appropriately scoped. The promise score would be moderate — it's useful but not transformative from an R&D efficiency standpoint. Maybe around 55-60? It's reasonable and low-cost to implement, but it doesn't advance the lab's scientific mission or create new capabilities. It's a necessary compliance overhead that's efficiently designed.\n