        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the perspective of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2799 is a \"Low-Overhead Traceability Research Initiative\" - a research project to minimize computational and storage overhead from high-granularity logging required by Article 12. It aims to:\n- Research compressed logging formats\n- Selective event-triggering algorithms\n- Maintain strict traceability without degrading real-time inference speeds\n- Benchmark against existing high-risk systems\n- Frame it as a publishable scientific contribution\n\nLet me assess this from my vantage point (technical viability, speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Positive aspects:**\n- Logging overhead is a genuine technical concern. High-frequency logging on every inference can indeed degrade real-time performance and consume significant storage over the system's lifetime (which for high-risk AI systems could be years).\n- Compressed logging formats are technically feasible - there's prior work in log compression, event sampling, etc.\n- Selective event-triggering is a well-known engineering pattern (only log when certain conditions are met rather than logging everything).\n- Benchmarking against existing systems is practical and grounded.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- This is framed as a \"research initiative\" and \"scientific project.\" From a prototyping speed/quality standpoint, I'm skeptical of open-ended research projects. The actual engineering solution here is relatively well-understood: you can use log compression (snappy, zstd), event sampling/thresholding, ring buffers, tiered storage (hot/warm/cold). These aren't novel research problems - they're engineering problems with known solutions.\n- \"Selective event-triggering algorithms\" risks conflicting with Article 12(1) which says systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events over the lifetime.\" If you selectively trigger, are you still meeting the requirement? The regulation requires logging capabilities that enable recording of specific event types (a), (b), (c) in paragraph 2. You can't just skip logging because it's expensive - the requirement is about what must be recordable.\n- \"Publishable scientific contribution\" framing suggests this will take a long time and produce academic papers rather than shipping engineering solutions. From my prototyping perspective, I need working solutions integrated into our systems within sprint cycles, not multi-year research programs.\n- The scope is vague: \"research compressed logging formats\" - we can just use existing compression libraries. \"Selective event-triggering algorithms\" - this needs to be defined carefully against the regulatory requirements in Art 12(2)(a)-(c) and 12(3).\n- However, there IS a legitimate engineering challenge here: designing the logging architecture for high-risk AI systems that meets Article 12 while maintaining real-time performance. The lifetime aspect (logs must persist over the entire system lifecycle) creates genuine storage challenges.\n\n**Technical viability assessment:**\n- The core problems identified are real but well-understood in distributed systems engineering.\n- The \"research initiative\" framing makes me worry about time-to-delivery.\n- The risk of \"selective event-triggering\" is that it might not meet the regulatory requirement for automatic recording - you need to be very careful here. The regulation says \"shall technically allow for\" which means the capability must exist, but doesn't necessarily mean every single micro-event needs to be logged at full fidelity in real-time.\n- Compressed logging: straightforward engineering, not really a research problem.\n- The benchmarking component is good and practical.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping perspective:**\n- This proposal would commit resources to an open-ended \"research