        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2635 is about \"Research on High-Fidelity Event Logging for Model Debugging.\" Let me break down what it's proposing:\n\n1. A dedicated research project to identify which specific system events are most predictive of failure or bias in high-risk AI\n2. Moving logging from passive record to active diagnostic tool\n3. Analyzing historical execution logs using anomaly detection\n4. Creating a taxonomy of critical events required by Article 12\n5. Publishing findings as peer-reviewed contributions\n\nNow let me assess this from my professional vantage point (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n- This is framed as a \"research project\" — it's exploratory, not an implementation. From a development engineering perspective, the core question is: does this actually lead to a deployable logging solution? The proposal talks about \"identifying which specific system events are most predictive of failure or bias.\" This is essentially an R&D exploration that may or may not yield actionable results within any timeframe.\n- The anomaly detection approach on historical logs assumes we have sufficient historical data, which for new AI systems we may not. This is a chicken-and-egg problem: you need the logging infrastructure to capture events before you can analyze them for patterns.\n- \"Creating a taxonomy of critical events\" sounds reasonable but is vague in scope. Without clear deliverables tied to specific Article 12 requirements (the four sub-points in paragraph 3, or the three categories in paragraph 2), this could drift indefinitely.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Concerns:**\n- This is a research project, not a prototyping effort. It doesn't propose building anything — it proposes analyzing and publishing. From my perspective as someone responsible for delivering working systems, this doesn't accelerate our ability to build compliant logging infrastructure.\n- The proposal conflates compliance engineering (building the log capture system that technically allows automatic recording per Article 12(1)) with post-hoc analysis of what those logs tell us. These are different problems requiring different resources and timelines.\n- \"High-fidelity event logging\" is mentioned but the actual work described is analytical/taxonomic, not about building the logging infrastructure itself.\n- The peer-reviewed publication angle is interesting for the institution's reputation but doesn't advance our prototyping velocity or technical deliverables.\n\n**What it gets right:**\n- The idea that logs should be more than passive records and serve as diagnostic tools is good engineering thinking.\n- Understanding which events matter most before building comprehensive logging could prevent over-engineering.\n- However, this insight can likely be gained through a much shorter scoping exercise or design review rather than a full \"research project\" with publication goals.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal is too exploratory and underspecified to commit implementation resources to. It doesn't propose building a prototype, it doesn't have clear technical deliverables tied to Article 12's specific requirements (the four minimum data points in paragraph 3 for Annex III 1(a) systems, or the three traceability categories in paragraph 2), and its primary output is \"peer-reviewed contributions\" rather than working software. The risk of drift is high — \"identifying which events are most predictive\" could go on indefinitely without a clear stopping criterion tied to compliance requirements.\n\nHowever, the underlying insight (that we should think carefully about what to log before building the logging system) has some merit. A short design study or spike might capture 80% of that value in a