on environments the lab already maintains for system validation.\n- The \"periodic\" nature means it doesn't require dedicated ongoing staff — could be folded into existing review cycles.\n- However, designing meaningful \"simulated failure scenarios\" that specifically target Article 12(2)(a) risk triggers is non-trivial work. It requires understanding the specific risk pathways in each system and crafting scenarios to test those pathways in the logging layer.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe proposal has merit as a compliance verification step, but from my resource-allocation vantage point:\n- It's not a new infrastructure build; it's a process/protocol.\n- \"Periodic\" limits the ongoing cost.\n- It could leverage existing test/validation infrastructure.\n- The risk of NOT doing this (regulatory penalty, loss of market access) is significant and would consume far more resources reactively.\n- However, the proposal as stated doesn't specify frequency, depth, or scope precisely enough to assess true resource cost. \"Periodic\" could mean quarterly or annually.\n- The simulated failure scenarios are the most resource-intensive part. Crafting them requires domain expertise per system type (especially for Annex III 1(a) biometric systems where Art 12(3) has specific logging requirements).\n\nOn balance, this is a reasonable, bounded commitment that protects against asymmetric downside risk. It doesn't consume large R&D resources if scoped well. The \"periodic\" framing keeps it from becoming an ongoing drain. I lean toward approval but with moderate confidence because the actual resource cost depends heavily on implementation scoping.\n\nI'll approve it at around 62-65 promise. It's a sensible compliance step that protects resources from worse outcomes, can leverage existing infrastructure, and is bounded in scope by its \"periodic\" nature. But it's not something I'd be enthusiastic about — it's defensive, doesn't advance the lab's scientific mission, and the resource cost de