ndard of evidence in AI research papers.\n- But the intellectual novelty is limited. Cryptographic hashing for data integrity and version control is well-established technology (git, IPFS, content-addressable storage, etc.). The specific application to EU AI Act compliance logging is a regulatory engineering task, not a research frontier advancement.\n- The \"improving overall scientific reproducibility\" claim is somewhat aspirational — this is a single-lab internal tool for compliance, not a general-purpose framework that would be adopted by the broader scientific community. It doesn't create new knowledge or open new research directions.\n\nOverall: This is competent engineering work with modest scientific value. It addresses a regulatory requirement well (specifically Article 12(3)(b) — \"the reference database against which input data has been checked\"), and the reproducibility angle gives it some intellectual merit, but from my vantage point as someone evaluating knowledge-frontier advancement and broader scientific impact, this is incremental infrastructure work. It doesn't open new research questions or significantly advance the state of knowledge. The cryptographic hashing approach is standard; the application to compliance logging is a regulatory engineering task.\n\nI'd say this clears a low bar but doesn't excite me from a research-merit perspective. It's useful, well-scoped, and addresses a real need (both regulatory and for reproducibility), but it's not frontier work. I'll lean toward approving at a moderate promise score — it does contribute to scientific rigor and reproducibility in AI systems, which matters, even if the intellectual novelty is modest.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My vantage point is specifically: knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, broader scientific impact. \n\n- Knowledge frontier: No. This applies existing cryptographic/versioning techniques to a regulatory compliance context.\n- Intellectual merit of research: Moderate. The provenance concept applied to AI search operations has some merit for ensuring that published results are tied to specific data states, but the implementation is straightforward engineering.\n- Broader scientific impact: Low-moderate. It's an internal lab tool. Unless it's open-sourced or contributes a framework others adopt, its broader impact is limited. The reproducibility benefit is real but narrow (limited to this lab's high-risk AI search operations).\n\nI think I'll approve at a modest score — maybe 45-55. It's legitimate work that supports scientific rigor, the provenance concept has intellectual value for ML evaluation methodology, and it addresses Article 12(3)(b) directly. But it's not pushing any frontier. The \"scientific reproducibility\" benefit is real but secondary to its primary compliance purpose.\n\nLet me settle on: approve at 50. It clears the bar because provenance tracking in AI systems does contribute to scientific integrity and reproducibility (which are core to my domain), and the specific mapping of execution-to-database-state is a methodologically sound contribution. But it's not high-impact frontier research.\n",
