        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1799\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This is an open-ended scientific research project (ablation studies, sensitivity analyses) rather than a technically bounded development task; the concrete minimums in Art. 12(3) and the principle-based categories in Art. 12(2) are implementable now through standard failure-mode analysis and system-architecture review without first completing an exploratory study on 'which internal state changes are most predictive.' From a prototyping-speed standpoint, committing scarce engineering cycles to a research question with no clear termination criterion or shippable component means I cannot integrate its output into a buildable logging pipeline on any foreseeable timeline.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 15\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1799 is about \"Research into Semantic Traceability for Complex AI\" - it's a scientific research project to define which internal state changes in high-risk AI are most predictive of risk or modification, using ablation studies and sensitivity analyses to identify critical system events for logging.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The proposal is fundamentally a research/exploratory project (\"Research into...\", \"A scientific project to define...\"). It's asking us to figure out WHAT should be logged before we build the logging system.\n- From a development standpoint, Article 12(3) already provides concrete minimum requirements for biometric identification systems (Annex III point 1(a)): start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons involved in verification. These are specific, implementable requirements.\n- For other high-risk categories under Article 12(2), the requirements are more principle-based (\"events relevant for identifying situations that may result in risk,\" \"facilitating post-market monitoring,\" \"monitoring operation\"). This does create some design ambiguity, but it's not a research problem - it's an engineering problem of mapping known system events to those categories.\n- Ablation studies and sensitivity analyses are ML research techniques. They're valid for model development, but using them to determine what to log is somewhat misdirected. The \"what to log\" question is more about system architecture, failure modes, and operational monitoring than about model internal states.\n- The proposal says it \"moves beyond simple timestamps to meaningful behavioral markers.\" But the regulation already mandates specific data points (not just timestamps). For other categories, you need domain knowledge of the specific use case, not necessarily ML ablation studies.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a research project, not a prototyping effort. It will consume engineering resources on exploratory work without a guaranteed deliverable that maps to a shippable component.\n- The lab's scarce implementation resources would be tied up in \"defining which internal state changes are most predictive\" - this is an open-ended research question with no clear termination criterion from my development perspective.\n- We could prototype logging infrastructure NOW based on the concrete requirements in Article 12(3) and reasonable engineering judgment for Article 12(2), without needing to complete a scientific study first.\n- The \"intellectual merit\" framing is appealing but doesn't translate to technical deliverables I can build and ship.\n\n**Key concerns:**\n1. This is research, not development. My function is about technical viability of solutions and prototyping speed/quality. A \"scientific project to define which internal state changes are most predictive\" is upstream of what I do.\n2. The concrete requirements in the regulation (especially Art 12(3)) don't need this research to be implementable. We can build logging for biometric systems today.\n3. For broader categories, the \"events relevant for risk identification\" question is better addressed through system architecture review and failure mode analysis than through ablation studies on model internals.\n4. Open-ended scope: \"identify critical system events that should be prioritized in logs\" - when does this research end? There's no clear technical deliverable I can integrate into a prototype.\n5. The proposal conflates compliance logging (which is about operational traceability) with ML interpretability research (ablation, sensitivity analysis). These are different disciplines.\n\nHowever, let me steelman it: there IS some value in understanding which system events matter most for risk detection, especially for novel or complex AI systems where the failure modes aren't obvious. If we're building a logging system and we have hundreds of possible events to track, prioritization matters for performance and storage. But this can be done through engineering analysis (failure mode trees, operational review) without a full \"scientific project.\"\n\nMy assessment: This is not technically viable as a development deliverable within my function's mandate. It's an open-ended research question that doesn't produce a shippable component on any reasonable timeline. The concrete requirements in the regulation can be implemented now without this research. I would reject it.\n\nPromise score: From my vantage point, this is low. Maybe 15-20. There's some marginal intellectual value but it doesn't serve prototyping speed or technical viability of a solution. It's not something I'd \"never back\" (so not 0) because the underlying question of event prioritization has SOME engineering relevance, but it's very far from what my function needs to be productive.\n\nI'll go with 15.\n",
