k of deploying a non-compliant system, which could lead to enforcement action, fines, or market withdrawal orders under the AI Act's penalties regime. This directly protects institutional survival.\n- The framing around \"costly retrospective engineering fixes\" is pragmatic but also compliance-relevant — if we deploy without proper logging and then get caught in post-market surveillance (Article 74), the remediation costs and reputational damage would be far worse.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. It doesn't specify who conducts the audit, what criteria are used for \"verification,\" or how it interfaces with Article 12(3)'s specific minimum logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics). However, as a protocol framework, this level of detail might be appropriate at the proposal stage.\n- It focuses on \"before they move to deployment\" — but Article 12 also requires logs over the \"lifetime\" of the system. The periodic check-gates partially address this, but there's an implicit gap: what about ongoing monitoring post-deployment? Actually, re-reading it: \"periodic check-gates during the R&D lifecycle\" — this could be interpreted as covering both pre-deployment and post-deployment if \"R&D lifecycle\" is broad enough. But it's a bit ambiguous.\n- The proposal says project leads must verify — from a compliance standpoint, I'd want independence (segregation of duties), but at the protocol level, having project leads self-verify with documented sign-off is a reasonable first layer. A fully independent audit might be overkill at this stage.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a solid, directly relevant proposal that addresses the core compliance obligation in Article 12. It creates an institutional mechanism to ensure we don't ship non-compliant systems, which is critical for:\n- Regulatory compliance (avoiding enforcement)\n- Institutional survival (avoiding fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover under the AI