        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2272\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This directly addresses the structural vulnerability I see most often in compliance audits: logging and record-keeping engineering (the technical substrate of Article 12(1)–(3)) is perpetually underfunded because it competes with novel research for the same discretionary budget line, and it loses. Earmarking a mandatory compliance overhead as a distinct, non-negotiable grant-application line item guarantees that automatic event recording, traceability logging (start/end timestamps, reference databases, matched inputs, verifier identification per 12(3)), and post-market monitoring data capture are actually built rather than deferred indefinitely. From an institutional-survival standpoint, failure to demonstrate Article 12 compliance during a market-surveillance audit or enforcement action carries existential risk (fines up to 7 % of worldwide turnover, withdrawal orders), so securing dedicated funding for this technical work is not optional overhead—it is the cost of staying in business. The mechanism is simple, integrates into existing grant processes without new bureaucracy, and removes the recurring political friction of justifying compliance spend project-by-project.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2272 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal: \"Compliance-Aware Resource Budgeting\" — a funding allocation model that earmarks a fixed percentage of every project's budget for regulatory engineering tasks, integrated into internal grant applications as a mandatory compliance overhead to fund technical implementation of Article 12 requirements.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- This directly addresses the core problem I face: compliance work (logging infrastructure, record-keeping systems) gets deprioritized when competing with research ambitions for finite budgets. If logging capabilities aren't built in, we cannot demonstrate compliance under Article 12(1)-(3).\n- Earmarking ensures that the technical implementation of logging (automatic event recording, start/end timestamps, reference database references, input data capture, verification personnel identification) is funded and not an afterthought.\n- Making it a mandatory line item in grant applications creates institutional accountability — researchers must acknowledge compliance costs upfront rather than treating them as optional or external.\n- From an institutional survival standpoint: if we can't demonstrate Article 12 compliance when regulators audit us, the consequences (fines up to 7% of global turnover, market withdrawal orders) are existential. Ensuring funding for this is critical.\n- \"Fixed percentage\" creates predictability and reduces friction — teams don't need to negotiate compliance budgets case by case.\n- It addresses the \"tedious but necessary\" problem directly: if logging engineering competes with novel research for the same budget line, it will consistently lose. Ring-fencing solves this.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- A \"fixed percentage\" is crude — not all projects carry equal Article 12 burden. A simple classification tool might need minimal logging vs. an Annex III point 1(a) biometric system which needs full (a)-(d) minimum logging. But this is a minor design detail; the principle of earmarking is sound even if the mechanism needs refinement.\n- The proposal focuses on \"technical implementation of Article 12 requirements\" — good, it's specific to record-keeping rather than being a generic \"compliance overhead.\"\n- There's a risk that researchers view this as tax and resist it, potentially leading to under-reporting of project scope or workarounds. But from my vantage point, the institutional mandate is clear: compliance is non-negotiable.\n\n**My professional assessment:**\nAs Compliance Officer, this proposal speaks directly to my core concern: ensuring that the technical infrastructure for record-keeping (logging capabilities per Article 12(1), traceability events per 12(2), minimum logging data per 12(3)) is actually funded and built. Without guaranteed resources, compliance engineering will perpetually lose the budget war against novel research. This is a structural fix that addresses a real and recurring institutional vulnerability. It protects institutional survival by ensuring we can demonstrate compliance during audits and market surveillance.\n\nThe proposal is well-scoped (Article 12 specifically), practical (integrated into existing grant processes), and addresses a genuine gap in how R&D institutions handle compliance costs. The \"fixed percentage\" mechanism is simple and implementable without creating excessive bureaucracy.\n\nI would approve this. From my vantage point, it's highly promising because it directly de-risks our regulatory exposure by ensuring the technical implementation of logging isn't perpetually underfunded or deferred. It's not a research proposal — it's an operational/structural one that enables compliance.\n\nPromise score: I'd rate this around 82-85. It's very aligned with my mandate, directly addresses a real vulnerability, and is implementable. The slight deduction is because the \"fixed percentage\" mechanism might need calibration (different risk profiles require different logging complexity), and it doesn't address whether the compliance engineering work itself will be done by in-house teams or requires external vendors (which affects budget adequacy). But these are refinements, not disqualifiers.\n\nLet me settle on 84.\n",
