ated on quickly.\n- Consortium work is inherently slow, consensus-driven, and politically complex. It does not align with fast prototyping cycles.\n- The proposal commits resources to a long-term standards effort rather than building and testing concrete logging implementations.\n- There's no clear technical deliverable I would prototype against — the \"templates\" are vague. Are they database schemas? API specs? Log format definitions? Without concrete technical specifications, it's hard to see how this accelerates our prototyping.\n\n**Resource Commitment Concerns:**\n- Leading a consortium is resource-intensive: meetings, consensus-building, political navigation, long timelines.\n- The lab's \"scarce implementation resources\" would be tied up in governance activities rather than building actual logging systems that we can deploy and test.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what concrete technical artifact would result — it's aspirational (\"positions our lab as a global leader\").\n\n**What it gets right:**\n- The underlying problem is real: Article 12 does require specific logging capabilities, and having well-defined event schemas would help implementation.\n- Open-source templates could be useful if they were concrete enough.\n\n**My assessment from the Development Engineer / prototyping vantage point:**\nThis proposal is fundamentally misaligned with my domain of concern. It's a governance/standards/governance initiative that produces white papers and consortium deliverables, not working technical prototypes. From a speed-of-prototyping standpoint, I would rather spend resources building actual logging infrastructure for our specific systems, testing it against Article 12 requirements, iterating quickly, and then contributing concrete implementations to any standards body. The proposal as stated commits scarce engineering resources to consensus-building and documentation rather than building and testing. It also lacks technical specificity — what are the \"schemas\"? What format? What tooling? Without that, I can't assess viability or plan a prototyping path.\n\nHowever, I should note that if done right, standard schemas could reduce our prototyping overhead in the long run by providing pre-validated structures. But as proposed, it's too vague, too slow, and too far from actual engineering work to clear my bar for committing implementation resources.\n\nI'll reject this with a low-moderate promise score — not zero because the underlying idea has some merit (standardized event schemas would help), but the execution as described doesn't align with technical prototyping priorities.\n",
