ucture. Without it, the lab simply cannot operate its high-risk AI systems legally in the EU.\n- The \"reduces administrative overhead\" framing means it actually frees up researcher time that would otherwise be spent on manual documentation, redirecting human effort toward actual research. This is net positive for scientific output per funding dollar.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Explicitly leverages existing version control and CI/CD. Good.\n- \"Living document\" generated from raw logs means we don't need separate documentation systems or manual curation teams.\n- The integration into existing workflows means researchers don't need to change their behavior significantly — the compliance artifact is a byproduct of work they're already doing.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Scope: \"automatically extract... into a living document\" could be interpreted very broadly. But the proposal ties it to specific artifacts (hyperparameters, dataset lineage, validation results) which are standard outputs already present in training runs. The extraction should be relatively mechanical.\n- Maintenance cost: any new pipeline needs upkeep. But since it rides on existing CI/CD, that overhead is minimal.\n- Is this over-engineering? For a lab developing high-risk AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, no — Article 12(1) mandates automatic logging, and Recital 71 requires documentation of \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes.\" This maps directly to what's proposed.\n\n**Verdict:**\nThis is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It:\n- Directly addresses a regulatory mandate (not optional)\n- Leverages existing infrastructure (CI/CD, version control) rather than requiring new parallel systems\n- Has high leverage: build once, benefit every training run indefinitely\n- Reduces ongoing administrative burden on researchers\n- Doesn't divert resources from scientific work — it protects time that would otherwise be lost to manual complian