eline\" could conflict with existing pipelines that were designed without this in mind — refactoring cost could be significant.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a sound infrastructure investment from an efficiency standpoint. The core logic — build once, use across projects — aligns well with resource-allocation efficiency. Article 12 makes logging mandatory for high-risk systems, so this isn't speculative; it's required work that will need to happen anyway. Doing it as a unified framework rather than ad-hoc per-project is the efficient path.\n\nMy concerns are:\n1. The scope (\"all high-risk projects\") might be premature if we don't yet have all those projects funded/active.\n2. \"Internal system states\" is vague and could balloon in cost.\n3. Integration into existing pipelines may be more disruptive than proposed.\n\nBut the fundamental approach is correct and efficient. I'd lean toward approving with moderate confidence, but flagging that implementation should be phased (build core framework first, extend as projects come online) rather than building everything for \"all\" projects upfront. However, the proposal as stated doesn't include phasing — it says \"across all high-risk projects\" which implies a big-bang approach.\n\nGiven my mandate is resource efficiency and I'm approving/rejecting based on whether it clears my bar: The core idea (shared logging infrastructure rather than per-project builds) is clearly the efficient path. The vagueness around scope and \"internal states\" are implementation details that can be managed in execution. The fundamental resource logic is sound: one framework > N frameworks. I'll approve but with a moderate promise score reflecting my concerns about scope creep and integration cost uncertainty.\n\nI'd put this at maybe 72-78. It's clearly the right direction, addresses a real mandatory requirement efficiently, but has some execution risks around scope definition and integration disruption that could erode the efficiency