ge that may require ongoing maintenance as regulations evolve or as the lab's systems change.\n- From a funding constraints perspective: the proposal is essentially an internal compliance automation tool. It doesn't advance scientific ambition directly. The question is whether the manual effort it replaces justifies the build cost. If the lab has multiple high-risk systems, amortizing this across them could make sense. If it's for one system, the ROI may be questionable versus hiring a dedicated compliance person.\n- \"Protects the lab's reputation during external audits\" — this is a reputational/defense argument rather than an efficiency argument per se, but it does reduce risk of costly audit failures.\n\nThe proposal leverages existing infrastructure (version control, training logs, CI/CD) which is good for resource efficiency. It addresses a real ongoing requirement under Article 12 (keeping records up to date). The automation angle reduces recurring labor costs. However, the scope as described (\"transforms raw R&D data into a format suitable for oversight authorities\") is broad and could balloon. The \"engine\" framing suggests significant development effort upfront.\n\nI think this is a reasonable proposal that addresses a genuine compliance need with an approach that builds on existing infrastructure. But from my vantage point, the scope is somewhat vague and the upfront cost vs. ongoing benefit needs clearer justification. I'll lean toward approve but with moderate promise score — it's useful but not transformative for my core concerns about scientific ambition vs. funding balance. It's a cost-center tool that reduces recurring overhead, which matters, but it doesn't free resources to do more science in any dramatic way. The existing infrastructure reuse is a plus.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My domain is resource-allocation efficiency and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure. This proposal:\n- Uses existing infrastructure (version control, training logs, CI/CD) ✓\n- Reduces ongoing manual labor for compliance documentation ✓\n- Has a clear operational integration point (CI/CD pipeline) ✓\n- But the \"engine\" to transform data into regulatory format is new development with unclear scope ⚠️\n- It's a one-time build cost that pays back over time through reduced recurring effort ✓\n- Doesn't advance scientific ambition but also doesn't impede it — it frees up people from manual documentation tasks\n\nI think I'd approve this. It's a sensible efficiency play that builds on what the lab already has. The promise score would be moderate-to-good — maybe 65-72 range. It's not a breakthrough, but it's practical and addresses a real recurring cost. The main risk is scope creep in \"format suitable for oversight authorities.\"\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 68.\n",
