having a single function call is excellent for researchers. It reduces cognitive overhead and integration friction.\n- However, \"a wrapper around existing logging frameworks\" — this is fine but somewhat generic. The key question is: does it actually capture what Article 12 requires? The regulation demands \"automatic recording of events\" — not just that the researcher calls a function, but that events are captured automatically over the system's lifetime.\n- There's a tension here: the proposal says researchers \"simply call a single function to record an event.\" But Article 12(1) says systems shall \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" This implies the logging should happen as part of the system's operation, not necessarily require explicit researcher intervention at every decision point. A pure SDK where you must manually call `log_event()` might miss edge cases or fail to capture events that occur deep in model inference pipelines.\n- That said, a good SDK can include middleware/hooks into ML frameworks (like wrapping torch inference calls, API endpoints, etc.) to automatically capture relevant events without requiring explicit researcher intervention for routine operations.\n\n**Concerns:**\n1. The proposal is somewhat shallow — it describes a wrapper around logging frameworks with metadata injection. It doesn't address:\n   - How logs are stored for the lifetime of the system (persistence, retention)\n   - How \"automatic\" recording works vs. manual function calls\n   - The specific field requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems\n   - Tamper-evidence / integrity of logs\n   - The \"lifetime\" aspect — where do logs live and how are they maintained?\n\n2. From a prototyping speed perspective, the \"single function call\" promise is attractive but potentially misleading. Researchers will still need to understand WHAT events to log and ensure all required fields are captured. The SDK reduces friction on format/metadata but doesn't