        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2498\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Lossy compression of audit-trail logs is technically incompatible with Article 12's traceability mandate: if a regulator or post-market monitoring review requires an exact event reconstruction, irreversibly discarded or altered log content renders the system non-compliant, and 'selective event triggering' risks omitting events the regulation explicitly requires (start/end times, reference database identity, matched input data, verifier identity for Annex III 1(a) systems). From a prototyping-speed standpoint, this proposal inverts the correct engineering sequence—compliant structured logging (timestamps, typed event schemas, retention policies) is well-understood and shippable in weeks—whereas committing R&D cycles to 'advanced lossy compression' of an audit trail produces a lower-fidelity artifact that still fails the regulatory bar while delaying a working implementation.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 12\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2498 is about developing \"high-efficiency logging methods\" that minimize storage and compute costs for lifelong recording, using lossy compression of logs and selective event triggering mechanisms.\n\nLet me think critically about this from a development engineering perspective:\n\n**Technical viability concerns:**\n1. **Lossy compression of logs**: This is a major red flag. Logs are audit trails. The EU AI Act requires traceability - you need to be able to reconstruct what happened, when, and why. Lossy compression means you lose information irreversibly. If a regulator comes asking \"show me the log entry from 3 years ago at exactly that moment,\" and your lossy compression has discarded or altered that data, you're non-compliant. The regulation says \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" - it doesn't say \"compressed logs\" or \"lossy compressed logs.\" Lossy compression fundamentally conflicts with the purpose of record-keeping for traceability and compliance verification.\n\n2. **Selective event triggering**: Article 12(2) specifies what logging must enable: identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations. The regulation sets minimum requirements (especially for Annex III point 1(a) systems). \"Selective\" triggering sounds like it might skip events that the regulation requires you to capture. This could be a compliance violation if events are dropped based on heuristics rather than regulatory requirements.\n\n3. **\"Minimize storage and compute costs\"**: While cost efficiency is always important in engineering, the primary requirement here is compliance - the system MUST technically allow for automatic recording. Optimizing for cost at the expense of completeness or fidelity would be a fundamental misunderstanding of what's being asked. The regulation doesn't say \"record events in the most efficient way possible.\" It says record them, and do so over the lifetime of the system.\n\n4. **\"Balancing regulatory needs with hardware constraints\"**: This framing suggests the proposal starts from an assumption that compliance is too expensive or resource-intensive, and therefore we need to find ways around it. In practice, logging infrastructure (structured logs, time-series databases, event streams) is well-understood engineering. The \"hardware constraints\" argument seems weak for most deployments - storage costs have dropped dramatically, and compute for writing structured logs is minimal relative to the AI inference itself.\n\n5. **Speed/quality of prototyping**: This proposal would be a research project that explores advanced compression and triggering mechanisms before we even have a working logging implementation. From a prototyping speed perspective, this is backwards. We should first implement straightforward, compliant logging (which is well-understood - structured JSON logs with timestamps, event types, etc.) and then optimize if needed. Spending R&D cycles on \"advanced lossy compression\" for an audit trail is technically misguided and would slow down our ability to deliver a compliant prototype.\n\n6. **The \"scientific opportunity\" framing**: While it's appealing to frame compliance as research opportunity, this particular direction (lossy compression of regulatory logs) is not scientifically viable in the way it's framed. You can't lossily compress an audit trail and still meet traceability requirements. The \"research\" here has a very low probability of producing something that both works AND complies with the regulation.\n\n7. **From a prototyping speed perspective**: A straightforward implementation would be: structured logging framework (e.g., OpenTelemetry, or a custom event schema), time-series storage, retention policies matching system lifetime. This can be prototyped in days to weeks. The proposed research project would take months to years and might produce something non-compliant anyway.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis proposal is technically misguided from my perspective. Lossy compression of logs that serve as regulatory audit trails is fundamentally incompatible with the traceability requirements. \"Selective event triggering\" risks missing events the regulation mandates. The framing of this as a \"research opportunity\" obscures what is essentially an attempt to cut corners on compliance while also being technically infeasible for its stated purpose. From a prototyping speed standpoint, it delays delivery of a working compliant solution by pursuing an optimization that (a) may be unnecessary at scale and (b) likely cannot be done losslessly enough to meet the regulatory bar.\n\nI would reject this proposal. The promise score should reflect that while I understand the cost concern, the technical direction is fundamentally wrong for this use case. Maybe a 15-20? It's not completely crazy in wanting efficient logging infrastructure, but the specific approaches proposed (lossy compression of audit logs) are technically non-viable for compliance purposes. I'll go with 15 - there's a kernel of valid concern about efficiency, but the specific technical direction undermines the regulatory purpose and would slow down our prototyping rather than accelerate it.\n",
