 but the proposal itself doesn't contribute new knowledge, new methods, or new understanding.\n- The proposal frames administrative relief as a research benefit, which is reasonable, but the *intellectual content* of the project itself is minimal from my vantage point. It's a compliance engineering task dressed in productivity language.\n- There's no novel scientific question being addressed. No hypothesis to test. No methodological advance.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: my domain includes \"broader scientific impact\" and freeing researchers from drudgery does have some (albeit modest) positive effect on research output. The proposal is also quite low-risk and clearly defined — it's not a moonshot that would consume resources with uncertain payoff.\n\nBut strictly from the vantage of \"knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact\":\n- Knowledge frontier: No advancement. It's operational plumbing.\n- Intellectual merit of the work itself: Low — it's template mapping and data extraction, well-trodden engineering territory.\n- Broader scientific impact: Indirect and modest (slightly more researcher time available for science).\n\nI think this is a borderline case. The indirect benefit to research productivity is real but small. The proposal doesn't advance the knowledge frontier at all. From my specific vantage point, it's not something I would champion or allocate research resources toward. It's an ops/eng task that happens to have a mild positive external effect on researchers' time allocation.\n\nI'll lean toward reject, with a moderate promise score acknowledging the indirect benefit but noting it doesn't clear my bar for intellectual merit and frontier advancement. Maybe a 30-40? It has some value as an enabling infrastructure piece but from my vantage point it's not research-worthy. Actually, let me think about this more carefully.\n\nThe instruction says \"approve every proposal that clears your bar and reject every proposa