e to the broader field of AI explainability or auditability, this specific proposal is narrowly scoped to internal regulatory compliance for a specific system class. It doesn't produce generalizable knowledge about AI systems.\n\n**However**, let me consider if there's any research angle:\n- The act of \"tracing model decisions back to training data\" could touch on interpretability research — but the proposal frames it as an audit exercise, not a research inquiry into whether/why traceability is possible or what its limits are.\n- \"Stress testing\" logs for traceability could theoretically reveal gaps in our understanding of how to make AI systems auditable — but again, this is framed as operational QA, not investigation.\n\nThe proposal is well-intentioned from a compliance standpoint and would be valuable to the compliance/legal function or engineering team. But from my specific vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), it's essentially an operational process design with no meaningful research contribution. It doesn't advance understanding of AI systems, doesn't generate publishable insights, doesn't open new research directions.\n\nMy assessment: This is a good compliance/operational proposal that I would not back from my research vantage point. It consumes implementation resources for what is fundamentally a QA process. The intellectual merit is low — it's applying existing verification concepts to a regulatory checklist. There's no frontier being pushed, no new knowledge being generated, and the broader scientific impact is negligible.\n\nDecision: reject\nPromise score: I'd put this quite low from my vantage point. Maybe 10-15. It's not harmful, it's reasonable as an operational exercise, but it has essentially zero research merit or frontier advancement from my perspective. I'll say 12 — there's a tiny sliver of potential that the \"stress testing\" framing could reveal interesting edge cases in traceability that mig