ake the sandbox bad; it just means it's necessary but not sufficient.\n- The proposal focuses on \"technical viability\" — compliance is about more than technical capability; it also requires organizational processes (who reviews logs, how long they're retained per Article 19, etc.). But that's outside the scope of this specific proposal.\n- Resource commitment: building a mirrored production cluster is expensive and resource-intensive. However, for an R&D institution developing high-risk AI systems, this is arguably table-stakes infrastructure.\n- The phrase \"without disrupting the primary R&D experimental flow\" suggests it's designed to be non-intrusive, which is good for operational feasibility.\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\nThis is a strong proposal. It creates a concrete, verifiable mechanism to ensure Article 12 requirements are met before deployment. The \"technical gate\" framing is exactly what regulators would want to see — evidence-based assurance that logging capabilities function correctly. For an R&D institution, this reduces the risk of deploying a system that cannot demonstrate compliance if audited by national authorities or notified bodies.\n\nThe sandbox approach also supports market reputation: being able to demonstrate pre-deployment validation of traceability capabilities signals maturity and regulatory readiness to partners, customers, and regulators.\n\nHowever, I should note it's not sufficient on its own — Article 12 requires lifetime logging, not just pre-deployment testing. But as a component of a broader compliance strategy, this is valuable infrastructure. It's a necessary piece.\n\nFrom institutional survival perspective: if a high-risk AI system fails to log events during an incident (Article 79), the institution could face significant penalties and reputational damage. Having validated that logging works before deployment mitigates this risk significantly.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-85. It's a solid, well-scoped proposal that directly addresses Article 12 compliance verification. I'm not giving it 90+ because:\n- It only addresses pre-deployment validation, not lifetime logging assurance\n- It doesn't explicitly address log retention periods (Article 19)\n- \"Edge case events\" is somewhat vague — what counts as edge cases? The proposal could be more specific about test scenarios mapped to Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c) and potentially the Annex III point 1(a) minimums in Article 12(3)\n\nBut it's clearly a valuable and necessary component of the compliance architecture. I'll go with approve at 82.\n",
