e in having structured sign-off if the system genuinely requires human verification before completing tasks. The question is whether this warrants a \"dedicated portal\" or can be embedded in the existing workflow UI.\n\nI think from my vantage point:\n- The underlying need is valid (Article 12(3)(d) does require capturing verifier identity)\n- But the proposed solution is over-engineered for prototyping purposes - it's a \"dedicated portal\" when what's needed might be a few fields in an existing log + an authentication check at a workflow step\n- Technical viability: high (it's not hard to build), but the scope makes it slow\n- The proposal as stated commits to building a new system rather than extending existing ones\n\nI'd lean toward reject because:\n1. It over-scopes a simple data-capture requirement into a full portal application\n2. It would consume significant prototyping resources for what could be a few days of work with a simpler approach\n3. The \"dedicated digital interface\" framing suggests building something new rather than integrating into existing infrastructure\n4. From a speed perspective, this is the wrong architecture - it adds a separate system to maintain, integrate, and test\n\nBut I should acknowledge that SOME form of structured verification capture is needed. The issue is the scope and approach, not the goal.\n\nPromise score: I'd put this around 30-40. The underlying need is real but the proposed implementation path is suboptimal from a development speed/resource perspective. It's not a bad idea in concept, it's just over-scoped and architecturally heavier than necessary for what Article 12(3)(d) actually requires at the logging level.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 35.\n",
