onment verifies that logging *capabilities* exist and work correctly at build time, but it doesn't address the \"over the lifetime of the system\" aspect — logs must be maintained throughout operation, not just verified once pre-deployment. However, the proposal says \"quality gate for deployment,\" which is a valid step in the lifecycle.\n- The scope seems reasonable and bounded — it's not asking to build the entire logging infrastructure, just a validation layer on top of it.\n\n**From compliance perspective:**\nThis is a strong, well-scoped proposal that directly supports our ability to demonstrate Article 12 compliance. It creates an audit trail (meta: we verify our logs are compliant). It reduces regulatory risk by catching issues pre-deployment. It's actionable and bounded in scope. The \"quality gate\" framing means it becomes part of the release process, which is exactly how a mature compliance program works.\n\nThe one concern is that it focuses on verifying logging at deployment time rather than addressing ongoing lifetime monitoring. But even so, having a validation sandbox that can be re-run periodically would support continuous compliance verification.\n\nI think this clears my bar as Compliance Officer. It's directly relevant to Article 12, well-scoped, actionable, and reduces our regulatory exposure. I'd approve it with a high promise score.\n\nPromise: I'd rate this around 85-90. It's very relevant to my domain, well-scoped, and addresses a real compliance gap. The slight deduction is that it only covers the pre-deployment validation phase and doesn't explicitly address the \"lifetime\" aspect of Article 12(1), nor does it clearly distinguish between the different sub-categories of high-risk systems with their different logging minimums. But those are refinements, not disqualifiers.\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 87.\n",
