le point of failure. If the logging middleware goes down, does the ML system go down? That's a critical dependency problem.\n- The \"standardizes how telemetry is collected across different AI architectures\" claim is where technical viability gets questionable. True standardization across heterogeneous architectures often requires either very coarse-grained abstractions (losing useful detail) or extremely complex adapters per architecture (defeating the \"unified\" purpose).\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a significant upfront investment before any prototype can be compliance-ready. Every new high-risk prototype must wait for the middleware to support its specific event patterns, or researchers must adapt their prototypes to fit the middleware's schema.\n- However, once built, it could genuinely reduce per-project compliance overhead. Researchers wouldn't need to figure out logging requirements each time - they'd just integrate the library.\n- The \"removes the burden of manual logging from researchers\" is a strong selling point for prototyping speed in the medium-to-long term. But the upfront development cost and integration friction could slow initial prototypes considerably.\n- Risk: if the middleware's abstraction doesn't fit a particular prototype well, researchers will either work around it (defeating compliance) or get blocked waiting for middleware updates (slowing prototyping).\n\n**From my specific domain (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\n- The concept is sound and directionally correct - automated, standardized logging is the right engineering approach vs. ad-hoc per-project solutions.\n- But \"universal\" is doing a lot of heavy lifting in this proposal. In practice, we'd need per-architecture adapters or very flexible schemas. The technical complexity of making it truly universal while remaining lightweight and non-intrusive is high.\n- The integration into \"core ML pipeline\" is risky from a prototyping speed 