        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain concerns are: resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal P2868 is a \"Post-Market Risk Detection System\" — an AI-driven monitoring tool that scans logs in real-time to flag anomalies, performance drift, and unexpected input patterns. It's framed as fulfilling Article 12(2)(a) requirements.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- This is a significant engineering effort: building \"heuristic and ML-based monitors\" that trigger alerts in real-time on production logs. This requires dedicated data engineering, ML model development for anomaly detection, alerting infrastructure, and ongoing maintenance.\n- The lab already needs to build the logging system itself (Article 12(1) — automatic recording of events). Building a sophisticated AI-driven monitoring layer ON TOP of that is a separate, substantial undertaking.\n- Is this proportional? Article 12(2)(a) requires logging capabilities that \"enable\" identifying risk situations. It doesn't mandate an autonomous AI-driven detection system. A human-readable log with appropriate fields could satisfy the requirement. The proposal goes well beyond minimum compliance into building a complex monitoring platform.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- Building ML-based anomaly detectors is scientifically interesting but also resource-intensive. Training, tuning, and validating these monitors requires data scientists' time that could be spent on the core product development or on meeting other Article 12 requirements (like the technical documentation in Recital 71).\n- The proposal frames this as \"protecting our market reputation\" which is a business consideration, not strictly a regulatory compliance one. While valuable, it adds scope beyond what's legally required.\n- Real-time scanning of production logs with ML models has ongoing computational costs (infrastructure, model retraining, false-positive management).\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The lab likely already has some monitoring/logging infrastructure from its operational systems. The question is whether this proposal leverages that or builds something new.\n- \"Heuristic and ML-based monitors\" suggests a fairly greenfield development effort rather than adapting existing tools.\n- However, if the lab's core product IS an AI system, building anomaly detection into it could be synergistic with other R&D work.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. Scope creep: The proposal goes beyond what Article 12(2)(a) strictly requires. The article says logging capabilities \"shall enable\" identification of risk situations — this can be achieved through well-structured logs that humans or simple rule-based systems can interpret, not necessarily a full ML-driven monitoring platform.\n2. Resource commitment: \"The team will develop a set of heuristic and ML-based monitors\" is a multi-person, multi-quarter effort with ongoing maintenance costs. This competes directly with other compliance work (technical documentation, risk management system documentation per Recital 71) and core product R&D.\n3. The proposal conflates the logging requirement (Article 12) with post-market monitoring (Article 72). Article 12(2)(b) says logs should \"facilitate\" post-market monitoring — it doesn't say the system itself must perform autonomous detection. Building an autonomous AI monitor is more aligned with Article 72's requirements but is a separate, heavier obligation.\n4. \"Real-time\" processing of production logs adds infrastructure complexity and cost that may not be justified by the regulatory text.\n\n**What would clear my bar?**\nFrom a re