ware or cloud providers that support them (AWS Nitro, GCP Confidential Computing). Slower to prototype but not insurmountable.\n- However, the \"dedicated R&D project\" framing suggests this is exploratory research rather than a concrete implementation. The scope seems broad - \"investigate the use of\" suggests uncertainty about whether these approaches will actually work for regulatory compliance.\n- The proposal conflates two different problems: (1) how to log data that satisfies Article 12, and (2) how to do so while minimizing privacy exposure. Problem (1) is straightforward engineering - you need structured logging with timestamps, references, input records, and verification personnel IDs. Problem (2) is more research-oriented but also potentially a non-starter if the regulator requires actual data access.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The technical approaches mentioned (hashing, secure enclaves) are well-known. There's no novel engineering challenge here that requires a \"dedicated R&D project.\" You can prototype basic cryptographic logging in days, not months.\n2. The fundamental question - whether hashed/encrypted logs satisfy Article 12(3)(c)'s requirement to record \"the input data\" - is a legal/regulatory question, not a technical one. Spending engineering resources on this without first resolving the compliance interpretation seems premature and risky.\n3. The proposal says \"investigate\" which is vague. From a prototyping standpoint, I want concrete deliverables: what system do we build by when? What does \"success\" look like technically?\n4. The \"competitive technical advantage in the market\" framing is marketing language that doesn't help me assess technical viability. Does this actually create a differentiator? Most logging solutions can be made privacy-preserving with standard techniques (encryption at rest, access controls). The novelty here seems limited.\n5. On the positive side: If we're building high-risk AI systems anyway, integrating robust logging from day one is good engineering practice. The privacy angle adds value but shouldn't be the primary driver of a \"dedicated R&D project.\"\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core technical elements (hashing, encryption, access control for logs) are straightforward and don't warrant a dedicated R&D project - they can be incorporated into standard development work. The novel/research aspects (verifying matches without exposing data to regulators) depend heavily on regulatory interpretation that hasn't been settled. Building this as a \"dedicated R&D project\" commits scarce resources to an uncertain payoff where the primary blocker is legal, not technical. From a prototyping speed perspective, I could have a basic privacy-preserving logging prototype in 2-3 weeks as part of normal development, not as a standalone research initiative.\n\nHowever, there IS value in thinking about this problem - but it should be scoped differently: as a design consideration within the main system architecture rather than a separate R&D project. The \"research\" framing is overkill for what are essentially applied engineering problems with some legal uncertainty layered on top.\n\nI'm leaning toward reject because:\n- The technical challenges described don't require dedicated R&D; they're standard applied engineering\n- The primary uncertainty (does hashing satisfy the regulator?) is not a technical question I can resolve through prototyping\n- It commits resources to an approach whose compliance value is unproven\n- From a speed/quality standpoint, this adds complexity and project overhead for what could be handled as a design decision within the main development effort\n\nBut it's not a complete zero - there IS something to explore here, just not at the \"dedicated R&D project\" level. I'd give it maybe a 30-35 on promise. It's not terrible, but it's mis-scoped and the primary risk is non-technical (regulatory interpretation).\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise