        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P1451) from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**Proposal P1451: \"Dynamic Post-Market Performance Benchmarks\"**\n\nThe proposal describes:\n- A set of continuous evaluation benchmarks for deployed high-risk systems\n- Detecting performance degradation or risk triggers\n- Addresses Article 72 post-market monitoring\n- Implements a streaming analysis tool comparing real-world input logs against baseline distribution profiles from validation phase\n- Alerts when statistical drift exceeds predefined scientific thresholds\n- Ensures long-term empirical validity while fulfilling legal monitoring obligations\n\n**My vantage point: technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.**\n\nLet me think about this carefully:\n\n1. **Technical viability:** The core idea is a streaming analysis tool that compares real-world inputs against baseline distribution profiles using statistical drift detection. This is technically well-understood territory — CUSUM, KS tests, PSI (Population Stability Index), KL divergence monitoring, etc. are all established techniques. The architecture of \"streaming input → compare to baseline → threshold alert\" is straightforward to implement. The technical risk is low because the underlying math and engineering patterns are mature.\n\n2. **Speed/quality of prototyping:** This is where I need to think more carefully. \n   - A streaming analysis tool with drift detection is a well-scoped, bounded system. It's not an open-ended research problem.\n   - The \"baseline distribution profiles created during the validation phase\" part requires integration with existing validation pipelines — that's a dependency but manageable.\n   - \"Predefined scientific thresholds\" — this is actually a design constraint that helps prototyping speed because it means we don't need to build adaptive thresholding; fixed thresholds are simpler to implement and test.\n   - The scope is clear: input stream → distribution comparison → alert. This is prototypable in weeks, not months.\n   - Quality: The approach of comparing distributions rather than point-by-point is robust. It's a proven pattern in MLOps (data drift detection).\n\n3. **Does it align with what the regulation actually requires?** Article 12(2)(b) says logging capabilities shall enable \"facilitating the post-market monitoring referred to in Article 72.\" This proposal goes beyond just logging — it builds an analysis layer on top of logs. That's a natural and reasonable extension. The regulation requires that systems *enable* such monitoring, not necessarily that they include full analytics built-in. But building this as part of the system's capability is a sound engineering response.\n\n4. **Concerns from my vantage point:**\n   - \"A set of continuous evaluation benchmarks\" — the plural and vagueness here could scope-creep. What exactly are these benchmarks? If it means just drift detection on input distributions, that's focused. If it means re-running full model evaluations continuously, that's a much bigger lift with higher compute costs.\n   - \"Predefined scientific thresholds\" — who sets them? This is a domain expert task, not purely engineering. But for prototyping, we can start with standard statistical thresholds (e.g., p<0.01 for KS test) and refine later.\n   - The proposal says it's a \"streaming analysis tool\" which implies real-time or near-real-time processing. That adds some infrastructure complexity but is still well-trodden ground (Kafka, Flink, or even simpler batch-processed streams).\n\n5. **Overall assessment from my vantage point:** This is technically sound, well-scoped enough for prototyping, uses established methods, and the architecture is clear. The main risk is scope ambiguity around \"a