tructure that may not exist - different research groups might use heterogeneous environments (GPU clusters, edge deployments, cloud instances, etc.). A middleware layer that only works on one central cluster might not cover all deployment scenarios.\n- The proposal says \"ensures Article 12 compliance by default\" but Article 12(3) has specific minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics-like) that go beyond generic timestamping - you need reference database tracking, input data matching records, and natural person identification. A \"universal\" framework might not handle these domain-specific logging needs well without significant customization per use case.\n- There's a risk of over-engineering: building an elaborate middleware framework when the actual compliance requirement might be satisfied by simpler, more targeted solutions for specific model types. The upfront cost could exceed what's justified if the lab only has 2-3 high-risk systems.\n\n**Balancing:**\nAs Lab Director focused on resource efficiency and existing infrastructure use, this proposal hits several of my core priorities: consolidation (no redundant work), leverage of existing central compute cluster, and compliance-by-default reducing per-team overhead. The main risk is scope creep in the \"universal\" ambition potentially consuming more engineering resources than a targeted approach would. However, even if the framework needs some domain-specific plugins for Article 12(3) requirements, the core architecture (timestamping, event capture, metadata recording) is genuinely reusable and addresses the bulk of Article 12(1)-(2).\n\nGiven my mandate:\n- Resource-allocation efficiency: STRONG fit - one build serves many teams\n- Funding constraints: The upfront cost is real but amortizes well if multiple high-risk systems exist; if only 1-2, it's less justified. But as a framework, it scales.\n- Existing R&D infrastructure: Leverages central compute cluster rather than building new infrastructure\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's the kind of consolidation investment that a resource-conscious Lab Director would support - you build once, deploy broadly, and every future high-risk system inherits compliance capability without additional per-system engineering cost. The \"universal\" scope is slightly risky but the core value proposition (shared middleware for event logging) is sound and directly maps to Article 12(1).\n\nI'll approve with a high promise score - maybe 82-85. High because it aligns strongly with my resource-efficiency mandate, but not perfect because the \"universal across all models\" ambition could lead to scope creep and the integration into just one central cluster may not cover heterogeneous deployment environments.\n",
