le. As written, it risks over-engineering.\n\nI'll lean toward approve but with a moderate promise score because:\n- The regulatory need is clear and non-negotiable (Article 12(3)(d))\n- The scope is bounded (one specific data point in logs)\n- But the implementation approach as described (\"develop a dedicated interface,\" \"secure authentication portal\") risks wasteful duplication of existing infrastructure\n- The resource commitment is moderate, not enormous\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My bar: does this clear it from my vantage point? The requirement IS real and mandated. Even if the implementation could be more efficient by reusing existing systems, the proposal identifies a genuine gap that needs to be addressed. The \"dedicated interface\" framing is concerning but the core deliverable (linking verifier ID to event log) is necessary regardless of whether you build a new portal or integrate with existing auth. I'll approve it because the regulatory requirement is explicit and non-negotiable, and the scope is bounded enough that even if the implementation approach needs adjustment during scoping, committing resources now to address this gap is justified. But my promise score should reflect the inefficiency concern in the proposed approach — maybe 62-68 range.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, with a promise of around 64. The need is real and mandated, but the implementation framing suggests unnecessary bespoke development when existing infrastructure could be leveraged, which is exactly the kind of resource waste I'm charged with preventing.\n",
