t of failure for the entire lab's model deployments. If the logging infrastructure goes down, does it block deployment? That could impede scientific progress.\n- Building a middleware layer requires dedicated engineering resources up front — a significant one-time cost that competes with other R&D priorities. Is the funding available now?\n- \"Every model deployment\" in CI/CD is a broad commitment. If we have many models, this integration work across all of them could be substantial.\n- The proposal doesn't address ongoing maintenance costs, which for infrastructure can be significant over time.\n- It's somewhat generic — it describes the WHAT (a unified logging framework) but not the HOW in enough detail to assess true cost. \"Middleware layer\" is a big engineering task.\n- Risk: building something overly centralized/complex that becomes its own maintenance burden, diverting resources from actual research.\n\nHowever, on balance, from a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this proposal is quite strong. The core insight — build once, deploy everywhere via CI/CD, avoid per-project retrofitting — is exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that pays for itself over time. It directly addresses the regulatory requirement (Article 12(1) automatic recording of events) in a scalable way. The fact that it plugs into existing CI/CD infrastructure means it's leveraging what we already have rather than building parallel systems.\n\nThe main risk is the upfront cost and the potential for it to become a bureaucratic overhead, but compared to the alternative (each project team independently implementing logging), this is clearly more efficient. The scope seems reasonably bounded — a middleware layer + schema + API integration into CI/CD.\n\nI would lean toward approving this. It's a classic \"build the shared infrastructure once\" play that maximizes resource efficiency. The prevention of retrofitting is a strong economic argument. Integration into existing CI/CD le