ation of natural persons involved in verification.\n\n**Concerns/weaknesses:**\n- Building a middleware layer is itself a significant engineering investment. For a lab that may have limited funding, committing scarce implementation resources to this could delay other projects. However, the question is whether it's cheaper than alternative approaches (per-model logging, manual compliance, etc.). I'd argue it IS more efficient in aggregate.\n- \"Standardized software layer\" — standardization is good for efficiency but could be over-engineering if the lab only has 2-3 high-risk models. However, even then, a shared component is better than N separate implementations.\n- The scope seems reasonable and bounded: it's not proposing to rebuild the entire ML pipeline, just add a logging middleware layer. That's a contained project.\n- \"Seamless compliance\" is a strong claim — in practice, ensuring the middleware captures all Article 12(2)(a)-(c) relevant events for diverse model types might require some per-model configuration. But the core framework approach is sound.\n- From a funding constraint perspective: this is a one-time infrastructure investment that then reduces ongoing compliance costs across all future high-risk models. Good ROI profile.\n\n**Balancing scientific ambition vs. funding constraints:**\n- This proposal actually PROTECTS scientific ambition by removing manual compliance burden from researchers' plates. It's an enabler, not a constraint.\n- The resource commitment is bounded (one middleware project) and the benefit is recurring (all future high-risk models inherit compliance automatically).\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Hooks into existing model runtimes — good.\n- Doesn't require new compute or storage infrastructure beyond what logging requires (which is modest compared to training costs).\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a well-scoped, efficient proposal that directly addresses a hard regulatory requirement (Article 12)