y — without it, we can't legally operate certain high-risk AI systems in the EU market. So it's table stakes, not optional.\n- The proposal is appropriately scoped to the minimum: Annex III point 1(a) systems only. It's not overreaching to all high-risk categories or trying to build a universal logging platform. That restraint is good from a funding perspective.\n- \"Mandatory sign-off portal\" — the word \"mandatory\" is important. It creates an operational overhead for researchers who must log in and click confirm. This could slow down research workflows. But it's what the regulation requires (Article 14(5) human verification).\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal says \"dedicated interface and secure database.\" This suggests building something new. A more efficient approach might be to integrate with an existing identity/access management system (like SSO), leverage existing database infrastructure, or use a commercial off-the-shelf audit logging tool rather than building custom.\n- However, the specificity of what needs to be logged (start/end times, reference database used, input data for matches, person identification) might make off-the-shelf solutions inadequate without customization anyway.\n- The \"secure database\" requirement is standard — most institutions already have secure databases for sensitive records. The question is whether this requires a new one or can use existing infrastructure.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a necessary compliance measure that is appropriately scoped. It targets exactly the narrowest category (Annex III 1(a) search/match functions) and addresses Article 12(3)(a)-(d) specifically. The main concern from my vantage is:\n1. Whether \"dedicated\" means building from scratch vs. leveraging existing infrastructure — this affects resource efficiency significantly.\n2. The operational burden on researchers (mandatory sign-off for every match review).\n3. It's compliance, not science — but it's mandatory compliance, so we have to do it regardless.\n\nThe proposal is well-targeted and doesn't overreach. It's a bounded engineering project with clear regulatory justification. From a resource perspective, it's something we MUST do if we want to operate Annex III 1(a) systems in the EU. The main inefficiency risk is building from scratch when existing infrastructure could be adapted.\n\nI'd lean toward approve because:\n- It's mandatory compliance (not optional ambition)\n- Scope is narrow and well-defined\n- Without it, we can't legally deploy certain systems\n- The resource commitment is bounded (one interface + one database for a specific function)\n\nBut my promise score wouldn't be very high because:\n- It doesn't advance scientific mission\n- \"Dedicated\" suggests new build rather than reuse\n- Operational friction for researchers\n- There may be cheaper ways to implement this (commercial tools, integration with existing audit systems)\n\nI'll approve it but give a moderate promise score reflecting that while necessary, the implementation approach as described isn't maximally efficient from an infrastructure-reuse standpoint. Maybe 55-65 range. It's something we have to do, but the way it's framed (build new dedicated system) could be more resource-efficient if reframed.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal is a candidate for implementation resources. If I approve it, I'm committing engineers and time to build this. The question is whether THIS specific formulation is the right use of resources. \n\nThe core requirement is real: we need to log who verified what, when, against which database, for Annex III 1(a) systems. That's non-negotiable if we operate those systems. The proposal identifies the right requirements correctly.\n\nThe \"dedicated interface\" framing is slightly concerning from an infrastructure perspective — why can't this be a module within our existing research management system or audit platform? But maybe it needs to be separate for security/isolation reasons.\n\nI'll approve at around 60. It's necessary, well-scoped, but the implementation approach could be more efficient by leveraging existing systems rather than building \"dedicated\" infrastructure.\n",
