tions by default\" but Article 12(3) has very specific requirements that vary by system type (only Annex III point 1(a) gets the detailed minimum). A single SDK might need to be configurable, and \"by default\" might oversimplify. However, a modular approach could handle this.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address data retention periods, log integrity (tamper-proofing), or access controls for logs — which are important for compliance but may be handled elsewhere.\n- It's a significant engineering investment. If the SDK is built wrong or becomes a bottleneck, it could delay projects. But from a compliance perspective, the alternative (every team building their own logging) is worse.\n- \"Prevents prototyping delays\" — from a compliance angle, this is good because rushed projects are more likely to skip compliance steps.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is strongly aligned with the compliance officer's mandate. It operationalizes Article 12(1)'s automatic recording requirement at an architectural level, ensures consistency across projects, reduces the risk of non-compliance due to individual team oversight, and leverages existing infrastructure. The main risk is over-engineering or scope creep, but the core value proposition — standardized, by-default compliance logging — is exactly what a compliance officer would want institutionalized.\n\nI'd give this a high promise score. It directly addresses the letter of Article 12(1) and supports 12(2) and 12(3). The \"by default\" aspect is particularly important for compliance because it removes reliance on individual team diligence. I'll approve with a high score, maybe 88-92. Let me settle on 90 — it's very strong but the \"by default\" language slightly oversimplifies what Article 12(3) requires (which is system-specific), and there are gaps around log integrity and retention that aren't mentioned.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. From a pure compliance officer perspective:\n- The SDK approach is excellent for institu