ch suggests it's designed to be composable rather than monolithic.\n- The technical approach (API wrappers on PyTorch/TF) is well-understood. PyTorch has hooks (forward hooks, backward hooks), TF has tf.summary and callback mechanisms. This is technically viable with moderate effort.\n- \"Plug-and-play\" for researchers is a good goal but the real question is: how much does it actually reduce friction? If researchers still need to understand what's being logged and configure which events to capture, the \"plug-and-play\" claim may be aspirational. But from a prototyping speed perspective, having a default configuration that captures Article 12(3) minimums out of the box would genuinely help.\n- The proposal is at the right abstraction level for a development engineering function: it's about building infrastructure that accelerates other work. It doesn't over-commit to specific model architectures or deployment environments.\n- Risk: scope creep if \"all high-risk AI models\" pulls in edge cases (on-device inference, legacy systems, non-PyTorch/TF frameworks). But the proposal specifically scopes to PyTorch and TensorFlow pipelines, which is reasonable.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage:**\nThis is technically viable — it's essentially building a logging/tracing SDK with framework-specific adapters. The engineering work is well-understood (hooks, interceptors, structured logging). It directly serves prototyping speed by making compliance infrastructure invisible to researchers. The modular approach reduces risk of over-commitment. I'd approve this.\n\nPromise score: From a development/prototyping vantage, this is high-value. It's not groundbreaking technology but it's the right kind of infrastructure investment that compounds across every project. I'd put it around 78-82. The main deduction is that \"all high-risk AI models\" is slightly over-scoped and the actual engineering effort to make it truly \"plug-and-play\" (vs. requiring per-model configuration) coul