"empirical studies comparing various logging granularities against their effectiveness in detecting edge-case anomalies in high-risk settings\" requires significant compute, data, personnel, and time. This is not a lightweight task.\n- The lab's domain concern is resource-allocation efficiency. A dedicated research project with open-ended scope (\"determine which specific log types are most predictive\") could balloon. There's no clear termination criterion or deliverable specified beyond \"leading the scientific discourse.\"\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- If we already have logging systems in development for Article 12 compliance, a parallel research project studying optimal traceability metrics could leverage that same infrastructure. That's a point in favor.\n- However, \"dedicated research project\" suggests separate resourcing, which may not be the most efficient use of what we already have. We could integrate this analysis into the design and implementation phase rather than spinning up a separate effort.\n\n**My overall assessment:**\nThe idea has merit — understanding what to log is genuinely important, and empirical study of logging granularity vs. anomaly detection is scientifically valuable. However, from my resource-allocation vantage point:\n1. The scope is too open-ended for a \"dedicated research project\" framing. It reads more like a multi-year research agenda than a bounded initiative.\n2. The \"leading the scientific discourse\" framing suggests this is partly about reputation/positioning rather than practical necessity. We need to log what Article 12 requires; whether we also lead discourse on optimal metrics is a different question with different resource demands.\n3. There's no clear link to the lab's existing implementation timeline or infrastructure. It could be done as part of the design phase without being a \"dedicated research project.\"\n4. The commitment of scarce resources to an open-ended empirical study, when