eters, data versions, and execution logs across the research pipeline\" — this is broad in scope. \"Across the research pipeline\" could mean many different systems, frameworks, and stages. The resource cost of making this comprehensive might be underestimated.\n- \"Maps technical metadata directly to Article 12 compliance categories\" — this requires a clear mapping schema. For Annex III point 1(a) (biometric identification), Article 12(3) has very specific requirements (start/end times, reference databases, input data for matches, natural persons involved in verification). A generic wrapper might not handle these edge cases without significant customization per system type.\n- The \"lifetime\" requirement in Article 12(1) means this isn't a one-time setup — it needs to persist and evolve as systems change. That's an ongoing maintenance cost.\n- However, the proposal specifically says it integrates with existing tools, which mitigates some of the infrastructure concern.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\n- The use of existing tools is smart — low capital expenditure, primarily labor for the wrapper development and integration.\n- The dual-use (compliance + reproducibility) improves the ROI narrative to leadership.\n- The scope is bounded enough (\"custom wrapper\" not \"new platform\") that it's a tractable project.\n- It directly reduces recurring costs (manual documentation) which compounds over time — good for long-term funding sustainability.\n\n**Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints:**\n- This doesn't require massive new funding; it's an integration/wrapper project on top of existing infrastructure.\n- It enables the lab to maintain scientific output (reproducibility benefit) while meeting compliance obligations, so it balances both.\n- The risk is scope creep: \"across the research pipeline\" could balloon if not scoped tightly.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a well-conceived proposal from my vantage point. It uses existing infrastructure efficiently, has dual purpose (compliance + research), reduces recurring manual overhead, and is scoped as an integration rather than a build-from-scratch project. The main risk is scope management (\"across the research pipeline\"), but the core idea of a wrapper over existing tools is resource-efficient.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. It's not a 100 because \"across the research pipeline\" is somewhat vague and could become a money pit, and the Article 12(3) specific requirements for biometric systems might need more than a thin wrapper. But it's solidly in approve territory — maybe 78-82 range.\n\nLet me settle on:\n- Decision: approve\n- Promise: 80\n",
