ties and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" A CI/CD pipeline pulling metadata from experiment tracking tools may not capture all required elements (e.g., risk-management system documentation, qualitative descriptions of capabilities/limitations).\n- The proposal says it \"replaces static reports with dynamic, version-controlled records\" – this is good, but there's a risk that automated generation produces technically correct but contextually incomplete documentation. For example, it might log that dataset X was swapped in but not capture the impact assessment or rationale for the change (which would be relevant for \"substantial modification\" tracking under Article 12(2)(a)).\n- However, as a compliance officer, I'd view this as a strong foundational infrastructure piece. It doesn't need to do everything; it needs to ensure that when changes happen, they are recorded and the documentation reflects them. The qualitative/analytical parts (risk assessment updates, capability statements) would still need human input, but the factual metadata layer is well-suited for automation.\n\nOn balance: This is a strong compliance infrastructure investment. It directly addresses a specific regulatory requirement (Recital 71's up-to-date documentation mandate), reduces a major class of non-compliance risk (documentation drift), creates audit trails, and frees researchers from manual compliance tasks that they'd likely neglect. The limitations (not capturing all qualitative documentation elements) don't negate the value—they just define scope. This is clearly something a compliance officer would champion.\n\nDecision: Approve.\nPromise score: I'd rate this highly. It's not a 100 because it doesn't cover the full breadth of Article 12/Recital 71 requirements (logging capabilities, risk-management documentation, etc.), and there are implementation risks around