ministrative burden\" - good intent, but if the pipeline produces incomplete or inaccurate documentation, it could create false compliance confidence and ultimately increase risk (and rework)\n- The proposal focuses on extraction from code/version control, but Article 12 also requires logging of events during system operation (runtime logs), which is different from development-time metadata. This seems to address only part of the requirement.\n- However, it does address the technical documentation aspect mentioned in Recital 71 about \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\"\n\n**Resource-allocation perspective:**\n- The upfront cost is an engineering team building a toolchain - moderate investment\n- Ongoing savings: reduced scientist time on documentation (significant if you have multiple AI systems)\n- Uses existing infrastructure (Git repos, CI/CD metadata, config files) - good leverage of what's already there\n- Risk: if the pipeline can't capture everything needed, you still need manual supplementation, and you've spent resources building a partial solution\n- The \"real-time\" aspect is attractive for keeping documentation current as required by Recital 71\n\n**Assessment:** This is a solid, well-scoped proposal that addresses a genuine resource efficiency problem. It leverages existing infrastructure rather than requiring new data collection. The main risk is scope - it may not cover all of Article 12's requirements (runtime event logging is different from development metadata), but as a component of the broader compliance strategy, it's valuable. From my perspective on resource allocation, redirecting scientist hours away from repetitive documentation and toward research is exactly the kind of efficiency gain I should be pursuing. The investment in a toolchain that uses existing version control and code infrastructure is reasonable.\n\nI'll approve this with a moderately high promise score. It's genuinely useful but has some scope lim