hrough verifiable computations. This is at the frontier.\n- However, there's a tension: DP adds noise, which fundamentally degrades information content. Article 12 requires specific data points (start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons). If you apply DP to these, can they still serve their regulatory purpose? This is a deep intellectual question worth exploring.\n- The problem is real but also somewhat constrained: it's essentially \"can we make logs private?\" which is a well-studied area in privacy research (private databases, secure enclaves, etc.). The novelty lies specifically in the intersection with AI Act compliance requirements.\n\n**Intellectual Merit:**\n- The combination of DP + MPC for regulatory logging is intellectually interesting but I'm skeptical about feasibility. DP typically requires adding calibrated noise proportional to sensitivity — for individual log entries containing specific data (like \"input data X matched against database Y at time T\"), the utility loss from adding meaningful privacy-preserving noise could be catastrophic for the compliance purpose.\n- MPC is more promising here because it preserves information while controlling access — you can encrypt logs or distribute them so that no single entity sees everything, but an authorized verifier (regulator) can reconstruct what's needed. This aligns better with Article 12's requirements.\n- The proposal somewhat conflates two different privacy concerns: (1) the data in the log being sensitive (e.g., biometric input), and (2) the metadata about the system itself (algorithms, training processes). Article 12 is really about the latter more than the former — it's about documenting how the system was built and operates. The \"privacy\" concern of the logs themselves is somewhat narrower than the proposal frames it.\n- Actually, re-reading Article 12(3), for biometric systems, you need to log input data that led to a match. That IS sensitive personal data. So there IS a real tension here.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If successful, this could provide a template for privacy-preserving compliance in regulated AI — broadly applicable beyond the EU AI Act (US state AI laws, other jurisdictions).\n- Publication at top-tier AI safety venues is ambitious but reasonable if the results are strong.\n- The broader impact is moderate: it's a specific technical problem (how to make logs private) that has clear applications but isn't going to transform our understanding of AI systems fundamentally.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a legitimate research question at the intersection of privacy engineering and regulatory compliance. It sits at the knowledge frontier in the sense that there's no established methodology for \"differentially-private regulatory logs.\" However, I have concerns:\n\n1. DP may be the wrong tool — it's designed for releasing aggregate information while preserving individual privacy, but here we need to preserve individual log entries (each specific event) while limiting who can access them. This is more of an access control / encryption problem than a noise-addition problem. MPC or homomorphic encryption might be more appropriate than DP.\n\n2. The proposal frames the tension as \"transparency vs confidentiality\" but Article 12's transparency requirement is quite specific and detailed — you can't just add noise to it and call it compliant. The regulatory purpose of the log (traceability, compliance verification) may be fundamentally incompatible with strong privacy guarantees on the same data.\n\n3. That said, there IS a genuine research question here about what level of privacy protection is compatible with regulatory utility, and how to design systems that achieve maximum privacy subject to a fixed compliance constraint. This is a meaningful optimization problem.\n\n4. The MPC angle is more promising than the DP angle for this specific application.\n\nOverall: I see intellectual merit in exploring this intersection. It's at the frontier of \"privacy engineering meets regulatory compliance.\" But I'm somewhat skeptical that DP specifically is the right tool — the problem might be better addressed through cryptographic access control, homomorphic encryption, or secure computation rather than differential privacy. The proposal hedges by including MPC as well, which helps.\n\nFrom a pure research merit standpoint: it's a solid but not groundbreaking research direction. It solves a real problem with moderate novelty. I'd say it clears my bar for funding — it's intellectually interesting, at the frontier of applying privacy tools to a novel regulatory context, and has clear practical impact if successful. But it's not a 90/100 idea that would make me jump out of my chair.\n\nI'll approve with a moderate promise score, maybe around 58-65. It's legitimate research at the frontier but with some concerns about whether DP is the right framing for this problem. The MPC component saves it somewhat. Let me settle on 60.\n",
