ses/Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat vague on implementation details — \"calculate infrastructure costs\" could be a very rough estimate or a precise model. Without specificity, it might become a checkbox exercise.\n- It doesn't address the quality of logs, only their storage/cost. Article 12(2) specifies what events must be logged for traceability; the proposal focuses on the cost side rather than ensuring the logging capability itself meets the regulatory bar.\n- \"Lifetime\" is ambiguous in practice — how long? The Act says \"over the lifetime of the system\" but doesn't define a fixed period. The protocol needs to handle this ambiguity.\n- Risk classification-based allocation is good in principle but the mapping from risk class to specific storage/compute needs isn't elaborated.\n\n**From my compliance perspective:**\nThis proposal addresses a real gap that could lead to non-compliance. If a project gets funded without accounting for lifetime log retention, and then the institution can't afford or technically support maintaining those logs, we're in violation of Article 12(1). The institutional mechanism (updating templates, budget committee review) is practical and creates accountability. It's not about building the logging system itself — that would be an engineering proposal — but about ensuring the financial and resource infrastructure exists to sustain compliance over time.\n\nThis is a solid, institutionally-oriented proposal that directly supports my mandate of regulatory compliance and institutional survival. It's not the most exciting proposal, but it prevents a very real failure mode: a system goes live, logs start accumulating, and then the lab realizes they never budgeted for 5-10 years of log storage at scale.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a practical governance mechanism that directly supports Article 12 compliance sustainability. My promise score would be fairly high — maybe 78-82. It addresses a real gap, has clear institutional mechanics, and