        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1382\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A single centralized logging framework integrated into the standard development kit is the most resource-efficient architecture for meeting Article 12 across a multi-team R&D lab: one build serves every downstream high-risk model, and hooking middleware into existing pipelines (rather than replacing them) respects current infrastructure. My main reservation is execution risk in a heterogeneous research environment where 'existing training and inference pipelines' range from production-grade to throwaway experimental scripts; the proposal's language implies a clean single integration surface that may not exist, potentially inflating cost beyond initial estimates.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen proposal P1382 from the vantage point of a Lab Director at an R&D institution focused on:\n- Resource-allocation efficiency\n- Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\n\nLet me assess this proposal against Article 12's requirements and my professional domain.\n\nThe proposal is a \"Unified Traceability Logging Framework\" — a centralized API and database architecture for automatic event logging across all high-risk AI model lifecycles, with middleware hooks into training/inference pipelines, integrated into the standard development kit.\n\nLet me think about this from my vantage point:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- A single unified framework rather than ad-hoc per-team solutions is efficient in the long run — one build serves all teams. This aligns well with resource allocation principles.\n- However, it's a substantial infrastructure investment upfront: middleware layer development, centralized database architecture, integration into every pipeline. That's significant engineering effort before any single team sees benefit.\n- The \"middleware layer that hooks into existing training and inference pipelines\" — this is where I need to be careful. R&D labs often have diverse, rapidly evolving experimental pipelines. Forcing all of them through a standardized middleware could create bottlenecks or require constant re-integration as models change. But the proposal says it's integrated into the \"standard development kit,\" which suggests it's meant to be the default from day one for new work.\n- \"Eliminating the risk of human error in manual logging\" — this is a real efficiency gain over having each researcher manually log events.\n\n**Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints:**\n- This is an infrastructure investment that doesn't directly advance scientific research. It's compliance overhead. However, it's also the kind of thing