n into the standard dev kit is smart. The main concern is scope and cost: building a comprehensive middleware layer that works across heterogeneous R&D pipelines is non-trivial and could be expensive. But compared to the alternative (each team solving this independently, or retrofitting after the fact), it's clearly the more efficient path.\n\nThe risk I see: in an R&D environment, \"existing training and inference pipelines\" are often experimental, one-off scripts, or rapidly evolving. The assumption that a single middleware layer can cleanly hook into all of them may be optimistic. But the proposal frames this as going forward (integrated into the standard dev kit), which mitigates some of that risk for new work.\n\nOverall: I think this clears my bar. It's the right architectural approach for resource efficiency, it leverages existing infrastructure (hooks into pipelines rather than replacing them), and it scales to serve all teams with one investment. The main reservation is execution complexity in a heterogeneous R&D environment, but that doesn't make the proposal wrong — it just means implementation will need careful phasing.\n\nI'd give this maybe an 72-78. It's clearly the right direction and efficient use of resources, but I have some concerns about scope creep (all high-risk AI model lifecycles at once) and the heterogeneity challenge in R&D pipelines that could make it more expensive than initially estimated.\n\nLet me settle on 74.\n",
