        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2947\",\n      \"rationale\": \"From a compliance standpoint, a documented and auditable classification process is the indispensable prerequisite for any Article 12 obligation: you cannot implement logging capabilities for systems you have not reliably identified as high-risk, and failure to do so exposes the institution to significant enforcement risk (up to €15M / 3% of global turnover) and reputational damage with regulators and market partners. The main reservation is that a purely quarterly cadence may be too slow for an R&D lab where project scope or intended purpose can shift mid-cycle, creating a compliance gap before the next review; additionally, framing the trigger narrowly around 'whether Article 12 applies' rather than the broader high-risk determination under Article 6 (which simultaneously activates risk-management, data-governance, transparency, and human-oversight duties) risks giving engineering teams a false sense of limited scope. That said, as a foundational governance mechanism that produces an audit trail of classification decisions, it directly protects institutional survival and is the logical first gate before any record-keeping investment.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 80\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen a single proposal (P2947) from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze proposal P2947:\n\n\"High-Risk AI Classification Framework. A formal internal auditing process used to categorize projects as high or low risk early in the R&D lifecycle. The lab will establish a quarterly review board that maps project goals against EU AI Act Annexes to determine if Article 12 applies. This creates a clear operational trigger for when record-keeping resources must be allocated. It ensures we do not waste limited funding and engineering hours on systems that are not legally classified as high-risk.\"\n\nFrom a Compliance Officer's perspective:\n\n**Positive aspects:**\n- Early classification is critical. If you don't know whether Article 12 applies to your system, you can't ensure compliance. Misclassification (treating a high-risk system as low-risk) would be a serious regulatory violation.\n- The quarterly review board provides a structured governance mechanism. From a compliance standpoint, having a formal process with documented decisions is important for demonstrating due diligence to regulators.\n- Mapping against Annexes (I and III) is the correct legal trigger - Article 12 only applies to high-risk AI systems as defined in Article 6 (which references Annex I and Annex III).\n- \"Clear operational trigger\" language aligns with compliance thinking: you need to know when obligations kick in.\n- From an institutional survival standpoint, failing to identify a system as high-risk and then not implementing logging capabilities could result in significant fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, up to €15M or 3% for other violations). Getting the classification right is foundational.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Quarterly\" review might be too infrequent. If a project shifts its intended purpose mid-quarter (e.g., pivots from a general-purpose tool to a biometric identification system), you could have a gap where Article 12 obligations are unmet. Compliance would ideally want continuous monitoring or at minimum a trigger-based review in addition to quarterly cadence.\n- The proposal says \"determine if Article 12 applies\" - but the classification determines whether the system is high-risk under Article 6, which then triggers ALL high-risk obligations (risk management, data governance, technical documentation, record-keeping, transparency, human oversight, accuracy/robustness/cybersecurity). Framing it narrowly as \"does Article 12 apply\" might give a false sense of scoping. However, this is the proposal's stated focus on record-keeping specifically.\n- The proposal frames the benefit primarily in terms of resource allocation (\"do not waste limited funding and engineering hours\"). While that's valid from an operations perspective, a compliance officer would note that the primary driver should be regulatory obligation, not cost-saving. Misclassification to avoid implementing logging could be seen as cutting corners on compliance.\n- However, the proposal does say \"formal internal auditing process\" which is good - it creates an audit trail of classification decisions themselves.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a foundational and necessary piece of compliance infrastructure. You cannot comply with Article 12 (or any other high-risk obligation) if you don't have a reliable, documented process for determining whether your systems are actually high-risk. The quarterly board provides governance structure. From institutional survival: misclassification risk is the biggest threat here - building a system that turns out to be high-risk without logging capabilities would be a severe compliance failure. This proposal directly addresses that.\n\nThe main weakness is the \"quarterly\" cadence potentially being too slow for fast-moving R&D, and the narrow framing around Article 12 specifically rather than the broader high-risk classification. But these are implementation details that can be addressed in execution.\n\nI would approve this. It's a prerequisite for compliance - you need to know your obligations before you can fulfill them. From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation), having a documented, auditable classification process is essential. If regulators come knocking and ask \"how did you determine whether Article 12 applied to System X?\", this framework gives us a defensible answer.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's clearly necessary and well-directed, but the quarterly cadence concern and the narrow framing hold it back from being a perfect solution. It's a strong foundation but needs supplementary triggers (e.g., project pivot reviews) to be fully robust from a compliance standpoint.\n\nLet me settle on 80.\n",
