        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to evaluate a single proposal (P2599) from my vantage point as a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**Proposal P2599: \"Compliance-Based Project Risk Screening\"**\n- A mandatory pre-development review process to categorize every new AI project as high-risk or otherwise under the EU AI Act.\n- Prevents accidental non-compliance before resources are committed.\n- Establishes a multidisciplinary committee (legal + technical leads) who sign off on risk assessment for every project proposal.\n- Output determines which logging requirements apply.\n- Secures institutional survival by ensuring no undocumented high-risk system is deployed into market.\n\n**My professional assessment as Compliance Officer:**\n\nThis is squarely in my domain. Let me think about this carefully:\n\n1. **Regulatory alignment**: Article 12 applies specifically to \"high-risk AI systems.\" The first critical compliance question is: does a given project produce a high-risk system? Without that determination, you don't know whether Article 12's logging obligations apply at all. This proposal directly addresses the threshold question that gates ALL downstream compliance work under the Act (not just Article 12 — but Articles 8-15 generally, plus market surveillance, CE marking, etc.).\n\n2. **Institutional survival**: If we deploy a system that IS high-risk but was never classified as such, we face:\n   - Fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover (Art. 99)\n   - Market withdrawal orders\n   - Criminal liability for senior management in some member states\n   - Reputational destruction\n   \n   A pre-development gate that forces classification BEFORE resource commitment is the single most cost-effective compliance control. The alternative — building a system, discovering it's