rise customers who are themselves subject to Art. 26 obligations and need to verify their suppliers' compliance posture.\n\n7. **Potential weaknesses**:\n   - \"Mandatory\" for EVERY project could be overkill for trivial internal experiments. But the proposal frames it as categorization, not full review — a 30-minute triage vs. a weeks-long deep dive.\n   - No mention of how the classification gets updated if a project's scope changes mid-development (substantial modification triggers under Art. 4). But that's an implementation detail; the core proposal is sound.\n   - Doesn't address who owns the ongoing monitoring post-classification, but again, that's beyond the scope of this specific gate.\n\n**Decision**: This is fundamentally aligned with my mandate. It's the single most important structural control for regulatory compliance at an R&D institution building AI systems. Without it, every other compliance activity (logging, technical documentation, risk management, post-market monitoring) is operating in a void because you don't even know which obligations attach to which project. I approve this without hesitation.\n\n**Promise score**: This is about as close to a 100 as I can give from my vantage point. It directly addresses institutional survival (avoiding catastrophic non-compliance), regulatory compliance (establishing the prerequisite for all Article 12 work), and market reputation (demonstrable governance). The only reason it's not literally 100 is that implementation details matter — the committee composition, turnaround time, and handling of scope changes need to be well-designed. But as a proposal to approve and commit resources to, I'd back this immediately.\n\nI'll give it a 95.\n",
