**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A prototype could be built relatively quickly: pick an existing experiment tracker, build a transformation layer that maps fields to Annex IV sections, generate a document template. This is a weekend-to-few-weeks MVP for a basic version.\n- However, \"clear and comprehensive format suitable for regulators\" is the hard part. Regulators want specific information in specific ways. The mapping from raw ML metrics/logs to regulatory language requires domain knowledge and iterative refinement with legal/compliance teams.\n- The prototype quality bar: Can we demonstrate that training a model automatically produces an updated section of the technical documentation? Yes, this is very demonstrable. A proof-of-concept could show: start training → experiment tracker records params/metrics/dataset info → API triggers document update → new version of Annex IV doc generated with timestamped changes.\n- Risk: Over-engineering \"real-time\" when batch updates at stage boundaries would suffice and be much simpler to implement reliably.\n- Risk: The \"living document\" concept introduces complexity around concurrent edits, versioning, and ensuring the document is always in a coherent state (not half-updated).\n\n**From my domain (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality):**\n- The core technical approach is sound and feasible. API integrations between experiment trackers and document generators are well-trodden ground.\n- A prototype is very achievable within a reasonable timeframe. The \"wow\" demo is straightforward: train model → doc updates automatically.\n- The main engineering challenges (regulatory language mapping, real-time vs batch tradeoffs, versioning) are manageable but require careful scoping to avoid scope creep.\n- This doesn't directly address Article 12's logging requirements (automatic recording of events/logs) as stated in the article text - it addresses Recital 71's documentation maintenance requirement. But that's still within the broader record-keeping domain and is a valid implementation target.\n- Actually, re-reading: the proposal says it \"addresses the continuous update requirement in Recital 71.\" This is about maintaining technical documentation up to date, which supports Article 12 but isn't exactly the same as implementing Article 12's logging capability. However, the training/test/validation logs that feed into this pipeline could also serve as part of the event recording required by Art 12(2). There's overlap.\n\n**My assessment:**\n- Technically viable: Yes, with some caveats about scope (real-time vs batch) and the regulatory formatting challenge.\n- Prototype speed: High - a meaningful MVP in weeks, not months.\n- Quality of prototype output: The demo would be compelling (automatic doc updates from training runs), but the \"regulator-ready\" quality claim needs validation with actual compliance review.\n- This is a well-scoped engineering problem that maps to existing tooling patterns.\n\nI'd rate this fairly highly from my vantage point. It's technically feasible, prototypable quickly, and addresses a genuine regulatory need. The main risk is scope creep on \"real-time\" and the gap between \"auto-generated document\" and \"regulator-acceptable documentation.\" But as a prototype target, it's very promising.\n\nI'll approve with a promise score around 78-82. It's solid engineering work with clear deliverables, but the \"regulator-ready format\" claim is where I'd want to see validation before full commitment.\n",
