d than it sounds.\n- The scope is broad: it needs to capture input data, timestamps, internal system states, reference databases, match results, person identification (per Art 12(3)). This is a substantial engineering project.\n- However, the sidecar approach does mitigate some of this — you don't need to understand every model internally, just observe events at boundaries.\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency assessment:**\n- One-time significant investment vs. ongoing per-project compliance costs. If the lab has multiple high-risk AI projects (or expects to), the amortized cost is favorable.\n- The sidecar approach means researchers don't need to divert time to compliance coding — this protects research throughput.\n- Risk: if the \"universal\" layer doesn't actually fit all architectures well, you may end up with a half-baked solution plus per-project patches anyway — worst of both worlds from a resource perspective.\n\n**Funding constraints:**\n- This is a capital investment in infrastructure. It's not cheap but it's bounded and one-time (plus maintenance). Compared to hiring compliance officers or having each team handle it individually, it's likely more cost-effective at scale.\n- The question is whether the lab has enough projects to justify this investment NOW vs. waiting until there's clearer demand.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Sidecar architecture explicitly designed NOT to require rewriting core model code — good. This means existing pipelines continue running while compliance is added externally.\n- This is a good fit for leveraging what already exists.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a well-conceived proposal from a resource-efficiency standpoint. The sidecar approach minimizes disruption, the universal layer amortizes cost across projects, and it frees researchers to focus on science. The main risk is the engineering complexity of making it truly \"universal\" across heterogeneous AI architectures, but the sidecar pattern miti