ctly with our existing ML pipelines\" - this suggests it's building on top of what we have rather than replacing everything. That's good.\n- But \"develop a specialized middleware layer\" and \"internal software library\" implies significant new development. The question is whether we're duplicating what our existing infrastructure already partially does (most ML pipelines already log some hyperparameters, dataset versions, etc.).\n- A \"secure, immutable repository\" - do we already have version control? Do we have audit logging infrastructure? Building a separate immutable store might be redundant with existing data governance systems.\n\n**Article 12 specific assessment:**\n- Article 12(1): automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime - this proposal covers training phase logs, but what about deployment/operational logs? The proposal seems focused on \"model training phase\" which is narrower than the full system lifetime requirement.\n- Article 12(2): logging for traceability relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring - the proposal captures hyperparameters and dataset versions, which is useful but may not fully cover \"events relevant to identifying situations that may result in risk.\"\n- Article 12(3) (Annex III point 1(a) systems): specific logging requirements about periods of use, reference databases, input data matches, natural persons verification - the proposal as described doesn't clearly address these operational/logging requirements.\n\nSo the proposal addresses training-phase documentation well but seems to under-address the full lifetime logging and operational monitoring aspects of Article 12. It's a partial solution presented somewhat broadly.\n\n**My decision framework:**\n- Is this efficient use of resources? Building custom middleware is more expensive than configuring existing tools (MLflow, etc.) for compliance purposes. However, if our existing pipelines lack proper integration points, some development may be needed. The proposal says it \"integrates directly with our existing ML pipelines\" which suggests adaptation rather than replacement - that's reasonable.\n- Does it balance ambition and constraints? It's a necessary compliance investment. Not ambitious scientifically but practically required. The question is whether the scope (internal library + middleware + immutable repository) is proportionate or over-engineered for what we need.\n- Does it use existing infrastructure effectively? Partially - it builds on existing pipelines, but adds new layers (middleware, separate repository).\n\nMy assessment: This is a reasonable and necessary investment direction, but the proposal as framed risks over-scoping (building full custom middleware + immutable repo when configuration of existing tools might suffice for initial compliance) AND under-scoping (focusing on training phase when Article 12 requires lifetime logging including operational events). From my resource-efficiency vantage point, I'd want a more targeted implementation rather than a grand \"framework.\" However, the core idea - automating capture of metadata to reduce researcher burden and ensure compliance - is sound and necessary.\n\nI'll lean toward approving because:\n1. The direction is correct and necessary for legal compliance\n2. It builds on existing pipelines (not replacing them)\n3. It reduces administrative burden on researchers (protecting scientific time)\n4. The scope, while perhaps slightly over-engineered in its \"immutable repository\" ambition, is within reasonable bounds\n\nBut my promise score should reflect that I have reservations about the build-vs-configure question and the training-phase-only focus. Maybe around 62-70? It's a solid direction but not a slam-dunk from an efficiency standpoint - there's risk of over-engineering and the scope doesn't fully cover Article 12's lifetime requirements.\n\nI'll go with approve at 65.\n",
