        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2791\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal maps directly onto Article 12(1)'s core obligation (automatic recording of events over the system's lifetime) and Recital 71's traceability requirements, and a standardized, automated layer across all high-risk projects materially reduces our regulatory exposure by removing reliance on ad-hoc, researcher-driven documentation. However, the 'absolute traceability' framing overshoots the regulation's proportionality standard ('appropriate to the intended purpose'), the description does not explicitly enumerate the four mandatory log fields in Article 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) biometric systems (use period, reference database, matched input data, verifier identity), and a single custom middleware becomes a critical dependency whose own integrity must be governed under change-management and post-market monitoring obligations.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 87\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P2791) from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, specifically regarding Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through my compliance lens:\n\n**Proposal P2791: Automated Provenance and Logging Framework**\n\nThe proposal describes:\n- A standardized software layer for automatic capture of hyperparameters, dataset versions, and runtime events\n- Covers all high-risk AI projects\n- \"Absolute traceability\" as the goal\n- Custom middleware API integrating with existing training pipelines\n- Logs critical telemetry into an immutable ledger\n- Aimed at compliance without manually burdening researchers during experimental phase\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\n\nStrengths from a compliance perspective:\n1. Directly addresses Article 12(1): \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" The proposal's core function is automated logging, which maps directly to this requirement.\n2. Addresses Recital 71's requirements: comprehensible information on development and performance throughout lifetime, traceability, technical documentation with general characteristics, capabilities, limitations, algorithms, data, training, testing, validation processes.\n3. The \"immutable ledger\" aspect supports the \"kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system\" requirement from Recital 71 — immutability prevents tampering with records.\n4. Standardization across all high-risk projects is a sensible compliance approach — it reduces the risk of gaps in specific projects.\n5. The focus on not burdening researchers aligns with practical implementation while ensuring the logging actually happens consistently (rather than relying on manual, error-prone processes).\n\nConcerns/Weaknesses from a compliance perspective:\n1. \"Absolute traceability\" is an overstatement — no system achieves absolute anything. Article 12(2) says \"appropriate to the intended purpose of the system,\" not absolute. This could be seen as either aspirational (fine) or a misreading of the regulation that might lead to over-engineering in some areas while under-addressing others.\n2. The proposal mentions \"hyperparameters, dataset versions, and runtime events\" — this is good but doesn't explicitly mention all the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification): period of each use, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons involved in verification. However, the proposal says \"all high-risk AI projects\" and \"runtime events\" which could encompass these, but it's not explicit.\n3. The \"custom middleware API\" approach has implementation risk — if it fails or isn't properly maintained, the entire compliance structure collapses. From a compliance standpoint, we need to ensure there's redundancy and that the system itself is subject to change management.\n4. The proposal doesn't address the post-market monitoring logging requirements (Article 12(2)(b)) explicitly, though \"runtime events\" might cover this.\n5. \"Immutable ledger\" — while good for integrity, we need to consider data protection implications (GDPR) of storing all this telemetry, especially if it includes input data that may contain personal information. As a compliance officer, I'd flag this but it's not a reason to reject the proposal.\n6. The scope is \"all high-risk AI projects\" which is correct per the regulation.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong, well-aligned proposal with Article 12 requirements. It directly addresses the core obligation (automatic recording of events over lifetime), provides standardization (reducing compliance risk across projects), and uses technical automation (ensuring consistency). The immutable ledger supports record integrity. The main gaps are: it doesn't explicitly enumerate the specific logging fields required for Annex III 1(a) systems, and \"absolute traceability\" is an overclaim relative to the regulation's \"appropriate to intended purpose\" standard. But these are refinements, not fundamental flaws.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This proposal directly reduces our regulatory risk under Article 12\n- Standardized approach means we can demonstrate systematic compliance to regulators\n- The technical automation ensures logs actually exist and are consistent (vs. relying on human processes)\n- It supports our ability to respond to regulatory inquiries with documented evidence\n- Market reputation: showing a robust, automated compliance infrastructure is a competitive advantage\n\nI would approve this. It's well-aligned with the regulation and addresses core obligations. The promise score should be high — maybe 85-90. I'm docking some points because:\n- \"Absolute traceability\" language could lead to scope creep or misallocation of effort\n- Doesn't explicitly address all Article 12(3) specific requirements for biometric systems\n- Custom middleware is a significant engineering commitment with potential single points of failure\n- No mention of how the logging system itself will be validated/audited (meta-compliance)\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 87.\n",
