tain it as frameworks evolve, and keep it updated as the Act's implementing guidance evolves.\n- The \"secure, immutable log store\" adds infrastructure cost - storage, security hardening, access controls, retention management. This is a capex/opex line item.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Integrating into the \"base Python environment\" suggests leveraging what's already there rather than building new tooling from scratch. That's good - it uses existing infrastructure.\n- The abstraction layer means existing research workflows don't need to be disrupted significantly. Researchers add a few lines of API calls rather than restructuring their pipelines.\n- However, \"across various ML frameworks\" could mean PyTorch, TensorFlow, JAX, etc. Supporting all of them is a scope risk that could bloat the project beyond what's needed initially.\n\n**Risks from my vantage point:**\n- Scope creep: \"various ML frameworks\" is vague. If it tries to support everything on day one, it becomes a massive engineering project with uncertain ROI.\n- Maintenance burden: The library will need ongoing maintenance as ML frameworks update and as EU implementing regulations refine what exactly must be logged.\n- Over-engineering risk: For early-stage prototypes that may never become deployed high-risk systems, building full compliance logging infrastructure might be premature. But the Act does require it for high-risk systems specifically.\n- The proposal doesn't mention phased rollout or which frameworks to prioritize first.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a genuinely good idea from an efficiency standpoint - eliminating N redundant implementations in favor of 1 shared one. It directly addresses my concern about resource allocation by preventing wasted engineering effort on compliance boilerplate. The integration into existing Python infrastructure is smart. The main risk is scope and maintenance burden, but the core concept is sound and cost-effective at scale.\